      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>As becomes a good Irishman, Mr. Harry Monk- house drives a jauntitlg-car, and the popular comedian is now to be seen this week at Hastings in his car of a day, and at night at the theatre, where his company are playing his own musical comedy, "Pat." As an advertisement of the show, the jaunting car comes in, as one may say, quite Pat.</p>
        <p>A Uganda "chief," by name Mika Sematimba, is going about with the Church Missionary Society, appearing at their public meetings, and occasion- ally saying a few words in broken English. He walked one thousand two hundred miles to the African coast, in order to come to England and obtain a glimpse of Western civilisation, (as it is said). He looks about eighteen years of age, but be is married and bas two children. He was pre- sented with a silver watch at a meeting in Man- chester the other day, and seemed to prize it immensely.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Cooper-Oakley, a sort of deputy high-Priest- ess of London Theosophy, has arrived in Melbourne and established herself for a year in Jolimont square in that city. By the Australian mail we have an interview with the lady, from which we gather that she was despatched by Mrs. Besant to forward the Theosophical cause in the colonies, to clear any misconceptions and in particular to ad- vance the doctrine of the universal brotherhood of man. Later on she is to be joined by Mrs. Besant in person but in the meantime Mrs. Cooper-Oakley will pave the way for Mdme. Blavatsky's successor by a series of lectures and by being always " at home" to inquirers.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. Swaby, who is to be ordained at Westminster Abbey on Saturday Bishop of British Guiana, is almost a solitary instance of a quondam Wesleyan minister who has attained to a bishopric. Dr. Swahy commenced life as a reporter, and after- wards became a Wesleyan minister. In 1871, how- ever, he was ordained a clergyman of the Church of England, and appointed curate of Ilyehope. For the last twenty-three years he has been vicar of St. Mark's, Meld, near Sunderland. His see in British Guiana will comprise an area of about one hundred and twenty thousand square miles.</p>
        <p>A wedding which will be of the greatest interest to the American colony in London and also to numerous English friends of the bride, is that ar- ranged between Miss May McClellan, the daughter of the celebrated commander of the army of the Potomac in the Succession War, and M. De,tprez, secretary to the French Legation at Washington. The wedding has been celebrated in Paris. Among those who have sent presents are the Comte and Comtese de- Paris and Princess Helene d'Orleaus. There are a large number of presents from Ameri- cans who have married Europeans or settled in Europe, among the donors being the Duchess de la Rochefoucauld and Mrs. Mackay. Miss McClellan, is well-known in London, where many friends wish her well. The bridegroom is a Catholic, and in consequence the bride was received into the Roman Church early in the week. Miss McClellan is tall and fair, with blue eyes and fair hair. She is highly educated, and speaks several lan- guages.</p>
        <p>Vivian Nickalls, who rows fifth man in the Varsity boat race this year for Oxford,is one of the most succe.ssful rowing amateurs of the century. Perhaps the most. interesting contest in which he has been successfully engaged refers particularly to the race for the Wingfield sculls in the year 1892. His victory on that occasicn established a record, which is not likely to be interfered with, for both his father and his brother Guy have held the dis- tinction, Vivian ;Ind Guy winning the event suc- cessively. Mr. Nickalls is an old Etonian, and in 1888 was promoted to a seat in the school eight, afterwards rowing No. 4 at Henley Regatta. Two years later a great honour fell to his lot when he was elected captain of the boats at Eton. In the autumn of the same year, Vivian matriculated at Magdalen College, just about the time when his brother Guy was being chosen as president of the 0.U.8.C. In 1891, he secured a seat in the Oxford Eight which defeated the Cantabs, and the spoils of other races fell to his share about the same time when he won the London Cup at the Metropolitan Regatta, and also the Diamond Sculls at Henley. Last year saw him fourth wan in the winning Ox- ford boat, but in the Diamond Sculls he was de- feated by J. J. K. Ooms, of Holland.</p>
        <p>Miss Shaw, the lady " special" of the " Times," in the colonies, was at latest at Adelaide, where 9he was the guest of the Premier and the Governor. The opinion seems to prevail in the colonies that Miss Shaw's contributions on Australian affairs would be much more accurate and permanently useful if she relied more on her own powers of ob- servation and lsss upon what she was told in " society "circles. As a leading Australian journal pertinently remarks, in referring to her pronounced approval of the revived lianaka traffic in Queens- land, "the Commissioner who makes a semi-royal progress through a country, heralded by cable messages, paragraphed in every newspaper, bols- tered with credentials from influential personages to governors and leaders of society—ay. to the very slave merchants and plantation nigger drivers them- selves, can by no possible chance be a trustworthy critics."</p>
        <p>As Miss 813 aw is now in the metropolis of South Australia, she may possibly take note of a signifi- cant change on the.governmental envelopes of that colony. Hitherto they have borne the orthodox official legend "On her Majesty's Service." But now they bear the democratic advice, " On Public Service." Is South Australia turning Republican like Queensland?</p>
        <p>The usual dogs have accompanied the Queen to Florence. A few years ago the only breeds kept in the Windsor kennels were collies, Skye terriers, and dachshunds, but when her Majesty was in Florence in 1888 she purchased some Pomeranians, or, to be more precise, Italian Spitz dogs. The pur- chase has proved to be exceedingly prefitable, and besides tne favourite Alamo there are Fluffie and Beppo, both of who appeared at the recent show in the Agricultural Hall, and are the offspring of Florentine parents. The Queen's breeder has been more successful, perhaps, with this breed than with any other of her Majesty's ventures. The dogs with the Court are under the charge of Francis Clark, the gillie, who thoroughly understands their ways, and whips them down when, in following the royal donkey chaise, they happen to become too boisterous.</p>
        <p>When the Queen is within her own dominions I the butter used at the reyal table is sent day by day fram the Home Farm at Windsor to the Court, I whether at Osborne or in the Highlands. Now that her Majesty is on the Continent the supply is I stopped, and if precedent be followed it will be sent to a West-end dealer for sale. The quantity of milk produced in the dairy department averages a hundred gallons all the year round, and it is only sufficient to supply the needs of the royal house- ii old under ordinary circumstances. In times of exceptional festivity as during the Jubilee, much larger quantities are required, and these are ob- tained by hiring milch cows from neighbouring farmer, as her Majesty, while not objecting to burehel's rat-ar being pplied by the tradespeople, has a great objection to run any risks in the milk supply. The Castle servants are now on board wages, and have to make their own arrangements as to their provender, the royal stores being erlyirely closed to them when the Queen is away.</p>
        <p>Tar! Han NIGHTCAP.—Have your sheets washed with Hudson's So', and they will always be sweet as roses. fresh ra stri breozra. There will be none of the disagreeable smell, ao objectionable in other soaps.—A. pure Dry Soap in fine powder.—Adtt. (2).</p>
        <p>4.bpuctiere's severe attack on Lord Rosebery's " Jingoistic " proclivities, brought the Grand Old Man to his feet in defence of his absent friend, and he vastly entertained the House with a good-humored analysis of Mr.Labouchere's " generalisiug " processes of argument. The Efousealso exploded in merriment at the ingenuous style in which, when the point in dispute was whether Uganda was six hundred or eight hundred miles from the coast, )Jr. .Gladstone settled the matter, as an expansive smile wrinkled the whole of his expressive countenance, with the epigrammatic observation, "It all depends upon where you place the milestones."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
