      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>DIARXST NILLIII</p>
        <p>As I mentioned last week, skirts are certainly gaining in width, and a medium size skirt for promenade or dance wear often measures five yards round, this being gored over hips, and the back fulness pleated well to the centre. The lining and material are made up together, and the foot trim- ming consists of rows of various witdth braids or velvet, ruches, flounce!, tucks, crossway folds, Sto. NotwiLlistanding the endeavour to introduce the 1830 fashions, many of the leading houses ,are pre- paring smart closefitting tailor-made gowns for Diming wear, and both pkin and fancy cloths are used. Close-fitting double breasted bodices open at the neck to show a linen collar and frilled fronts, are yet in good style, as also are the smart little coat bodices fastened with one button at waist, showing a chequered cloth or linen vest. Skirts to tailor-made gowns are cut with a wide front and shaped back, or with a gored front, side gore, and shaped back, for little fulness is allowed at the hips, although more width is certainly given to the foot part.</p>
        <p>The daintibet and most becoming little bonnet coneeivable, is composed of black satin, which fabric is just at present the rage both for millinery and dressmaking. The form of this lovely little head-gear requires no words of mine to help por- tray its many attractions, and the simple severity of trimming, though eminently chic, most readily lends iteelf to hand manipulation. The brim is plainly covered in the black wain, which, Around the crown, is slightly eased on, the crown itself being quite flat. A rouleau of black velvet is laid on the brim half an inch from the edge, and another ronleau divides the brim from the crown. The pointed cheese-cutter—l know of no other designation for this quaint arrangement is entirely covered with black velvet, and two black plumes are arranged with the fronts downwards, so that the tips curl over themselves. Some women have a kind of feeling that there is something disgraceful in acknowledging a fondness for pretty clothes, or that it shows a certain superiority to the rest of the world in not caring for them at all. Why should not a woman wish to be a pictnre to other eyes, as well as using her own in seeing painted pictures ? Everyone will admit the beauty of colour and texture in silk and velvets —why should she not be as ready to admit it when they are combined in a garment? The wise woman is she who makes use of the material at her com- mand. Though she will not spend all her thought on dress, she will know that graceful and artistic garments are a large part of her effectiveness.</p>
        <p>An elegant visiting or reception dress for a young married lady is made of crepon crepe in a deep shade of suede, with stripes of green velvet down the skirt at intervals to each side of the full and Ilightly trained back, which is gored; over the full bodice is worn a 17 andyek collarette and corselet with folded belt, which are, like the handsome sleeves, made of Ow 'velvet; the fulness across the bust makes the corsage very becoming to a slight figure. Liberty velvet, or even velveteen, ean be used, where silk velvet is found too expen- sive.</p>
        <p>The dainty ruffles, elaborate collars, and fine sleeves of past fashions gave the ladies of the time plenty of opportunities of showing their skill in embroidery. Nowadays we have only coarser things to work, which afford less scope for the display of fine elaborate work. But pretty aprons are muoh in vogue, and I cannot help thinking that any maiden who wishes to revroduoe the delicate work of the early years of this century miflit do so with great effect upon fine cambric or Nainsook muslin in the borders, bib, and pocket of a worked apron.</p>
        <p>For a dinner gown I have not seen anything prettier than one made in pale brocade with semi. gored train and Victorian corsage draped in lace or fancy gauze across the bus, with berthe and sleeves of dark velvet, the basque being to correspond. Of course, if made in plain satin or silk this kind of dress can be trimmed in a variety of ways, and a jet tablier could be worn with it, as they are again to be much in vogue: black lace again can be used also very effeotively on such a dress. _ -</p>
        <p>--- - With regard to the increase of cigarette-smoking among women, some very conflicting evidence has been secured from the several leading tobacconists and others. Dr. Norman Kerr says, "It is within my knowle.lge, professionally, that lady smokers' have of late years been on the increase. I speak of ladies of family, position, or wealth. In several instances I have known unmarried ladies who have been regular smokers, but wives of professional and milLary, as well as of City, men have also been regular worshippers at the shrine of the divine weed.'"</p>
        <p>-- - After towels are worn in ho'eq, take two or three of them, fold the ends over to the middle, and baste each one carefully, so that the whole places may overlap those which are badly worn. Then place two such prepared pieces together and baste them again. liten stitch them crosswise, length- wise, bias, and in all directions. This makes a rather thick, soft cloth, which is worth more as an absorbent than any other that can be found. Most useful housecloths can be made from pieces of old towels, table-cloths, napkins, and the like. The smaller and badly-worn pieces are placed inside, and, with better pieces to use as covers, the whole is quilted together either by machine or by hand. Sincerely yours, Jzionn.</p>
        <p>Apple Butter (American Recipe). This is generally made in large quantities. Boil down a kettleful of cider to two-thirds the original quantity. Pare, core, and slice the apples, and put in as many as the cider will just cover. 801 l slowly, stirring often with a flat stick, and, when the apples are tender to breaking, take them out with a strainer. Put ia a second supply of fruit and repeat the operation. Pour all together into a large tub, cover it, and let it stand for twelve hours. Return it to the kettle and boil the fruit down, stirring all the time, till it attains the con-</p>
