      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 25,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 25,</p>
        <p>His Lordship: Here is a long statement, coverin g page after page, and no attempt is made to prove either of them. Mr. Gully : After all this brag about things they would prove and substantiate at the beginning of the case, my friend refuses to put a single witness in the box to prove any of these statements which have been repeated with circumstances of aggravation. Do not suppose for a moment that Mr. King has not been per- fectly ready and willing to go into the box. Mr. King is here, ready to go into the box to answer all the charges made against him, and it is only because my friend, after giving me every :reason to suppose that he was coming here to substantiate some of the charges has not done so, that I have not put the plaintiff into the box in the first instance. The course he has chosen to take precludes me from putting him in now. I do submit to you that it is a case in which, after the suggested apology resented in a most contumelious manner, the plaintiff is entitled to most substantial damages. Mr. Shee, in his address to the jury, referred to Mr. Gully's action as the skilful tactics sometimes resorted to by able counsel, and for some reason they had the play of " Hamlet" with Hamlet lett out. Either the plaintiff would not go into the box or those who knew his case better than he did himself would not let him. That being so, he refused to put any of the defence witnesses into the box. It seemed to him that the most that could be said in this case was that the chemical workers of St. Helens had gone over the line, and the most Mr. Gully could ask the jury wan what damages they could give to the paid president and organiser of a trades union. The learned Judge in summing up pointed out that the action was undefended ; that the question for the jury was simply one of damages, and that for the purpose of assessing damages the jury could not discriminate between the defendants, who were jointly responsible for the letter of the 11th November, which was the substantial ground for complaint. That letter it was admitted, contained numerous falsehoods in fact, and though there might be fair criticism upon facts it was idle to talk of fair criticisms upon falsehoods. The alle- gation that defendant tried to deceive the Royal Commission was quite unfounded. Having regard to the number of minor offences charged against Mr. Kiag in the particulars, Mr. Gully was perfectly within his right, although the course wa.:, unusual, in leaving the defendant to offer proof of those charges ; but the fact that plaintiff did not go into the witness box and submit to cross-examination would, and he thought should, influence the view of the jury as to dams ges, because, although they were bound to give damages, the amount of damages depended on the estimate they formed of the character of the plaintiff. Plaintiff had beyond question been grossly libelled ; but as he had chosen to leave them in the dark as to what manner of man he was, they had not the evidence usual in these cases, upon which to assess his loss of reputation. The jury gave the plaintiff £loo,and his lordship gave iudgment with costs. THE SALE OF THE "CHRONICLE." _ .</p>
        <p>case</p>
        <p>them</p>
        <p>The " St. Helens Chronicle," against which Mr. P. J. King, president of the Chemical and Copper Workers' Union, was awarded £lOO damages for libel, has been sold. "Stubbs' Gazette" announces that Mr. William Swarbrick, lately trading as the St. Helens Printing and Publishing Co., of 3, flardsha.w-st., St. Helens, has as- signed, by way of absolute sale, the goodwill and inte- rest in the printing and stationery business, and in the proprietorship of the " St. Helens Chronicle" to James W. Hall for £1,150. The sale was made on the 11th March, and the bill filed on the 14th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>BEL ACTION AGAINST A ST. HELENS NEWSPAPER. TRIAL AND VERDICT. DAMAGES FOR A TRADES UNION SECRETARY.</head>
        <p>BEL ACTION AGAINST A ST. HELENS NEWSPAPER. TRIAL AND VERDICT. DAMAGES FOR A TRADES UNION SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at the Liverpool Assizes, before Mr. Justice Charles and a special jury, there came on for hearing the action King v. Swarbrick, in which damages were sought for alleged libel published in the " St. Helens Chronicle," in which plaintiff was called a liar and a perjurer. Mr. Gully, Q.C., M.P., and Dr. Commins were counsel for the plaintiff, and Mr. Shee, Q.C., and Mr. T. Swift represented the -defendants. In his statement of defence, Mr. Swar- brick, the editor and proprietor of the paper, denied the publication or that it was libellous. The other defendants offered practically the same defence, with the addition that the libel was true in sub- stance and fact. Mr. Gully, in his opening, described the plaintiff, Peter John King, as a gentleman who, for some time past, had taken a prominent part in certain matters in St. Helens. He came to St. Helens some years ago as an election agent. In that way he became acquainted with many people, especially a great number of chemical workers in St. Helens, who looked upon him as a person who would have considerable power of and facility for or- ganisation, and who requested him to assist them in forming a union called the Chemical and Copper Workers' Union. That was started in 1889. Large numbers joined it, and branches were started at Widnes and other places. The plaintiff as an election agent otherwise took a prominent part in politics, and the "St. Helens Chronicle" was a newspaper on the oppo- site side of politics, and had occasion to refer to him in those not always polite terms in which provincial politi- cal opponents, when they became heated, were some- times in the habit of referring to each other. Andrew Hodgins and Patrick Collins, the other two defendants, were originally members of the union formed by the plaintiff. They subsequently ceased to be members, and became members of a rival one started in March, 1892, I under the title of the Amalgamated Society of Chemical and Copper Workers. James Smith, a man who had been acting in some capacity in the original union, but who had been dismissed by a resolution which gave the reasons for its action as drunkenness, errors in accounts, and divulging he secrets and working against the union, became connected with the new society in the autumn of 1892. Both of these societies were intended to be unions and friendly societies. In the autumn of 1892 there began to appear in the" St. Helens Chronicle," which appeared to be upon friendly terms with Hod- gins and Collins, letters and paragraphs reflecting upon Mr. King, who was referred to as "Pay Jay." On the 11th November the " Chronicle " published a letter from Messrs. Collins and Hodgins, who com- plained of a paragraph which appeared in the "Liverpool Echo," reflecting upon the society and its promoters. The letter said that they (Messrs. Collins and Hodgias) did much to make the old chemical union what it was, and " helped Mr. King to the position which, unfortunately for the chemical workers of St. Helens and other places, had disgraced and abused the principles of trades-unionism." The letter further stated that they were told the Committee of the union were anxious to rid themselves of a big surplus item, but that he (King) stuck like the proverbial leech. He (King) bad used falsehood and deceit to undermine what was a powerful institution. The author of the pare graph (referring to King) said he was a " professional in deception, having bad a long experience in the prac- tice and taken special honours, as we shall be able t.) show. Here is an instance. The chemical workers were examined before the Royal Commission, and the presi- dent of the Chemical and Copper Workers' Union stated that they had 10,000 members but on examination ad- mitted that they had only 2,000. As a matter of fact, there are not more than 500 members in all the branches. He further stated that the society paid sick pay of Bs. a week downwards, according to the length or time sick, from the funds, knowing perfectly well that he was stating a lot of falsehood en oath." The evidence before the Royal Commission Mr. Gully remarked, was not given on oath, as a newspaper proprietor ought to have known, and the remark that King's evidence was given on oath was made to make Mr. King appear more des- picable in the eyes of the workers. On the 25th Nov- ember a letter written by Collins was published in the "St. Helens Lantern," in which it was stated that " this King of bluster knows that I have not been able to earn my livelihood by my wits 'or on the ignorance of other men." On the same day an article was written in the" Chronicle" attacking Mr. King and stating that " undeniable facts had been said about him in their columns, and that he was only the friend of the toilers so far as it suited his own interests." The news- paper defence to this serious libel was that the observa- tions they had published were fair comments in matters of public interest. The course open to them was either to apologise or to say that what they said was true, and they were prepared to prove it. But to try and sneak out of a libel like this with a dirty defence was a course which aggravated considerably the offence of which the newspaper had been guilty. The defence of Hodgins and Collins was that so far as the statements were statements of facts they were trae, and so far as they were matters of opinion they were fair comment. They isegan by saying that they denied the publication of the letter complained of. When in- terrogated as to whether they had written the letter they denied it upon oath; but he should be able to show by evidence that the letter was one which was concocted at a meeting of the rival union by James Smith, the dis- charged servant of the union of which Mr. King was president, and by Hodgins and Collins, and he would be able to prove that those who were so ready to impute perjury to others had themselves committed perjury. Then there were some general statements that the society had not flourished under him, but these gentlemen had done all they could to make it dwindle and decay. Arthur Travis, chemical labourer in St. Helens, said he was one of the original members who helped to found the Union of Chemical and Copper Workers, and he was one of the committee. Twelve months ago he left the union, and became one of the committee of the new union. About the end of October the question of writing an attack upon Mr. King through the newspapers was before the committee, and James Smith was deputed to do it with the assistance of the defendants Hodgins and Collins. He did not see the letter before it was published, but finding it did not appear in the newspapers he asked Hodgins, who told him that the editor of the " Adver- tiser ' had refused to publish it, but it would appear in the " Chronicle " next week. After it had appeared wit- ness told him he thought the letter a little toe severe— that that was not the kind of letter they had been t'lld off to write. Hodgins replied that the one that was re- fused was stronger still, and that he had " drawn it a little milder." (Laughter.) At the committee meeting afterwards some of the members seemed satisfied and others not. Hodgins and Collins said it was all right, and that they would defend it if it came to a trial, as they could prove what was in it. Mr. Shee, as a preliminary to cross-examination, asked the defendants Hodgins and Collins to stand up for iden- tification. He then said, " These men are illiterate men."</p>
        <p>Mr. Gully : I believe they are not. Mr. Shea (after a pause) : I call no witnesses; my friend does not call the plaintiff. His Lordship: That means that it is a question of damages simply. Mr. Gully : Very well ; I will take my friend at his woid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>MR. GLA.DSTONiS HOME LIFE</head>
        <p>MR. GLA.DSTONiS HOME LIFE</p>
        <p>--- Au interesting account of Mr. Gladstone's home life has just appeared in several papers. The secret of his extraordinary length of days and the perfec- tion of his unvarying health is no doubt largely to be found in the remarkable longevity of the Glad- stone family, a hardy Scottish stock with fewer weak shows and branches thaa perhaps any of the ruling families of England. But it has depended mainly on Mr. Gladstone himself and on the undeviating regularity of his habits. Most English statesmen have been either free livers or with a touch of the bon vivant in them. But Mr. Gladstone is a man who has been guilty of no excesses save, perhaps, in work. He rises at the same hour every day, uses the same fairly generous but al- ways carefully regulated selected diet, goes to bed about the same hour, pursues the same round of work and in- tellectual and social pleasure. An extraordinary varied life is accompanied by a certain rigidity of personal habit I have never seen surpassed. At Hawarden, of course, it is simpler and more private than in London. In town to-day, Mr. Gladstone avoids all large parties and great crushes and gatherings where he may be expected to be either mobbed or bored or detained beyond his usual bedtime. Personally,. Mr. Gladstone is an example of the moat winning, the most delicate, and the most minute courtesy. He is a gentleman of the elder English school., and his manners are grand and urbane, always stately, never condescending, and genuinely modest. He affects even the dress of the old school, and I have seen him in the morning wearing an old black evening coat, such as Professor Jowett still affects. The humblest passei-by in Piccadilly raising his hat to Mr. Gladstone is sure to get a sweeping salute in return. Tnis courtliness is all the more re- markable because it accompanies and adorns a very strong temper, a will of iron, and a habit of being regarded for the greater part of his lifetime as a per- sonal force of unequalled magnitude. Yet the mo:t foolish, and perhaps, one may add, the most imperti- nent, of Mr. Gladstone's dinner-table questioners is sure of an elaborate reply, delivered with the air of a student in talk with his master. To the Cleth, Mr. Gladstone shows a reverence that occasionally woos ' the observer to a smile. The callowest curate is sure ' of a respectful listener in the foremost Englishman of the day. On the other hand, in private conversation ' the Premier does not often brook contradiction. His temper is high, and though, as Mr. George Russell has I said, it is limier vigilant control, there are subjects on which it is easy to arouse the old lion. Then the grand eyes flash, the torrent of brilliant monologue , flows with more rapid sweep, and the dinner table is ! I breathless at the spectacle of Mr. Gladstone angry. As to his relations with his family, they are very charm- -. .</p>
        <p>It-is-a pleasure to hear Mr.' Herbert Gladstone— his youngest and possibly his favourite son—speak of " my father."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>MANNERS AND MATRIIION Y.</head>
        <p>MANNERS AND MATRIIION Y.</p>
        <p>That men don't marry is a frequent complaint now-a-days, and the omission—a serious one—is generally set down to their own supineness, selfish- ness, ccnceit, and dislike' to keeping two persons on one person's salary:' These cause especially the last, are no doubt partially to blame, but not altogether so. Many men who either never marry or postpone matrimony to the lustre berween 33 and 40—the most marrying age, according to a , , writer in a daily paper—are in receipt of salaries which would more than bear the double burden. Nor are the majority of youths, so far as our exper- ience goe-, offensively selfish or conceited. It does not seem to be remembered that in women them- selves lie some of the causes which make men draw shyly off. Girls overlook the fact that manner has much to do with the fatalities and destinies of life, and with none more than mattimony. Men hate self con- ciousness in a woman, and unfortunately self-conscious- ness is the most salient fault of the fin-de-siecle English girl. Pretty she most indisputably is, gifted with a fine physique, and often with plenty of mental power. As a rule she is well dressed, and, in short, possesses most of the externals likely to win to the masculine heart. But too often she is self conscious to a degree which she would ridicule,if she saw it in a man,and sometimes does ridicule in her fellows. Her very air as she walks by with more than mannish assumption of independence, joined to obvious satisfaction in her own get-up the ag- gressive little swagger of her walk,sometimes even, alas, the swinging arm, is enough to neutralise all the charm of her pretty face and healthily developed figure. And in social intercourse she is apt to make a most fatal mistake. . .</p>
        <p>How often has one not met a girl who, so long as she had only her sex to talk to, was calm, sensible, and natural, even if not very amusing, but who, the moment that a young man came into the room, underwent a sort of "lightning trick," which divested her of her real charm, and transformed her into a giggler and a jester, without a sober word to say ? No sooner did the coat and trousers which clad sone very ordinary well- meaning sort of youth approach her corner of the room, than her head was thrown back like that of the charger who scents the battle; she was on the " qui vive " in the presence of the enemy, and resolved to conquer him by fascination. Alas, poor fore-doomed fire-sider. She only frightened him away. He may have stayed to joke and chaff for a while, and then he went forth and ridiculed her among his male friends, and perhaps gave her some nickname which will stick by her as long as any of them remember her at all. As to marrying her, words won't express his disgust at the notion. And yet, after all, perhaps she is a girl who has the wherewithal to be attractive, if only she would not overlay Nature's work with this veneer of affectation. Men like a girl to be natural, kind, sensible, and sym- pathetic—in short, to be really womanly, a quality per- fectly compatible both with high mental culture and with athletic prowess. Whatever is aggressive or as- sumed will prove a dead failure in the matrimonial market. No doubt some girls will rejoin, "But we don't want to marry ; we don't care what men think of us." Then, we answer, if you do not care, why do you take so much trouble to be totally different to your own real selves when the eligible man makes his appearance? Moreover affectation is injurious in all social relations, for woman and children detect and dislike it as much as men do themselves. Every age has its especial defects, and perhaps the fin-de-siecle young woman would be too delightful alto- gether if this blight of self-consciousness did not breathe over the garden of her charms. It is certainly one of the reasons "why men don't propose." A quiet, refined manner, a frank, open glance, and a way of speaking modest and to the point, the cleverer the better, but free from slang, will go far to win the sex which is by no means so stupid as it sometimes seems. " The Prin- cess,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>DARK WOOD.</head>
        <p>DARK WOOD.</p>
        <p>A TALE OF LONEMOOR.</p>
        <p>BY MRS. HARRIETT LEWIS, A.utlior of "The Sundered Hearts," "The Bair Salome," "The Double Life," ke.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIX. PIETRO.S INQUIRIES. Lf.rd Darkwood's valet arrived at Penistone late hi the afternoon, and proceeded directly to the inn. ft er upper—for he was not one to do things hur- riedly, or to neglect hl creature comforts—be walked to the churchyard, which he had once</p>
        <p>visi.cd many years ago. Ile had no difficulty ha finding the grave he sought in ore corner, neglected, and covered over with dark eeds, with a dingy slab at its head, with the name " Magaalen " chisefled upon it. There was no other nu.und in all the churchyard so utterly forlorn and</p>
        <p>forsAken. Mrs. Quillet had never dared to plant a flower up na that grave, lest gossips should suspect the sevret she had guarded so carefully. She never visited it. The stone had been put up to protect the grave from invasion—to show that it was "permanent." Pietro stood beside the mound and read the name _ .</p>
        <p>upen the stone, and his dark sleek face wore a sLIIIIIr.Te and mocking smile. "They said that the butler at Lonemoor paid for the grave and funeral," he said to himself. "What a burial for her? Why, she was Squire Markham's only child and heiress. She had been brought up in luxury, she was her fathers idol, and yet here she lies buriad in a grave paid for out of the charity of two old servants. I wonder that she can rest in her grave. And what a death was hers! Crazy, wan- dering in the darkness and the storm of that wild December night, and stumbling into a hollow at last and lying there to die. I w nder that the si,nor is not haunted by her ghost 1" °lie stooped and plucked a handful of the weeds and seatte'red them to the winds. " Ive done some dark deeds in my time," he mused, "but I never did anything half so base as her murder. For the signor murdered her before she wandered back to Lonemoor. He made her in- sane. Is there such a thing as retribution, I wonder? Are people called to aft account hereafter tor their sins! Then, if so, my lord will have a long account to settle, and the blackest sin in all the list is the one he comMitted against Miss Markham." Again Pietro's eyes dwelt upon the name cut upon the stone. " Magdalen '1 he repeated. "I remember seeing her, so proud, so gay, so smiling, with her rich garments and her soft courteous ways, as true and gracious a lady as ever stepped. And she was but little more than a child. Who would have dreamed In those days that she would ever have perished as she did ?" He lingered a while longer among the shadows of the gloomy place, and then slowly returned to the Inn. The next morning after breakfast he hired a spring-cart and a moor pony and set out for Lone- moor. He had never visited the lonely old country house, his investigation years before being confined to Pen- istone. He traversed the moor, and came in sight of the grim mansion. Its windows were all closely shuttered; the air of very desolation reigned over house, and gardens, and stables. The gates were open. He drove into the grounds and around to the stable yard. Here he alighted, a lad holding his horse. "I want to see Mr. and Mrs. Quillet," said the valet, dropping a shilling into the stable boy's hand. "Are they at home ?" "Yes, sir," was the prompt answer, Pietro's fine garments and gold chain impressing the youth. " Here, Meggy, you take this gentleman in to see Mrs. Quillet,' he added, raising his voice. A girl, who was in the act of crossing the yard with a pail in her band stopped at this address and courtesied to the visitor.</p>
        <p>Pietro regarded her curiously. She was a buxom young woman, with a broad, red stupid face, and awkward movements. She addressed the valet in the broadest Yorkshire dia- lect, and bade him follow her." "She can't be the girl," thought Pietro, obeying her. "Miss Markham's child could never turn out like this. What a fool lam I The girl may be dead, or they may have put her in an orphan asylum. I shall probably not find her here." Yet he regarded the housemaid or two he en- countered on his way with the keenest scrutiny. His guide, who was an assistant dairymaid, with the most menial services to perform, and who knew nothing whatever of the duties of housemaid, con- ducted Pietro directly to the housekeeper's room, opened the door, and ushered him in without announcement. Mrs. Quillet was alone by her fire, the day being chilly, engaged in her usual work of darning house linen. She started up indignantly at Pietro's en- trance. He, with all the grace of a dancing master, his long thin, tightly-encased black legs showing considerable agility, bowed and advanced, and apologised after a most flourishing and lower class foreign fashion. "I came to see you on business, madame," he said, his accent betraying his alien tongue. "I pray you excuse me, but the maid brought me directly into madame's presence." "I don't want to buy anything, said Mrs. Quillet, suspiciously. "I don't want any jewellery or smuggled goods." The Maltese smiled, exhibiting his pointed white teeth. "I have nothing to sell, madame," he exclaimed. "I am come from a foreign country. I want to ask you simply a few questions." "I will call my husband," Baia the housekeeper. "You can talk with him." "No, madame, I prefer to talk with you," said the Maltose. "One moment—don't call your hus- band—l come to inquire about the young lady who is buried in the churchyard at Penistone—the lady whose grave is marked with a stone on which is the name Magdalen.' " Mrs. Quillet dropped into a chair. She was too startled to reply. What did her visitor know of the occupant of that dishonoured grave? The Maltese drew a chair near the hearth and sat down in it, apparently absorbed in the process of warming his hands. "It's a cold day, madame," he remarked. "November opens badly. These moors must be cold in winter.' "What have you to ask about the young lady buried at Penistone ?" asked the housekeeper, in a fluttering voice. "There is no date of death upon the stone," said Pietro, watching her sleepily with his half-shut eyes as a cat watches a mouse. "Did this lady die nearly seventeen years ago ?" Mrs. Quiliet nodded. "Did she come to Lonemoor in the month of November seventeen years ago ?" asked Pietro. Mrs. Quillet again assented. "1 have heard the story from the physician, or surgeon, who was called here to attend her upon ie night of her arrival," declared Pietro. "The case was very mysterious, so he considered it, and lie remembered her quite well. But he declared her insane, and said that she never uttered a co- herent word while in this house nor told her name." "That is true," said Mrs. Quillet, eagerly; "she never did." "She never told one word concerning herself?" "Not one word. She was insane r '• Yes ? And you had no means of discovering her identity ?'' Mrs. Quillet was silent. What could he mean? Had he any suspicion or knowledge of the real Identity of that wandering girl ?'' How could I have ?" she asked, after a long pause, which seemed to be terrible. "She wore no wedding ring; her clothes were not marked." "No wedding ring? Huns 1" said the Maltese, not betraying that the fact had been as well known to him as to the old housekeeper. "Have you not the least idea in the world, madame, who that girl really was Another terrible silence, which seemed to Mrs. Quillet a virtual confession, then she stammered a negative in a voice that contradicted her words. Pardon me," said the Maltese, politely, "but I am in search of a young lady who disappeared about the time this young woman appeared here. I have but lately got upon the track of your mysterious visitor. She may be—she must be—the same per- son." A load was removed from Mrs. Quillet's soul upon the instant. If this man were searching for someone he had formerly known he could not sus-</p>
        <p>veot her secret. IA great many people disappear -every year, I am told," she said still a little tremulously. "I hardly think the said, you are searching for was the one who appeared here." "She would have been likely to come here in her mad delirium," said the valet, reflectively, still watching the woman sleepily. "Had she been sane, this would have been the last place in the world which she would have visitedz_ but in ker</p>
        <p>insanity, With guiding reason wiertiarown, with uncontrollable longings, I think she would have made for the moors." "Yet you are mistaken, sir. The person you seek did not come here. The lady was English," asserted the housekeeper with rising courage. "The lady I seek was English." " Still, I am sure it was not the same," said Mrs. Quillet, desiring to put an end to his interrogatories. which she found exquisitely pain4ul. "Who was the lady you seek? What was her flame ?" The valet's sleepy eyes emitted a quick, sharp gleam. He reflected a moment, made quick de- cision and said deliberately— " Her name? It was Miss Clare Markham."</p>
        <p>The housekeeper was as if thunderstricken. Her face was grey in its sudden pallor. Her eyes protruded from their sockets. She looked as if threatened with a paralysis. She was literally stupefied. The name sounded in her cars as if shouted through a trumpet. The walls seemed to echo.it with a peculiar mockery. She gasped for breath. It was some minutes before her heart quieted in its t hrobbings : before her brain cleared; before she took in the full meaning of Pietro's words. And then her first thought was of the proud old squire. Was his name, after 8,11 there years to be given up to scorn and contumely? Was the dishonour of his old house to be proclaimed to the world? Was the name of her former nursling, whom she had loved to idolatry, to be flouted and sneered at by the very lowest people in all the country side? She could not bear it. People believed Miss Markham to have died abroad in all her youth and innocence. Must they know that she hid wandered back to Lonemoor in her insanity, and that she was buried in that dishonoured grave at Penistone ? Must the vulture that had gnawed so many years at Squire Markham s heart be made a show of to gaping gossip-mongers? Why had this man come here with these questions? Why had he spoken Miss Clare's name? He had known her perhaps? He knew the mystery of that last year of her life. Something better and nobler than curiosity filled the breast of Mrs. Quillet, as she resolved to probe the mind of this stranger. Perhaps he knew some good of her dead darliog ? Perhaps he could throw some ray of light upon that black and awful mystery in which her young life had ended? Yet, with the instindt of a tigress de- fending her young, she mud huskily— " Miss Clare Markham! Why, she died abroad eighteen years ago—a year before the woman appeared here." SO Stoke Markham gave out," said Pietro, coldly. "He declared her death because she was dead to him. But you and I know that she was not dead. The woman who appeared here—the woman who is buried in Penistone churchyard— was Miss Clare Markham."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>proi L'</head>
        <p>proi L'</p>
        <p>sistency of brown soft-soap. Spice or flavour to taste; i keep it n stone jars in a cool place. It should last good right into the spring.</p>
        <p>For years past a Sunday school by telegraph has been held weekly on an American railroad. The superinten- dent asks the class questions over a wire at different stations, prayers are said, and a chapter in the bible read—all by wire.</p>
        <p>Twenty-two acres of land are needed to sustain a man on flesh meat ; while the same amount of land under wheat feeds forty-two people ; under oats, eighty-eight people ; under potatoes, Indian corn or rice, 176 people ; and under the plantain, or bread-fruit tree, over 6,000 people.</p>
        <p>Fish Scallop. Pick any cold fish from the bone, and mix with it a teacup of milk or cream, a large teaspoonful of anchovy sauee, a little pepper and salt, and a saltspoonful of made mustard. Put it into a stewpan over a moderate fire for two or three minutes, stirring it all the time, then put it into scallop shells or a dish, stew some grated bread thickly over it, and a few thin pieces of butter; brown it and serve hot.</p>
        <p>Many of the most dangerous organic diseases may be traced to the common practice of drinking tea, coffee, and other fluids at a high temperature. The direct ap- plication of heat to the mucous membrane of the stomach excites an inordinate action of the blood- vessels, which, in time, becomes inflamed and ultimately the swelling stops the opening, when death must inevi- tably ensue. Fluids must be taken only at blood-heat, and placing the little finger in wkat you are going to drink is a simple practical test, for if this can be borne without inconvenience no danger need be appre- hended.</p>
        <p>-- •At you have a great deal of hair, a pretty way to ar- range it is to divide it, after fastening, into two. Then braid each half into a loose braid of four strands, and lay it round your head in such a manner that it will be quite broad and high on top, gradually sloping in and narrowing as it gets down the back. Ah, but there's nothing prettier than the smooth, shiny hair parted in the middle of the head, combed down over the brows, and then brought round to the back. If it is becoming to you, by all means adopt it. If it is not, be wise and let it alone. Here one is allowed more latitude. For the hair may be gathered quite low at the back, and waved a very little at the side. If there is any natural wave in your hair, then let the little locks cluster about your ears, and there's nothing more bewitching than this mode of arrangement. If you are very young you may wear your hair at the nape of the neck with perfect opriety.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CHAPTER XX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XX.</p>
        <p>TILE ITALIAN RECEIVES A CHEM. Pietro's assurance staggered Mrs. Quillet. Eti really knew what he asserted then. " Of course I have no wish to proclaim my know- ledge," said Pietro. ".1 have known this many years, but I have never breathed it to a living soul. The secret is safe with me. I knew Miss Markham well. She was living in London while you mourned for her as dead !" "You knew her ?" "Yes, and a nobler lady never lived.' The hot tears rushed to the old woman's eyes. Her withered mouth quivered. "Tell me something of her," she pleaded throw- • ing caution to the winds in her thirst to hear good tidings of her young mistress. "Was she married 2" The valet was silent. The housekeeper moaned and sighed bitterly. "Had she a lover ?" she whispered, presently. "Yes," said Pietro. "They were devoted to each other for the first few months; then they quarrelled and he cast her off. That's the whole story, madame —a sad story but a common one. She went insane, and then, possessed with the thought of seeing her father again and her old home, she must have wan- dered back to Lonemoor." " Why have you come to inquire after her ?" asked the housekeeper abruptly. "If she were what you say—and mind I deny that the girl who came here insane was Miss Markham—why should you inquire after her after all these years?' "Of course I have an object in view. All people are actuated by motives. I want to inquire after the child she left." "Miss Markham died at Berlin," said Mrs. Quil- let, resuming all her guardedness. "Everyone knows that in this vicinity. Her death nearly broke her father's heart. The woman who came here had evidently been a lady, although she hed become a mere vagrant. She left a child, as any one at Penistone can tell you." "What became of the girl It was a girl I have heard. Did she die 7"</p>
        <p>"No, she is living." " Is she here ?" asked Pietro eagerly. "No, she is not." "You put her in an asylum ?" inquired the Mal- tese disappointedly. "Not so. We educated her, my husband and I," said Mrs. Quillet. " And a week or two ago she left us." "She is educated? She went away to become a governess, I suppose?" "I believe that was her intention," declared the housekeeper, determined to admit to him only so much as he could readily learn from any country gossip, and to defend Miss Markhani's reputation to the last. _ _</p>
        <p>The announcement that the girl had been educa- cated rather dashed the spirits of the Maltese; for if she were educated she might hold herself as be- yond his reach. But he reflected that Mrs. Quillet might call the merest smattering of ordinary school-learning an "education." And besides, the girl's origin would prevent her rising very far above him. In fact, he would prefer her to have the varnish of a ladylike seeming. "Where is she now ?" he inquired. Now Gwen's whereabouts could not be discovered from any one in Yorkshire. Only the butler and his wife knew her address. And because of young Orkney's persistent attempts to discover it, and for other reasons, they had resolved to keep it secret. Mrs. Quillet saw no reason to break this resolve for the enlightenment of this foreigner. She did not like his appearance. It was clear that he had known Miss Markham, and was conversant with the mys- tery of her fate : but it was equally clear that be did not intend to reveal his knowledge. Why, there- fore, should she tell him what she knew? Moreover, she reflected that he had said, or intimated that Miss Markham had not been married There was no one then who had any right to the girl that could compare with her right. She de- cided not to give him Gwen's address. "What do you want of her ?" she asked. "That I will tell her,'' he answered. "1 decline to tell you where she is," said the housekeeper. "No one has any right to her. She is nothing to you." "I know her parents," said the Maltese. "I know all about her. I demand her address." "And I refuse to give it. That is, unless you give me good reasons for wanting it." Pietro was not prepared with "good reasons," Be flamed up with anger. "I have learned what I wanted to know," he declared. "I can find out her name from anyone in the neighbourhood. As for the rest, I can find out her address easily enough. I have only to ques- tion the station-master or booking clerk at Penis- tone station. She went away only a week or two ago. I shall have no difficulty in finding her." And quite satisfied with what he had learned, and the beginning he had made, Lord Darkwood's valet departed to pursue his investigations else- where.</p>
        <p>(To be continued).</p>
