      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>NEW BOOKS, &amp;c.</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKS, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>For those who know something about the use of the Slide Rule, and would like to know more, a nevi edition of a work on the subject has been edited by John Chadwick,engineer, Manchester, and published by John Heywood for 28. The new matter treats on the following subjects : The power transmitted through leather belts, screw cutting in the slide lathe ; the weight, strength, and safe working load for ropes of hemp, iron and steel wire, also for chains and metal bars; the maximum speed for turning metals ; also rules for finding the dimensions of the vital parts of a steam engine, Rm., iudeed,such is the utility of the instrument for performing arith- metical calculations with rapidity and certainty', and so easily is it understood, that it its ad vaia- tages and simplicity of operation were more gener- ally known,no bank,office, shop, or workshop,where numerical calculations of any description are re- quired, would be without it. Under the title of " The Lady's Dressing-room," a de- li,ghtfully interesting book is just published by Cassell and Co. It is a translation from the French of Baroness Staff° by Lady Colin Campbell, and treats on the study of hygiene and the cultivation of personal beauty,boldly,and yet delicately. It is essentially a ladies' book,and by being carefully read, and the advice contained in it being care- fully followed, will go very far towards making them " beautiful for ever." Another edition of " Boswell's Life of Johnson "Is issued by Macmillan and Co., edited, with an introduc- tion by Mowbray Morris. A great number of editions have been published of this famous Biography, the present one being printed from the text of the one issued in 1.804. by Malone, after the death of Boswell, whom he had helped with its progress from the first, and whose plan, so far as he had lived to indicate it, was carefully followed. We say the same of the boor as Boswell's friends said of him—it is" cheerful, and ' pleasant and gay," though like himself it has its darker moments ; which make its perusal more varied and . attractive than it otherwise would be. To say that Cassell's are issuing a number of penny illustrated stories, is to assure the reader that they are , much above the average of cheap literature. "Chums "published by the same firm, still holds its own, and ranks second to none among the boys' papers of the day. " The Strand Magazine" for March is full of interest- ing material, the " illustrated interview " article on the popular actor and actress, Mr. and Mrs. Kendal, being as interesting as anything in it. The companion to" The Strand" "The Picture Mag- azine "is as lively as ever, the " Comic Pictures "are the best we have seen for some time ; they are comic without being coarse. "The Young Ladies' Journal" is increasingly popular. The April No. contains plain directions and perfect illustrations of tho present fashions, in addition to manf useful and elegant designs for a great variety of articles not affected by changes of fashion, and a number short and continued stories, and useful recipes.</p>
        <p>WAS JACOB GROSS A FOOL? "1 can't make one of a party of thirteen,"he exclaimed; "some of us will be sure to die within a week." Thus spoke barber Jacob Gross,of Batavia,on the 12th of November last. The occasion was a dinner party. When the guests were all seated Gross noticed that there were thirteen at table.</p>
        <p>The others tried to laugh him out of his superstitiofl. but he insisted that he would not eat as one of the core' pant of thirteen. A fourteenth guest was therefore added to the number.</p>
        <p>"Now we're safe," said Gross, and the festivities pro' needed.</p>
        <p>Gross boarded at a hotel in Batavia. Ten days later the hotel was burned to the ground. The next morning the body of a man was found in the ruins. It was the body of barber Gross. Now,this is a curious thing to happen,certainly; but it is more than that. Do you believe the-o is anything itt the common notion that thirteen is an unlucky nutnber ? or that Friday is an unlucky day of the week? M much business is done on the 13th of the month as 03 any other date,and on Friday as on any other week dal' You wouldn't refuse to take thirteen eggs for a dozen if your grocer insisted on it, neither do you have more bad luck on Fridays than on any other day of the seven' No, no, it's all humbug and nonsense. Barber Gross's superstition had nothing under the sun to do with hie death. Besides, he dined as one of fourteen persons, n'3' thirteen. Don't be silly. Understand this : Nature indulges in no eenseless tricks. She kills men without hesitation for violating the laws of life, but not for assembling in groups of</p>
        <p>thirteen at dinner. Here we have a man who says was afraid to eat. Why, in Mercy's name, was he afraia to eat P Had he, too, some idle and foolish stuff in bi 4 head about bad luck ? Not a bit. He'd been glad enough to have eaten in a thirteen party on Friday i 9 the dinner would only have stayed on 1115 stomach andi digested after he got it down. But it wouldn't, nod'</p>
        <p>his fear grew out of that. - He says, " I had a fullness and tightness at the chest') after meals, and such a dizziness would seize me that could scarcely see. This was in the spring 1887. I fel' tired, dull, and heavy, with a sinking sensation at theli stomach. My appetite was variable, and I didn't k0°514 what to eat. In fact nathing seemed to suit me. There, was a feeling of weight and pain over the eyes and at the' back of my head. I became very weak, and it was witll difficulty that I kept on with my work. In this way I c°l/..' tinned for twelve months,during which time I saw a d°6e: tor and took various medicines; but none of them dial)). ) any good, and I grew worse. In June,lBBB, I read in the Darlington Times about a person who had been handleci just as I was, and had been cured by a medicine callecl Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. On the strength °</p>
        <p>this I got a bottle from my brother, William T.. 1 grocer, Copley Lane, and began taking it. In a sb°l4" time all pain left me, and I was able to eat and diget3 my food, and have since been well and strong. I staft take the Syrup occasionally and if I feel any signs Of old complaint, a dose or two sets me right. I collier, and have worked at Woodland Colliery for ove`' ten years. If you think the publication of this lettett: might be of use to others, you are at liberty to make °lli use of it.</p>
        <p>"Yours truly,</p>
        <p>JOSEPH TEASDAT'•</p>
        <p>(Signed) "Copley, Butterknowle, Durham, "November sth, 1891." Now that Mr. Teasdale is cured of his ailment, °did, gestion and dyspepsia, he would probably not refuse 3: invitation to dine with twelve other nice people 3,,,,crij1 day. And in such case we stand ready to guaran'—i, that none of the party will die within a week, especialiA if they all take a dose of Seigel's Syrup immediatell ° rising from the table.</p>
