      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>VITAL NATIONAL QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE VITAL NATIONAL QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The great question occupying the House of Commons tt present engages every mind of the British Empire. ihe entire kingdom, as well as the whole of Europe, !waits with increasing interest as the hour approaches ler" yea' or nay." What will the answer be? There are few times in life vihen so much seems to °Pend on these little words, but there is a time of still nlore vital importance : when the husband and father lies stricken with disease; when life hangs upon a slender thread ; when, in answer to the wife's appeal, !` Shall it be darkness or gloms, shall it be life or must ne die ?" the physican must answer" yea" or " nay." When Mrs. Fenn asked her family doctor and friend if her husband could live he said, "Nay, we can do no more for him." But in spite of this verdict, Mr. Albert Fenn, °f FlOily Cottages, Bawdeswell, East Dereham. Norfolk, rug saved from death to tell his own story :—" My rOubles began three years ago, when I was taken with pains across my back and shortness of breath, Which necessitated my calling, a doctor, who treated me ,1:„41 and on, as I felt better or worse, until October, 1890. ihe disease rapidly developed, and dropsical swelling, cdtdinencing in my feet, extended to the legs and abdo- Men. I was confined to the bed nine weeks, but as I aeetned better at Christmas, I got up, but caught cold, Which quickly brought on a relapse. The agony now beta • me intense, impossible to lie down or sit erect, but Zaa propped in a reclining position by pillows. By June e two attending doctors gave me up, and my nine children, four of whose were away, were summoned to saY 'good-bye.' And to-day, please God, lam as well as ever in my life and feel 1 have many years of active life before me. "I then measured sft. 2in. in girth and 2ft. arcund 1119 calves, my wife asked the doctor if she alight then tri Warner's Safe Cure (obtainable of all chemists and dealers, 2s. 9d. and 4s. ed. per bottle), to which he agreed, signing my club certificates. The doctor had ordered stimulants, as I was extremely weak, and for Inc time the new medicine did not seem to do me any good. I got discouraged but my wife said • Never dasPair! Although still very weak I discontinued the :”liskey to give the Safe Cure all possible chance and I,4lEnediately began to mend. I shall never target the 103' and relief I experienced when I was first able to lie 112 bed, which I had not been able to do for about a year. legs and feet burst, and water in astonishing quantities oozed from them almost continuously. My doctor's astonishment can be imagined, and his very 'Words were, " Well, if you recover, no nobleman in the I:ountry need die, and shall go out of busineAs." I have eleelated myself off the club, and written the secresary that if any of the brothers (Oddfellows), should be Unfortunate enough to suffer from Bright's Disease or IPS' derangement of the kidneys use Warner's Safe Cure. 't cures when doctor's acknowledge they are powerless. owl weigh fourteen stone, am the picture of health, Well and strong as ever in my life. A striking con- trast to the pitiful object of eighteen months ago, given by doctors, family, friends, and neighbours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>W-I.I_RRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>W-I.I_RRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>-- A special meeting of the Warrington Town Conn- e, 'was held at the Town Hall on Saturday morn- ing, for the purpose of appointing a borough treasurer and other officials. There were present tblie Mayor (Alderman E.Greenall), Aldermeßlack- burst, C. J. Holmes, T. 11. Sutton, and J. E. Wright; r, ''noncillors Shaw Green, 11. Roberts, A. Browne, Fairclough, Hepherd, Tinsley, Muir, Hutchin- ", Smethurst, Greening, Shaw, and Dr. Cannel!. Alderman HOLMES moved the appointment of Mr. Fairhurst as treasurer of the borough, at a salary of er annum ; and the appointment of Mr. W. H. Woodcock as collector of rates and assistant treasurer, a‘t. a salary of £2OO per annum, and that each of the ar',.'re officers be required to devote the whole of his time Ole duties of his office, and also that Mr. Fairhurst be Le;quired to give security in the sum of £l,OOO and Mr. goodcock in the sum of £5OO to be effected through a liarante, Society. It was also proposed " that Mr. rt Sutcliffe, of Bolto-., be appointed assistant sur- veyor, at a salary of £l5O per annum." blr moving this he said that the Finance Committee va,i7lng very carefully considered the question of the to"„e'lno7 of the borough treasurer's office unanimously lara,iuded to recommend Mr. Fairhurst to the post at a of all' °f 350 per annum. Mr. Fairhurst was 39 years yea-go, and had been in the service of the Corporation 19 oetijdsin aTtlole committee had no difficulty whatever in recommend Mr. Fairhurst on account of his tutgre.xPerience—they felt that he would prove in the ear.) The committee also recommended Mr. he had done in the past—a faithful servant. L f-200 to the office of first collector, at a salary of and . Per h.,a corium Mr. Woodcock was 32 years of age, Re had a! in the service of the Corporation 18 years. e the r ~,aYa been a reliable servant. With regard to guarant-i4:oo relating to the provision of a proper the Ri -ee lcir the officers recommended to be appointed, nance Committee decided to recommend that the seporation pay the premiums to some guarantee • e!Y- The committee having made inquiries found • it was the universal practice for Corporations to 13 a Y the premiums. Councillor Slum-Grackle, in seconding, said that he confidently expected that the gentlemen selected to fill the offices maned would come up to what was expected them.</p>
        <p>Councillor Him. moved an amendment that the salary of Mr. Fairhurst be fixed at £250 and that of Mr. Wood- cock at £l5O. He took that course because the rates of the town were already so very high, and it was only last Year that both had an increase in their salaries. From £lB2 Mr. Fairlsurst had an increase to £2OB, and a fur- ther advance to £350 in twelve months he submitted as a very large one indeed. About the same time Mr. Woodcock had an increase from £ll7 to £l3O, and to give him an advance to £2OO was too much. Whilst admitting that the duties of both officers were difficult, 3,1 that both were very competent gentlemen, and whilst further agreeing that the Corporation could not t cure better men to do the work, yet, having regard to :Te Present state of the town and the rates,he submitted that they should not throw away money too recklessly. 711ett the Council should further bear in mind that it wee Very likely in the course of time that Mr. Fairhurst Would think that he was entitled to an advance of salary 411,1,C19 doubtless, the Council would give him an increase. St. Helens, where there was a larger population than arrington, the Borough Treasurer was only in receipt of ,£250 salary. That amount, he thought, was a .very btu' salary to commence with, and as much as ought to given to him. With regard to Mr. Woodcock he had to objection to give him £2OO salary in a fepw years, he thought that £l5O was quite sufficient fer the 4"auent- (Hear, bear.) Councillor 6MISTITURST seconded the amendment, and °ritended that now a large rate was going to be levied rI salaries should of the epidemic he thought such high should not be paid. it Councillor Dr. CANNEL/. contended that the salary 1101"2 Proposed to give to two intelligent men was a 0 much, and he referred to Councillor Smethurst 8.41 'foreigner." (Laughter.) to uluncillor SuminnsT .sbjected to the term, and re- rt:ca_by calling the doctor" an Irishman." (Renewed vaaortr.). He must ask the doctor to withdraw the (I" foreigner." aoCo. uncillor Dr. CANNELL emphatically refused to do</p>
        <p>is The ir It • not out of order. r,OR : n I nr•-mencillor BROWSE said that he would like to enter his in-,c),tes,, t against the amounts of the salaries. The argu- -"' of Councillor Muir. he submitted, recoiled on the ;erninittee. He said that the Council could not get re- persons to take the offices named under the be—'ProPosed to be given. What had the committee to m doing to allow Mr. Fairhurst to collect the rates up n the Present at £lBO per annum, which figure had -nly been recently advanced to £2OB P Further, he twhOught that it was unjust to Mr. Fairhurst that Mr. p„?fideock should have £2OO per annum, whilst Mr. -17hurst had to be content for years on £lBO. By giv- -1 "r• Woodcock £2OO to start, the Council were It'factically admitting that Mr. Woodcock was a better coleZ than Mr. Fairhurst. It was unfair that Mr. Wood- pair, ehOuld start at practically the same sun? that Mr. ,gainnurst ended with. He publicly made his protest st the amount of the salaries. 't ~.4_aldertnan SuproN said that Mr. Fairhurst would have responsibility and the duties he would have to th.n"lh would lac more important and also require more r p„.e than his previous occupation. He thought Mr. ~:71!'hurst and Mr. Woodcock ought to be given to 1 0 iZlerstand that if they receive these sums, they must 3,4 "Poet any increase for a considerable number of ger:tl4. Fle did not see how they could well expect any It 4 I ernall to occupy a position of such responsibility at , h ess salary than £350 a year. Supposing Mr. Fair- , jir. Were to refuse the position altogether, he would i underl knew, could they find a man so. competent to k t4r, 2.:ke the duties of this office than he Is at a less sum isid £350? He did not think £350 was too much con- e Ili ring the amount of work there is to do, and they , 105 t not fvrget that the duties of the Borough Treasurer li _l4O materially increased during the past few years. The :nrcit connected with the office was twice or thrice as t'tat' has it was ten or 15 years age. He would therefore i;..i 'rod theresolution. r,;_`::ouncillor ROBERTS opposed the amendment whilst 'lying the gentlemen who had moved and seconded it, credir r e, , war ! ior an earnest desire to keep down the rates of Lii. un_, ring.ton within their proper limits. They seemed to .., uerrate the importance of the office. Ten years ago 1e Corporation increased the salary of the Borough treasurer to £5OO a year, and ten rears ago the popula- , of Warrington would be 10,000 less than it is now. lq, 'S rateability would be proportionately less' and the !,. r,eBPorisibilities and duties of the office would also be ilvsvT°Portionately less. Since that time they had become it, "rs of property worth a quarter of a million of money connection with which much must come under the care, so far as the financial arrangements are ceneerned, ei ,the Borough Treasurer. If the Corporation was v_gent ten years ago in increasing the salary of pue, 8 8 i blai he wrong wre there * now in giving £l5O a year less, when is the added responsibility of the financial manage-</p>
        <p>ment of the Water Works. Be also found that the ex- penditure of the finance department of the borough, by this proposal, would be £648 a year, against £936 a year under the past arrangement. The Finance Com- mittee would, therefore, have a margin of £238 to work Upon. A good deal had been said about the rates of the borough and the shopkeeping class. He (the speaker) had sprung from that class, and he challenged anyone to have a greater sympathy with them. Rut there were other ratepayers in Warrington besides shopkeepers. There were the large manufacturers who were suffering quite as much as the shopkeepers, but they did not find them protesting against any reasonable salary to the officers of the Corporation. The only argument he had heard against the proposed salaries was, that they could get men for less money, and St. Helens had been men- tioned, but he did not know whether the borough trea- surer there performed the same duties as at Warrington. The two officers proposed for these appointments have been allowed to do private work outside their duties, which it was now rightly proposed to prevent. The Council had deprived them of any opportunity of this kind, and they were both men of considerable ability. He thought, therefore, it would be wrong to support the amendment. Councillor R. T. FAIRCLOUGH briefly supported the re- solution, and the amendment was put. Councillors Hill, Smethurst, and Shaw voted for the amendment, and Alderman Wright and Councillor Browne did not vote. The minutes were carried by a large majority.</p>
        <p>THE RECTOR ON UNITARIANISM. SOME" AWFUL ERRORS." The fifth of the series of " instructions "on the six principal forms of dissent was given on Sunday evening at the Parish Church by the Rector (the Rev. F. W. Willis). There was a good congrega- tion. The RECTOR said the Unitarians were the lineal des- cendants of those Presbyterian ministers who were eject- ed in 1662. In 1778 nearly all the general Baptists,and the Presbyterians joined them' and they formed a separate communion in England. But America was THE LAND OF PROMISE for them—the land of their real success, with great men like Charming. Parker, and Emerson. He passed from their history to dwell, secondly,upon their strong points. The Unitarians had always made a great point of intel- lectual freedom, and they had reason to respect them for I it. But they should remember that man was not corn- posed of reason alone. There were three parts—con- science, imagination, and reason—and the Church sup- plied what was necessary for all three. She had her creeds, the faith once for all delivered to the Saints for the conscience; and she did not scruple to bring in the aid of music and art for the imagination, and the Church would be the last to debar people from the right of the full exercise of the power of reason. That was a strong point, and he believed that their ministers, as a rule, were very intelligent, very able men. Their second strong point was the unity of God,and rightly understood the Church would not quarrel in this matter. The Bible and the Church thought much of thq unity of God. They knew what they meant when they spoke of the unity of God, that it was a Trinity in unity, and a unityin Trinity The Unitarians did not think much of the unity of God, and they, as Churchmen, knew how they were taught that the Lord their God was one God, that "Thou shalt worship the Lord thy God with all thy heart, with all thy mind and with all thy soul." Now, the next stro'hg point with the Unitarians was the Fatherhood of God, the love of God, that they were His children, that God , loved them and everything He had made. That again, was a very strong point with them, and growing out of it, and the last one he would mention, was the brother- hood of man—that if God loved them they were His I children,and they ought to love one another. They I could see how the Church could go along and agree with the Unitarians in every one of those points. But now he came to what he must dwell upon, and what he must call their great errors, though he called them Christians in a sense, because they professed to be Christians, and in the prayer they used just before,it spoke of those who " professed to be Christians." But look at their two great errors, on which he should dwell at some length. The first was the DENIAL OF THE DIVINITY Or JESUS CHRIST. That seemed so dreadful to them as Churchmen, so aw- ful to think of the denial of the divinity :of Jesus Christ. They said that just as they held that the worship of the blessed Virgin Mary detracted from what was due to Jesus Christ, so the worship of Jesus Christ detracted from what was due to God But they said that Christ was God. They were told that they were to honour the Son even as they honoured the Father. The Unitarians said that if Jesus Christ was God, then Christianity was a most awful failure. Now he wanted to look at the other awful error of Unitarians. It followed naturally out of the other, the denial of the atonement. He was going to read to them how, accord- ing to the Unitarians, they understood the parable of the Prodigal Son. This was the way they said they had to read it in order to make their view right :—" But If Jesus held the current doetrine of the Atonement, then should we have to read : ' And he arose and came to his father. And though his father saw him when he was yet a great way off, yet he went not forth to meet him. And the son said unto him, Father, I have sinned against heaven, and in thy sight, and ain no more worthy to be called thy son.' And the father said to his servants, "Thrust him forth into outer darkness, for he is not redeemed.' But his elder son was in the field, and as he came and drew nigh to the house, he saw his brother weeping in the darkness of the night. And he had com- passion, and ran and said unto his father, ! Lay upon me the punishment of this my brother ; let me bear the burden of his sin, so shall he be redeemed, and dwell with us in the house. Then the fattier said be it as thou wilt.' And he said to his servants, Lay on this my elder son many stripes!' And they smote him sore till he cried out, ' My father, why bast thou forsaken me ? ' Then the father said to his servants, Call in my younger son from the outer darkness, for now can we make merry and be glad, for his chastisement has fallen on his brother !' " He (the Rector) said, in all charity and love, that that was a CariCSIUre,RII awful misrepre- I sentation. Why did he suffer that awful agony ? Other men had gone to death with a smile on their lips, Ridley, Latimer and others. They could bear their torture with a smile. Why did Christ suffer such untold agony ?—becaus6i He knew the importance of His death. The teaching of the ' last three centuries in many parts of the Church had been substitution—that Jesus Christ died in their stead. llt was easy to see the injustice of that—that thejust should die for the unjust. Again, they could see the awful difficulty arising, the dilemma in which they were put, that they still had to die. fie wanted to show them that the teaching of the Fathers was not substitution, but that JESUS CHRIST WAS THEIR REPRESENTATIVE. That was what he wished to bring out, that Jesus was not a man, but the man. mankind. It all flowed naturally from this—the Jesus Christ, the Son of God, took their nature upon Him before birth, and so ne was a man. If they had seen Jesus Christ in the streets they would have said" There is a man," but they would have been wrong. There was only one person in Jesus Christ, a divine person, having twe natures, the human and the divine. He. the sinless son of God, took upon Himself their sinful nature and in that nature He died. He was their representative, their proxy, not their sub- stitute. There were three most diffizult words, but most important words to be understood in connection with their Lord's death, namely. PROPITIATION. REDEMPTION, RECONCILIATION. Propitiation satisfied God's law of holiness by a sinless one dying in a simple nature. He did not die in their stead, but lie died on their behalf as their representative, and they died in Him. Redemption was the result of propitiation in relation to Satan. It was the idea of some, notably of Origen, but only of some during the first thousand years, that a m ranso was paid to Satan, but don't make the mistake a certain Unitarian minister made, of thinking that all the fathers believed that. He would show them that the Church did not believe that any price was paid to the Devil or to God. He said that the idea of some, but only a few, and notably Origen, during the first thousand years, was that a ransom was paid to Satan, but the catholic fathers of the Church did not say so. and a thousand years after Christ Anselm taught that the ran- som was paid to God. Now, both were wrong. It was that just as God redeemed the Israelites from Egypt by the blood of a lamb, as an acknowledgment for the blood, or soul, or life—it was the same word in the original—and no compensation was paid to the Egyptian bondmaster, or to the destroying angel, so Christ redeemed them by His own blood, and no compensation was paid to Satan or to God ; His blood or life being an acknowledgement for theirs. They saw he had explained ' the meaning of the word redemption. Now, let them look at the other word, reconciliation. Just as redemp- tion was the result of propitiation in relation to Satan, so reconciliation was the result of propitiation in relation to God. The word reconciliation was the same as atone- ment' and if they spelt atonement in this way—at-one- meet, they got to the truth. They were reconciled to God by the death of Jesus Christ, and God was recon- ciled to them. What did that mean 7—simply that the bar of sin was removed, and so God's grace and mercy could flow down into the soul.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>SUGGESTED BOYCOTTING OF A LIBERAL BREWER.</head>
        <p>SUGGESTED BOYCOTTING OF A LIBERAL BREWER.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening Mr. Asquith, in reply to Mr. Flynn, said his attention had been called to a letter wherein the writer asked his fellow traders to boycott Mr. S. Whitbread and his son because of their action on the Local Veto Bill. It appeared to be a public declaration by the writer of his intentions to cease to dpal with Messrs. Whitbread and Co., and an invitation to others to follow the same course , —(Laughter.) He was not aware that such a statement constituted a breach of any law. In any Case, he thought the firm in question was well able to take care of itself.—(Cheers.) Mr. Flynn : Is the right hon. gentleman aware that for much less aggravating utterances editors and print- ers of newspapers in Ireland have been sent to Frison ? —(Cries of" Order.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>TB E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 25, 1893.</head>
        <p>TB E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 25, 1893.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. I celebrated</p>
        <p>NEWCASTLE PROGRAMME. ..nd every election in England, Ireland, Scotland, and Wales was fought upon the policy enunciated in that programme. He failed, therefore, to see on what ground the great constitutional struggle should be repeated in the constituencies, except upon some: question of detail and not of principle. (Hear, hear.) Ii r. Burgess gave his vote with the full knowledge that these questions were before the public, and that they were questions with which the Liberal party were bound to deal. It was, therefore, somewhat inconsistent for any man who voted Liberal (he assumed Mr. Burgess voted Liberal) to turn round now and say that " I am a Liberal, but before all things I am a Churchman." Mr. Burgess should have taken up his position at the election. lie had known Mr. Burgess all his life, and there was no I man in Warrington for whom he had a greater respect. or whose friendship he valued more hiahly,but hoped he would see the error of his ways yet,and make as public a recantation of his mistake at some early time as he had done of his desertion of the Liberal party. (Applause.) Mr Roberta read extracts from Mr. Buraess's speech, including the following:—" If it was found necessary to make an appeal to the constituencies before the Irish Church was disestablished, they had a greater right to do the same now,when they considered the case of the great National Church .1 the country. Did it not seem strange that he (Mr. Gladstone) of all men should lend the glamour of his great name to a scheme which would paralyse the development of Christianity in Wales, and daunt the energies of Churchmen ? Why was that , gross act of imustice to be committed? It made one's I blood almost rise within him to think of it." If (Mr. Roberts said) there was one thing more than another about which the people of this country were con- vinced in the character of Mr. Gladstone it was his un- doubted Christianity. (Applause.) All his powers of statesmanship, his personal rectitude of character, his great abilities as an orator and statesman combined would not have given him the tremendous hold he had upon the English people if it had not been for his undoubted piety, the Christian nobility of the life he had lived during the past 50 years. Was it possible that a man living as Mr. Gladstone did on the verge of , eternity, with all his undoubted characteristics, and his I intense love of the Church in which he was born, and to which he had belonged all his life, would do anything to paralyse it or daunt its Christianity? (Applause.) Mr. WILLIAM FIERPOINT, in supporting the motion, welcomed Messrs. Guest and Crosfield amongst them. The band of Liberal leaders in Warrington was small, and they were, therefore, only too pleased to find that gentlemen outside their ranks in some measure took the trouble to give them their support. They were expect- ing great things from Mr. Arthur Crosfield. (Applause.) They were indebted to him for what he had done in the past, and he was able to assure the meeting that he had done more for the Liberal party than appeared on the surface. When he was relieved from the cares of the large business he had upon his hands they would, he trusted, be able to see him occupying a position of con- siderable importance and interest to them as Liberals. (Applause.) Mr. Pierpoint then proceeded to refer to the Home Rule and other Bills. Be contended that the Tories did not like the policy of Home Rule, because when it was cleared out of the way they would have a freer and a wider field for pushing on the GREAT aIIESTIONS OF DOMESTIC POLICY which the Conservatives did not wish to see brought for- ward. That, in a great measure, was at the bottom of their obstructive policy. (Applause.) Alluding to the Veto Bill, he asked how many publicans in Warrington would receive compensation if it were allowed by law? Would any of Messrs. Walker's, Messrs. Greenall's, or Mr. Boddington's managers get it? No. The whole of the compensation would go into the hands of the squire at Walton Hall, and into the hands of the executors of the late Sir A. B. Walker. The aroount that would be received by the keepers of the houses would be very small indeed. (Hear, hear. \ Then there was the Welsh Suspensory Bill and Mr. Burgess had expressed the fear that there was going to be Coercion perpetrated by the Liberal party. He did not think it was likely there would be Coercion not so much indeed as was practiced be even the party to which Mr. Burgess had attached himself Mr. Pierpoint concluded by a reierence to the presentations to Dr. Houston arid to Sir Gilbert Greenall, and to the order for the articles given to the last-named gentleman being given to a Liverpool firm. The tradesmen of Warrington were, he said, passed over. Whenever possible a favour of this kind should be given to Warrington tradesmen, but no doubt the committee of selection wanted a " little trip to Liverpool." (Laughter and applause.) Mr. W. ROBERTS also supported the resolution. The resolution was then put to the meeting, and unanimously and heartily agreed to. Councillor FLEMING then proposed that the best thanks of the meeting be accorded to Messrs. E. J. Guest and Arthur Crosfield for their speeches, and remarked that the presence of those gentlemen amongst them was a good sign for the future. (Hear, hear.) Owing to the long distance he lived out of town, Mr. Guest had not been able to do as much for them as a party as they would have liked, while with regard to Mr. Crosfield they expected even better things of him in the future. (Hear, hear.) Mr. T. Moutas seconded the resolution which was carried amidst acclamation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SUPPORTING THE GOVERNMENT'S POLICY.</head>
        <p>SUPPORTING THE GOVERNMENT'S POLICY.</p>
        <p>SOME LOCAL CRITICISMS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the members of the Warrington Liberal Association was held in the Liberal Club on Tuesday evening, when there were present :—Dr. Gornall (in the chair), Councillors H. Roberts, J. 11, Fleming, G. Plumpton, and Messrs. A. Crosfleld, W Pierpoint, E.&amp; Guest, T. Morris, Wm. Roberts, J Harrison, Elijah. Ford, T. Pemberton, and others. The object was to consider a resolution approving , of the policy of the present Government. LETTER FRODit DLR. EYLLNDS. The following letter had been received from Mr. John Rylands, J.P. :—" The Grange, Thelwall, near Warrington' March 21st, 1893. Mr. J. Clayton. Sir,—l find I shall not be able to attend the meet- ing to-night, and I regret to miss any opportunity of protesting against the avowed obstructive policy of the so-called gentlemanly party in Parliament. Hoping you will have a good meeting, 1 am, yours truly, JoHN RYLANDS." The CHAIRMAN briefly explained the objects of the meeting and having expressed his approval of all that the present Government had so far done, called upon Mr. E. J. GUEST to move the following resolution : " That this meeting of the Warrington Liberal Associa- tion, desires to express its unabated confidence in Her Majesty's Government, and its hearty approval of the various measures introduced by them to the House of Commons, which it firmly believes will tend to promote the welfare and prosperity of the people, and trusts— that notwithstanding the obstructive policy adopted by the Tory party—they may speedily become law."—Mr. Guest remarked that he was sorry he had hitherto been unable to take a very active part in the Liberalism of Warrington ; but it was not the result of any lack of sympathy with the cause; it was solely in consequence of certain behests upon his time in consequence of his private life—his health, in fact. He had felt it his bounden duty that evening to come and express his sympathy with them. (Applause.) It had been ru- moured that the Liberal Association were despairing of the success of their body. The rumour was an absurd one, and it would be a disgrace to the town of Warring- ton if such a thing were to happen. Warrington had pandered too long to the influences of the Tory party. (Applause.) A party which had done nothing of benefit to the town, but which had succeeded, with singular success, in crushing and destroying public spirit. Such a man as the late Mr. John Walker, of the Warrington Examiner, had done more to arouse public spirit and honest opinion than half-a-dozen of the foremost leaders of the Warrington Conservative party. (Loud applause.) He was glad to think that the line of demarcation be- tween Liberalism and Toryism was becoming more clearly defined than it had been for a considerable period. There was a little time ago a tendency for the Tories to rob the Liberals of their measures, and there was not that clear distinction between the two parties that there was now. but it was very clear that day. (Hear, hear.) There was no doubt that the Tories were showing themselves the party of privilege and the Liberals as ever the party of justice, and they had privi- lege and justice contending one against the other. Justice was at the root and centre of every measure the Liberal Government was bringing forward—justice to Ireland, the Home Rule Bill. (AN:Luse.) They had given the franchise to Ireland. They had asked Ireland what she wanted, and she said she wanted a domestic Parliament for the management of her own internal affairs. He believed Mr. Gladstone's measure would give that power to the Irish people consistently with the maintenance of the empire. If there was a collision —and he did not think there would be any more than there was between Colonel Saunderson and Mr. Redmond in the lobby of the House of Commons—(laughter)—it would be the fault of the Orangemen and the Tory party, and not the fault of the Nationalists, and it would be a disgrace to them. Alluding to the Local Control Bill, he characterised it as a measure of justice ; he did not see any injustice in it at all. The Conservatives talked about not taking away the living of the publicans. He believed the brewers were putting that forward. The brewers did not care about giving compensation to the publicans. If they were turned out of their living they would not pay them compensation, and if publi- cans had raised the position of public-houses they would not give them the "unearned in- crement." Where was the justice of maintaining the Church in Wales F The should be remembered, was elected after a considerable</p>
        <p>struggle in the censtitUencies upon three questions more particularly than upon any otheri—Home Rule for Ire- land ,Disestablishment and Disendowment of the Church, and Local Option These questions were included in the endowments and income of the Established Church there</p>
        <p>amounted to about £250,000. The poor Nonconformists I in Wales contributed for the support of their Church 1 £400,000 a year, and when they considered the large incomes the bishops of the Church of England received, ( and the difference of £250,000 as compared with the £400,000 did not represent the relative disparity between the Dissenters and the Church of England. Thirty-one ( Welsh members out of 34 were in favour of the dises- tablishment of the Welsh Church, and those 31 were elected by an average majority of something like 2,000 each, and the three Tories who contended for an Estab- lished Church in Wales were returned by a majority of about 200. Thus they would see that an overwhelming majority of the people in Wales were anxious for a , Disestablished Church, and they had a right to have it. (Applause.) Mr. ARTHUR CROSFIELD who was received with loud applause, seconded the motion, and contended that, to take the Cabinet man by man, they never had a stronger one than at present, a Cabinet containing more earnest, thorough-going men. (Haar, hear.) In seconding the resolution he did not think they were necessarily bound to include every item the Government bad initiated. They were now fairly launched on the great era of posi- tive legislation, and there must inevitably be some difference of opinion as to the method and degree to which that positive or constructive legislation could be carried. Individual freedom of thought had always been a healthy and sound distinguishing feature of the the common sense that taught them that whilst they Liberal party, and it was by no means incompatible with must have minor differences of opinion they could not all have their awn way with regard to Government or to other questions. If even a first class Liberal Govern- ment was not perfect, as compared with that a Conser- vative Government was fourth or fifth best. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) The Tories were on the eve of an attempt to do—what? to undermine the whole fabric, upon which Liberalism was based—he meant t) under- mine the principle of trust in the people' and the devel- opment in a healthy direction which responsibility fwlrp might bring with it. The Conservatives were going in that great which, ieltalaatkr icampaigntnogppoosti all nt by ignore every argument with y through point, theyte latehad years.d ri ye They n them • ignored all, and appealed to ignorance and prejudice on the one hand and the incompetence of so many so-called politicians on the other hand. (Applause.) Councillor ROBERTS, in supporting the resolution referred to the remarks delivered last week by Mr. C. S. ' Burgess at the Chuich Defence meeting held in the • Co-operative Hall. He said the present Government, it</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>_...-w-... THE LIBERATOR FRAUDS.</head>
        <p>_...-w-... THE LIBERATOR FRAUDS.</p>
        <p>DIRECTORS CONVICTED. The trial of Wright and Hobbs for forgery and fraud in connection with the Liberatorßuilding Society resulted on Wednesday in a conviction. Sentence was deferred, and a charge against Hobbs of making false entries in books was opened. At a meeting of the creditors of J. W. Hobbs and Co., Limited, yesterday, accounts were presented showing liabilities amounting to nearly 3i millions and a deficiency of £453,098. The proof of the Liberator Building Society was for £2,105,694. The Chairman said he could hold out no hope of any dividend as the assets were all mortgaged.</p>
        <p>ARE YOU ILL? Sufferers from Gravel, Lumbago. Piles, Pains in the Back, Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaints. Diseases of Kidneys, Bladder, Urinary Organs, Stone, Gleet, Stric- ture, Sciatica, Rheumatism, and Gout, will find a positive cure in Holdroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box, and if not satisfied your money will be returned. Price Is 11d. Of all c,iemists, or post free for 12 stamps from B OLD- ROTC'S Medical Hall, Cleckheaton, Yorks. Don't be put off. If you cannot get them write to the Proprietor, and a box will be sent west post.—Advt. The trial of Mr.de Cobain, an ex-M.P.for east Belfast, on criminal charges preferred against him by the autho- rities, was held at Belfast, on Tuesday. Several wit- nesses gave. evidence of a grave character against the accused. The jury found the prisoner guilty, and he I was sentenced to twelve months' imprisonment.</p>
        <p>TDROAT AFFECTIONS AND 110ARSENE8S.—AU suffering from irritation of the throat and hoarseness will be agreeably surprised at the almost immediate relief afforded by the use of " Brown's Bronchial Troches." These famous " lozenges" are now sold by most respectable chemists in the country at Is lid per box. People troubled with a 'hacking cough," a.' slight cold," or bronchial affections, cannot try them too soon, as Similar trOokles, if allowed to progress, result Is serious Pulmonary and Asthmatic affec- tions. See that the words " Brown's Bronchial Troches "are lon the Government stamp around each box. Prepared by I Joss I. BROWN SONS, Boston. U.S. European depot, 3, Parringdon-road. London. -Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL SANITARY</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL SANITARY</p>
        <p>AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting was held at Dutton Workhouse, on Tuesday. Mr. J. Mclntyre presided, and there were present the Rev. J. W. N. Tanner, Messrs. H. E. Heywood, J. Baguley, E, Aaron, T. Lewis, J. Brandreth, James Whitlow, E. Bretherton, J. Weir, Joseph Knowles, W. Whitlow, A. Jackson, John Ashton (clerk), and G. Ashton (deputy clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>HALTON BROOK SEWERAGE-.</head>
        <p>HALTON BROOK SEWERAGE-.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from Messrs. Potts, Potts, and Gardner, stating that Mr. Evans, agent to Sir Richard Brooke, has approved of a course proposed to be taken in regard to the drainage into Halton Brook, and the Clerk was instructed to enter into an agreement.</p>
        <p>TV E MEDICAL °mom In the absence of Mr. Williams, who had been called away to London, Mr. Heywood moved that Dr. Joseph Adams be re-appointed medical officer for the ensuing year, at a salary of £l2O per annum. In a letter he had written, Mr. Williams stated that he should very much like to have been present to bear testimony to Dr. Adams' zeal and very active interest in the Authority's business during the smallpox epidemic. To that extent he could speak from personal experience, such as no other member of the Authority had had, and he had no hesitation in stating that he dil his duty to the utmost which circumstances permitted, and at great personal sacrifice of time and convenience. To him and Mr. Farrington he felt sure the Authority were indebted for almost a total cessation of the trouble, and both well deserve especial consideration. Mr.Farrington had been indefatigable, and he had taken risks which no money could pay for,and all (like Dr. Adams) outside his actual duty.—Mr. T. Lewis seconded the motion, which was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>PENNY FLECK FARM.</p>
        <p>The following let-e;i;;Aiii-e-s--n 1:e-Cei-C:ed re this place : Westwood, Whalley Range, Manchester, March Bth, 1893. Dear Sir,—l am much obliged for your letter,and would not have written you so pressingly but the place is not fit to live in as your inspector, who has been to see it, will no doubt have told you, and the landlord I simply refuses to do anything, whatever either to the house or its surroundings ; the fences are all broken down and five or six gates deficient. Of course this does not concern you, but my son has been foolish in placing himself under such a landlord.—Yours very truly, W. G. Thompson.—The Medical Officer said he had visited the Farm, Crowley (owner Mr. Sutton, Antrobus), and found the cellar flooded with water and the walls soaked with damp. This condition constituted a nuisance, dangerous and injurious to health. The cellar should be properly drained and the sewerage arrangements should be rectified.</p>
        <p>SMALLPDX DECREASING. The Medical Officer (Dr. Adams) roported that a death occurred at the Smallpox Hospital on Monday, only nine cases remain now. Two cases of typhoid fever had occurred at Halton. The Nuisance Inspector said Mr. Wesley Haddock had died from the latter disease, and before his death he attributed the cause of the fever to the insanitary condition of the weighing machine place at Sutton Weaver Station.—The Inspector was instructed , to deal with the matter. PROPERTY OWNERS AND WATER SUPPLY. The following letter was read: 9; Eunismore Gardens, S.W. March 17. Sir,—l am directed by Mr. Brunner to acknowledge receipt of the resolution you send him on behalf of the Runcorn Union, and to say that the matter 1 of that resolution has his approval and will enlist his support should any measure be taken to give effect to it. —Yours faithfully, Corm; H. BROWNING. Jno. Ashton, Esq.—The resolution referred to declared that the cost of providing water supplies should be borne by the 1 owners of property and not by the occupiers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>RUNCORN COMHL;SIONERS AND THE DISINFICTOR.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN COMHL;SIONERS AND THE DISINFICTOR.</p>
        <p>I letter from Mr. Marshall, secretary to Runcorn Beard of Commissioners, asking upon what terms the Authority would allow that body the use of the disin- fector and conveyance.—The question was referred to a Committee.</p>
        <p>THE INSPECTOR AND SMALLPDX. Mr. Heywood gave notice that at the next meeting he would move that extra remuneration be paid Mr. Far- rington, the sanitary inspector, for his extra services he had given in regard to the smallpox epidemic and disin- fector. On that day Mr. Heywood said he would be pre- pared to name a sum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>BRITISH AND FoREIGN BIBLE SOCIETY. MEETING OF WARRINGTON AUXILIARY.</head>
        <p>BRITISH AND FoREIGN BIBLE SOCIETY. MEETING OF WARRINGTON AUXILIARY.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Auxiliary of the British and Foreign Bible Society was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Tuesday night. There was a good attendance. The chair was occupied by Mr. R. Garnett, J.P., and among those present were the Revs. E. C. E. Carleton, J. Yonge, J. Kirtlan, T. Ross, J. Warnock, and E. T. Reed, and Messrs. John Fairclough, S. B. Stringer, W. Barlow and P. Silcock, and Miss Bessie Dutton. Mr. Garnett in his opening remarks, regretted the absence of Mr. J. Chariton Parr, who was advertised to take the chair that evening, but who had found it impassible to attend. He said that the Bible did not need any defence, and he only wished that his hearers would read mere of the literature put forward by the Bible Society. He was quite certain that there were two societies, which, if more were known about them, people would become much more in touch and in sympathy with them. They were the British and Foreign Bible Society, and the Mis- sionary Society. He was surprised that people of edu- cation knew so little about them. The Rev. John Yong* in the absence of Mr. William Owen, then read the treasurer's report which showed that the receipts for the past year were £212 15s. ld., and expenses, including a free gift of £lO5 to the parent society, were £ll2 3s. 10d leaving a balance of £lOO us. 3d. The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton moved the adoption of the report. He agreed with the Chairman that if people knew more about the society, they would give it more support. It was the handmaid of Missionary Societies. Mr. W. Barlow seconded the motion, and it was carried unanimously. Upon the motion of Mr. JonN FAIRCLOUGH, seconded by the Rev. T. Ross, the following officers were elected :—President, Mr. J. Charlton Parr ; vice- presidents, Sir Gilbert Greenall, and Mr. John Cros- field ; treasurer, Mr. Wm. Owen ; secretaries, Revs. E. C. E. Carleton and John Yonge, and Mr. Percy Silcock ; committee, Messrs. E. J. Guest, John Guest, J. Hall, C. H. Kerry, John Fairclough, and the local clergy and ministers, and the following ladies—Miss Harding, Mrs. Garnett, Miss Dutton, Miss Winifred Smith, Mrs. Armi- tage, Mrs. J. Fairclough, Mrs. Gernall, Miss Garner, and Mrs. Reed. The introduction of ladies on the committee is a new departure,and much good was expected from it. —The Rev. F. C. Moss afterwards gave an interesting lecture on missionary work in Madagascar,illustrated by limelight views. The lantern manipulater was Mr. A. H. Lee. The lecturer, referring to the Roman Catholics, said that they made very little headway among the hea- thens, and most of their converts were from the Pro- testants. The Bible in the Mallagasy language had re- cently undergone a revision, and 10,000 had been sent to Madagascar and were sold the first day! A vote of thanks to the Lecturer was passed, on the motion of the Rev. E.T. REED, seconded by Mr. S.B. STRINGER, and the meeting closed with a vote of thanks to the Chairman,pro- posed by the LECTURE a, and seconded by Miss Durrox.</p>
        <p>fence,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. In the House of Commons on Monday evening, Mr. GLADSTONE, replying to questions, said the Government did not intend to take part as a Government in the dis- cussion of the Channel Tunnel Bill, but to leave it to the House.—Mr. LABOUCHERE called attention to Sir G. Pon- TAL's Mission to Uganda, which he strongly condemned and moved a reduction of the vote referring to it. Mr. GLADSTONE said Sir G. Portal had not gone to Uganda to bind by any proceeding the liberty of the Government er the House, but had gone to report on the best means of dealing with the government of that country. Mr. A. J. BALFOUR said it would be impossible for any English Government te withdraw from the responsibility which had been undoubtedly undertaken. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN said we could not leave the country, whatever it might cost. The Uganda vote on a division was passed by 368 to 46. TUESDAY. In the House of Commons on Tuesday afternoon Mr. Fowunt moved for leave to introduce a bill to make better provision for local government in England and Wales. After giving details of the constitution of the proposed parish councils, the mode of their election, and the powers to be conferred upon them, he said the House had only to look at the great towns, such as Manchester, Birmingham, and Leeds, to see what had happened within the last half century through the people govern- ing themselves,and he hoped that similar benefits would be conferred on the rural parts of the country. No qualification for membership Was to be required, and women were to be capable of serving. Proceeding to the question of the poor-law unions, the right hon. gentleman said the bill abolished ex-officio guardians and all qualifications, and the voting was to be by ballot, with one man one vote. Those who represented the rural districts would be constituted the rural district council ft.r the district which they represented. The bill was brought in and read a first time. At the even- ing sitting Sir. J. LUBBOCK moved a resolution in favour of giving local authorities power "to carry out the general wish's of the shopkeeping community with reference to the hours of closing." Mr. Aserrrn ex- pressed his own sympathy with the motion, but said the Government desired that hon. members should feel themselves perfectly free to vote according.to their own wishes. He himself should vote for the resolution. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN expressed the opinion that the unanimous vote of the House on this motion would pledge it to speedy legislation. After some further discussion the resolution was agreed without a division. WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday, Mr. GLAD- STONE in reply to Mr. LABOUCHERE, said the whole ques- tion with regard to Uganda, bristled with points of difficulty, on no one of which had they information sufficient to form an opinion. The Under Secretary for Foreign Affairs had a right. he thought, on further in- formation to express a different opinion of his own. The Consolidated Fund Bill was read a second time. Captain Grice-Hutchinson moved the second reading of the Clubs Registration Bill, which aimed at the suppres- sion of bogus clubs. The motion was seconded by Mr. Caine. Mr. Shaw-Lefevre said the Government sup- ported the bill. After discussion it passed the second reading, and was referred to a Select Committee. Mr. T. Wrightson moved the second reading of the Work- ing Men's Dwellings Bill, which was agreed to. Mr. Bolitho moved the second reading of the Sale of Intoxi- cating Liquors (No. 2) Bill, which sought to create a licensing board and establish a local veto. The debate on it was adjourned after Sir William Harcourt had promised that the Government would support the second reading.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY. Ai the House of Commons onThursday evening, Iselr.A. J. Batyoun asked whether the Premier was not aware that there were certain broad issues connected with the Government which could not be raised except on the vote on account, and that gentlemen sitting on the Opposition side of the House in previous Parliaments had thought themselves justified in circumstances not more grave than the present :in asking for a day for a vote of censure on the Government. He asked Mr. GLADSTANE YO give the Opposition a day before Easter for the discussion of a vote of censure in regard to Ireland. Mr. GLansToNn said that when the right hon. gentleman declared the motion he was going to bring forward he should be prepared to deal with it, and consider what day should be given to the discussion. Mr. BARTON moved the adjournment of the House in order t 3 draw attention to " the danger to public peace and security of property in Ireland caused by the condonation of serious crime by the Executive Govern- ment." He based his case on the release of a man named FOLEY, convicted at Cork under the Explosives Act. Mr. JOHN MORLEY defended the action of the Irish Exe- . cutive in this particular instance, and also its policy generally in these cases. Mr. ASQUITH and Lord R. CHURCHILL took part in the debate, which resulted in the rejection of Mr. BARTON'S motion by 262 to 222 majority for the Government, 40. After the division Mr. BALFOUR handed in the terms of the proposed vote of censure. Mr. FOWLER moved the second reading of the Registration Bill. Mr. BALFOUR spoke of the measure in adverse terms, and expressed a hope that it would be referred to a select committee.</p>
        <p>LORD SALISBURY'S VISIT TO ULSTER. Lord SALISBURY has, on the advice of his medical man, decided to postpone his visit to Ulster, and the meeting he had arranged to address at Belfast on the 3rd of next month will be deferred to a later date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held in the Town Hall on Wednesday. There were present, Messrs. R. Heaton (in the chair), Balharry, Ratcliffe, Shaw, Davies, W. Houghton, J. Houghton, A. Jolley, A. J. Jolley, Smith, Longton, Gill, Hill, Muir, Aukland, Swinton, Clarke, Kilshaw, Mather, Pilling, Turton, Warburton, Hibbert, and Dr. Pattison. Messrs. J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (deputy clerk), C. .D. Ward (mastel), and H. M. Mines (superintendent of the Padgate Schools.) The minutes of the last meeting were confirmed. The list of cheques, among which was a sum of .£55 allowed to the clerk for additional expenses, was also passed, on the motion of Mr. Ratcliffe, se- conded by Mr. Kilshaw. HOUSE COMMITTEE. The Clerk announced that the House Committee had prosecuted Wm. Tomlinson for absconding from the House. He was brought before the magistrates and was sent to gaol without hard labour. The cost in this case amounted to £2 Os. Id., a large sum the Clerk thought for so small a punishment.—Mr. Davies said that the Committee would not have thought it worth while to go on with the case, but they thought it a mat- ter of principle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>COMPLAINT.</head>
        <p>A COMPLAINT.</p>
        <p>The CLERK received a letter from the Local Govern- ment Board respecting a communication they had re- ceived from MaryiGarmoyle, who lives in Warrington, who complained that her husband had been refusod re- lief by the doctor, unless he went into the house. The Local Government Board had forwarded the letter to the Clerk, and also said that they could not interfere with the local authorities in the matter. It was resolved to put tha matter into the hands of Mr. Shearer to make inquiries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>SCHOOLS COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOLS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Schools Committee recommended that two men be employed to carry out work at the reservoir at Pad- gate,and that 10,000 common bricks be procured for the same purpose, and also that the superintendent be instructed to pay the men 51. per hour from his petty cash account. This reservoir is for a go6d supply of water in case of fire and also for bathing. The doctor report- ed that the children kept healthy. It was recommend- ed that twii trombones be bought at a clst of £6 9s. as an addition to the hand. Mr. W. Houghton moved and Mr. J. Houghton seconded the adoption of the recom- mendations.</p>
        <p>THE NECESSITY OF PERMISSIONS. The CLERK said that they would have to go to the Local Government Board for permission to alter the reservoir. Mr. HEATON : There is a great deal of trouble in applying to the Local Government Board. We shall have to ask permission to sow watercress soon.—The Clerk : But there are structural alterations.— Mr. Heaton : A short time ago at Paelgate, the commit- tee built a new cesspool without writing to the Local Government Board. It took a deal of time to get this permission.—The Clerk : This scheme will involve an outlay of £5O. It is my duty to advise. You must not follow me if you want your own way. The recommendations were ultimately passed. PRAISE FOR THE "WARRINGTON UNION. The CLERK announced that the auditor was pleased with the accounts of the union, and they also received praise elsewhere. The Local Government Board often referred people to them for information on different subjects. During the present 'week a letter had been received fnain Hull with reference to the Padgate schools as the Local Government Board had recommended them This was very complimentary to the union. TENDERS ACCEPTED. The following tenders for the ensuing year were accepted :—Flour, oatmeal, Messrs. Pierpoint and Co. ; beef and mutton, Mr. W. Gill ; stone, Warrington Slate Co. ; milk, Mr. J. W. Houghton; bread, Mrs. Eckersley, firewood, Mr. Wm. Heaton ; beer, Mrs. Brundreth ; coal and slack, Messrs. Evans and Co.; glazing, Mr. Walter Longshaw ; drapery, Messrs. Fletcher and Lee ; brushes Mr. E. Chesters ; leather, Haynes Leather Co. ; spirits, Mr. E. Greenall ; groceries, coffins, and potatoes, were referred to the House Committee. These tenders were dealt with by a committee of Messrs. Pilling, A. JOlley, Ratcliffe, and Clarke.</p>
        <p>It is said that on several of the grnt lines to the North third-class dining carriages will be introduced this summer.</p>
        <p>HAT% COMPOUND. a clemulce. t anPdk ne expectorant for Coughs and Colds, 90., 131 d. 0, all Chenlists.-4d..</p>
