        <p>IT would not be easy to exagger- The Local Geyer/in:lent ate the importance of the Local Bill. Government Bill which was laid before the House of Commons, on Tuesday even- ing, by Mr. FOWLER, the President of the Local government Bard. It will extend to the rural districts of England and Wales, a power of self- Prernment which they have never hitherto en- joyed, but of which, in many respects, they have ,Ste°ll greatly in need. The establishment of parish Councils was one of the leading items in the Programme which the Liberal party, when in °PPosition, formulated at Newcastle, and it is already admitted by those who are best qualified ,t°‘ ferns an opinion as to the requirements of rural rnunities, that Mr. FowLEß's measure will this pledge in a most satisfactory manner. 'llere are thirteen thousand rural parishes in and Wales, but of these nearly six thousand h4ve a population of less than three i andred, and it is not proposed that parishes with ess than that population should have a separate ,e°1111cil• These small parishes will be grouped 411 other parishes. The Parish Councils will supersede the vestries in the performance of all duties which all which are to; distinctly ecclesiastical. 111 vestries, as Mr. FOWLER said, are oeerspit survivals, having the form, but not thz e Power of local government." All their func- "ls with regard to the holding of parish pro- and the making of rates, through the over- seers and churehwardens, would be transferred to h- ° new councils. In addition to this, the Parish tb°llll°lls will have powers given to them which vestries did not possess. They will be em- Powered to provide and acquire buildings for pub- lic offices and meetings, and for other public pur- Poses and to acquire land for recreation grounds arid for public walks. They will be able to utilise any 811PPIY of water within the parish ; to acquire ae„ right of Way, of beasement or other right in or out the Parish, • the acquisition of which would be cial to the inhabitants of the parish ; to ildrea with any open ditch or the collection of any heallnag,e likely to be prejudicial to the public the bth„' and to hold any gift of property for ing Ce't of the parish. All powers for acquir- -and under the Allotments Act will be bttyem. They will be in a position either or hire land, compulsorily, if the 1'413:al landlords will not come to a voluntary agree- -41 1 nt. As to ways and means, the Councils will h ~aVe the right to borrow money within specified utaits and under certain restrictions. It may lOW be asked in. what manner of way are these P'l:arish Councils to be appointed or elected. 11 tIS satisfactory to know that their election is linbjected to no anti-democratic restrictions. erY Person—man or woman—who is entitled vote at either a Parliamentary or a County tonncil election will be entitled to vote for a parish e°T,nricillor, and there will be no plural voting. attile Council will consist of not fewer than five, not more than fifteen members, and it will :_ect its own chairman. It will be elected ,11ally within a month before April 15. thotf. PowLaa's scheme includes, in addition to tio oNation of Parish Councils, also the crea- , °f District Councils. The jurisdiction of the r :Nth Council is to extend over the parish; that /3,,!'11e District Council will extend over the e'en 44re, ,or,rather,the part of the Union which is not Ito read, provided with a Town Council, a Board of ttil)r°vernent Commissioners, or a Local Board. 00 Other words, the jurisdiction of the District will be co-extensive with that of the ex- qti g Rural Sanitary Authorities. Boards of riC,fclians will remain, but they will be popula- .4l4'4 All Property qualifications will be abolished, : btllere will be no plural voting and vet- -3' Proxy as at present. Voting papers will aPPear from the election of Guardians, for vot- ' o+s will be by ballot, and there will bo " one man ' th'ie v,Ote." Duly qualified women will not only be n 4014 t, "ue but also to sit as members. At pre- ' eeor Rural Sanitary Authority is practically a fo,ninr ittee of the Board of Guardians. In the b_6 eit will consist of all those members of the —oard ttieta, I Guardians who are elected for rural dis- til. T and it will be known as the District Coun- ts chairinan will, by virtue of his office, be a polvo' of the peace; and it will have additional how fra given to it, amongst others those which nhelisa,ll upon the Highway Board, which will be those 4ed. Boards of Commissioners, such as rkeh of Runcorn and Newton, and Local Boards, vertoals those of Lymm and klaydock, will be con- eis into urban District Councils. They will reed tie'ed by ballot on the modern franchise sum- their Pin the words," one man one vota," and frool°hairrnen will be entitled to dispense justice Tnetbrsolocal bench. and .15111, taking it all in all, is a great measure, beetiPregnant with results. Even the Tories have all syeeonatrained to speak respectfully of it, or, at With f 11,ts'it friaY be said that they have blessed it this ,!I"nt criticism. It is not at all likely that elit, Will, in its progression through P arlia- s/tteris. ent with much direct opposition. An 144 ,I°ll of local government is so ardently vie,s,eos.'t in rural districts that the Tories will, in fatqro a Possible general election in the near 1114 th be on their good behaviour in that respect. Illtioh ere are many methods of obstructing legis- tle-- Parish Councils will, to a great extent, ef to se the Squire and the Parson from the places Power th and privilege which they often hold riot Wjl e Present state of a things, and they will it all g Y submit to that deposition. It s not this nlikely therefore, that the introduction of 1100 of ' 15111 will lead to an increase of that obstr hest., rhanientary business which has already Of ried to a flagrant degree. But if anything deee;vl3ect!'ind should occur the country will not be stren and the hands of the Government will gthened in dealing in a firm and peremp-</p>
        <p>tory fashion with the devices of Mr. LOWTHER, and his alders and abettors.</p>
        <p>IF we may judge by the proceedings Early in the House of C.munons on Tues- Closing day evening, there is at least one form of " Local Option " which meets with universal acceptatk.e. Sir J. Luaaocx proposed a resolu- tion declaring "that it is desirable to give to local autimrities such powers as may be necessary Ito enable them to carry out the general wishes of the shopkeeping community, with reference to the hours of closing," and this resolution was passed unanimously. Sir JOHN LUBBOCK is a veteran in the struggle for early closing of shops. He has in former Parliaments introduced two Bills to bring about the end he has in view, but in neither case were they acceptable to a majority of the peoples' representatives. In one Bill he propozed the establishment of a compulsory half-holiday, and in the other he tried to effect the closing of all shops in the country at eight o'clock on five days of the week, and at ten on the other day. There was cer- tainly nothing very exorbitant in either of these demands, but the hard-and-fast nature of the regulations they proposed, which were to affect all parts cf the country alike, made them unaccep- table to the House of Commons. His present proposal is more flexible and, therefore, more practicable. It is in accordance, too, with the report of a Parliamentary Committee which has inquired into the subject. That Committee reported that the evidence of witnesses for the most part went to show that "any regulation of retail trade would be practicable only by local authority after being set in motion by the traders themselves." The debate which took place on Sir JOHN Lulu:too/Cs resolution WAS of An intoresting character. Mr. Aswan, the Home Secretary, made an able and sympathetic speech in support of the proposal, and one or more representatives of almost every section into which the House is divided endorsed the resolution. The passing of an r.hstract res elution does not, unfortunately, make a law, but it is, at all events, a pledge of future legislation.</p>
        <p>A Chicago engineer has expressed the opinion that the phenomenal development of his native city is birgely due to the successful application of mechanical refrigeration to its great industries. A paper that was read at a meeting of the Liver- pool Engineering Society the other evening, by Mr. M. C. BANNISTER, C.E., on "Cold Storage Works and other Installations," goes to show that there is nothing very improbable in this state- ment. The great slaughter-houses of Chicago could never have reached their present dimensions had it not been that the export of beef and mutton is aided by the process of refrigeration. Up to 1883 mechanical refrigeration was mainly used for the manufacture of ice and the cooling of water, but since then it has been applied to an enormous extent in the freezing and consequent preservation of perishable commodities. It is , largely due to this rapid development of a new process that at the present time nearly a fourth of the food supply of the people of this country is imported. One of the "notions " of the coming Chicago Exhibition will be the refrig- erating plant used for the purpose of cooling the lwater supplied to the Exhibition down to a temperature of thirty-eight degrees Fahrenheit. Mr. BANNISTER foretells the day when the refrigerating appliance will be found in most houses, just as we now find appliances for gas lighting. The re- frigerating medium will be"la,id on"just as water is, and the amount of " cold " which the householder uses for preserving his provisions will be recorded in a meter and paid for accordingly. In the dis- cussion which took place on this interesting paper, Mr. Boursors, the city engineer of Liverpool, in speaking of the value of a system of cold storage incidentally mentioned that if the English ports were blocked in time of war the country would be starved out in seven days, and that London would be in the throes of famine in three days.</p>
        <p>HENRY MILLING &amp; CO, LTD. ARE @PERRINO THR CHOICEST SEVILLE ORANGES, FOR WINE AND MARMALADE: AT MOST REASON- ABLE PRIORS. 5, HORSEMARRET-STREET. WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
