      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>ST. EDWARD'S CHURCH, RUNCORN. The Rev. Father Jerom, S.J., has conducted mission services in this church each evening during the present week, and his discussion has been listened to by large congregations. Go to PRITCHARD'S for the best five guinea watch a work- ingman can have.—Adut. CRUELTY TO A HORSE AT BOLD. On Thursday, at the Widnes Petty Sessions, Joseph Smith, a carter, employed by Mr. J. R. Whalley, farmer Bold, was summoned for working a horse when in an unfit state.—Mr. F. J. Poole defended.—A fine of 2s. 6d. and 12s. costs was inflicted. PRITCHARD'S have now stood the test for 50 years, andquality and first-class Workmanship are still guaranteed.—Advt. ST. JOHN'S BAND OF HOPE, RUNCORN. A public meeting was held in connection with this society, in St. John's Schoolroom, on Wednesday even- ing. The chair was occupied by Mr. Robert W. Flarrop, who made a few interesting remarks. A miscellaneous programme was gone through by the members. JOSEPH PRITCHARD keeps spectacles to suit all sights from a shilling a pair.—AdVt. ELLESHERH-STREET IMPROVEMENT CLASS. On Wednesday evening a very successful and interest- ing "At Home was given, under the auspices of the above class, in Ellesmere-st., Schoolroom. A large num- ber of persons partook of an excellent tea. During the evening an enjoyable programme was gone through. JOSEPH Parrcmins.Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, Hlg et.. and 4, Bridge-st.. Runcera.—Adrt. A CELEBRATED VIOLINIST. We are pleased to hear that we are soon to have in Runcorn an opportunity of hearing the famous Hungar- ian violinist, M. Tivadar Nachez. He has had the honour of playing before the Queen, and her Majesty recently showed her appreciation of his musical skill by present- ing him with a handsome breastpin, having the mono- gram V.R.I. set in jewels, Associated with M. Nachez will be several excellent amateur musicians.</p>
        <p>JOSEPH PRITCHARD'S sale shop is 51,Hign-st., nis workshop 4, Bridge-st.—Advt. MARINERS' GRIMM, RUNCORN. On Tuesday evening a very successful temperance meeting was held at the above church, when the Rev. W. J. Adams (Liverpool) presided. The Chairman opened the proceedings with a bright, earnest address. A prac- tical address was also delivered by the Rev. A. W. Riley. A duet was admirably rendered by Messrs. C. and A. Timmins, and a pianoforte solo by Mr. Isherwood was much appreciated.</p>
        <p>TOWNSIIIP MEETING AT HALTON. The annual meeting of ratepayers was held at Halton on Thursday night, when the Rev. James Hanby, Wes- leyan minister, was elected Guardian for the ensuing three years. Mr. John Baguley was appointed way- warden. So far as we are aware, Mr. Handley is the first Wesleyan minister electefl a Guardian, and had it not been for the reduction in the qualification brought about by Mr. Fowler, he would not have been eligible. Both PRITCFIARD'S Shops are under personal supervision.and austomers may rely upon recei ring every attention.—Advt.</p>
        <p>BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, RUNCORN. On Tuesday evening, under the presidency of Mrs. Walton, the ladies of the sewing meeting in connection with the above place of worship gave a most interesting entertainment. There was a large attendance the schoolroom being filled by an enthusiastic audience. The entertainment consisted of tableaux, songs, recita- tions, and pianoforte solos. The tableux, representing "Joan of Arc," " Lady Macbeth," " Nydia," " Good- morning," and many others were highly artistic concep- tions.</p>
        <p>When in want of a chaste and elegant wedding or birthday present go to PRITCHARD'S, 51, High-st.--Advt. SUNDAY SCHOOL CONFERENCE AT WIDNES. On Thursday afternoon a conference of Sunday school workers was held in Prince's-st. Chapel, in connection with the Primitive Methodist Sunday School Union. Rev. W. Thornley who presided, gave a short address, and Mr. Crampton (Warrington) read a. paper on " Some aspects of Sunday school work." The paper having been criticized by three or four of those present, an en- couraging and helpful address was delivered by the Rev. J. H. Hunt, Connexional Sunday sch.ol secretary. In the evening an excellent address was given by the same gentleman on " The Sunday school and its mission." Mr. J. Griffiths (Warrington) presided. If you want a lucky wedding ring go 10 51, Illas-sr.,where you will geta present with it.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>EX ,I(IYNER, SATURDAV ?IC ROit '25, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EX ,I(IYNER, SATURDAV ?IC ROit '25, 1893.</p>
        <p>A TOOTHSOME' THIEF. On Wednesday morning at the Town Hall, New- ton, before Mr. A. C. McCorquodale, a man named William Barton, a blacksmith of St. Helens was charged with having stolen a box of sweets from Earlestown Station, the property of the Londpn and North Westehi Railway Company, on Tuesday night. It appears the prisoner was in the waiting room, having booked for St. Helens, and near the booking office door were 9 boxes of sweets. The booking clerk, Mr. W. Burrows, had occasion to leave the booking office for a few minutes, and on his return, he noticed the prisoner had something bulky in his possession, and on counting the boxes, he saw that one was missing. The 11.50 p.m. train for Liverpool coming, prisoner entered one of the cotapartment!, and emptieethe sweets from the box into a pocket handkerchief, and on finding that the theft had been discovered, he threw the sweets out of the carriage window. The train was detained, and P.C. Crichton who was called in took the prisoner into custody. tie was remanded un- til Saturday. Liw MOWERS.—GARLAND, Ironmonger. Warrington and Eurlestown. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>FORTHCOMINO ELECTION OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>THE FORTHCOMINO ELECTION OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>We understand that Mr. Philip Speakman, C.C.. does not intend to seek re-election at the forthcoming election of Guardians for the Runcorn Union. Mr. Speakman has acted as a Guardian for the poor for many years,and his retirement will occasion much regret in many circles. The other retiring Guardians will be nominated, viz., the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. S. Taylor, T. Lea, and A. R.Norman, and in addition the list ef candidates will be increased by the names of Messrs. H. J. Grice, E. Imison, Thomas Handley, G. Kirkham, Benjamin Kniveton, and Wm. Handley-, There are seven Guar- dians required,and the term of office is now three years. So far the contest has excited little interest.</p>
        <p>JOSEPH PRITCHARD has the best stock of [Myer and W60.01u in the teem.—Advt.</p>
        <p>A WATerntiN togoWNEit, IN THE WEAVER. About eight o'clock on Saturday night,a flattnan,named Richard Sandbach, of Northwich, was drowned in the Weaver near Saltport. The deceased was a watchman on the dredger there, and he was ferried to that craft by Thos. Naylor's Perch Bridge. After Hough had rowed some distance from the dredger, he heard a splash in the water, and he shouted bnt.received no qnswer. He told the men at the lock, and they went to the place and brought up the body of the deceased by grapples in half an hour.—Mr. Worsley held an inquest on the body on Tuesday at the Weaver Hotel. It was stated that de- ceased was 44 years of age. He had had a " sup ' of drink, and it was thought that he, had fallen into the water when attempting to leave the dredger to go to the lock. A verdict of " Accidental death" was re- turned.</p>
        <p>JOSEPH PRlTcnean is prepared to wind and keep in repair, private clocks from 10s, per annum.—Advt. THE " INNOCENT " THEFT AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At Widnes Police Court on Monday, Jane Marsh, of Mersey View, Halebank, was charged with haying on Saturday night stolen four yards of lace from a stall in the market, the property of Alfred Walker, draper, Manchester, and also a pair of stockings, the property of Charles Broadbent, of Buttermarket-St., Warrington.— P.C. Almond who was on duty in the market, saw the prisoner take the lace and place it in her bass, and he apprehended her. At the Police Station her bass was found to contain a pair of stockings, which it was as- certained had been stolen from Broadbent's stall.—ln regard to the lace the prisoner said she had no intention of stealing the lace, it was done quite innocently. She denied that the stockings produced were those found in her bass,—She was fined ss. on each charge, or 14 days. _ _</p>
        <p>If you wantyour watch repaired by skilful workmen, take It to I'RITCIII.IIDP9.—Advt.</p>
        <p>MEETING OF TEACEERS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the members of the Runcorn and District Branch of the National Union of Teachers was held last Friday evening, in the Greenway-road Boys School. There was a fairly good attendance, and the president, Mr. Jordan occupied the chair. The secre- tary, Mr. Moulton, stated that the Committee had on the motion of Mr. Epps (Weston), seconded by Mr. Smith (Widnes), resolved that the Runcorn and District Association call the attention of Mr. J. T. Brunner, M.P., and Mr. J. S. Gilliat, M.P., to the importance of a motion to be introdueed in the House of Commons,by Sir Richard Temple, viz., " That in the opinion of this House it is desirable that a National State-aided system of superanuation for teachers in public elementary schools, in England and Wales, should be established at an early date." The Secretary said copies of the proposi- tion were forwarded to Mr. Brunner and Mr. Gilliat,who had sent favourable replies. On the motion of Mr. Jor- dan, seconded by Mr. Crosby, it was resolved to raise subscriptions in aid of the Teachers' Benevolent and [1 Orphanage Fund, befere the Easter Conference. The con- sideration of the question of joining the Cheshire Dis- trict Union was deterred to the next meeting. The elec- tion of the executive of the National Union of Teachers, consisting of 24 members, was next proceeded vvith,Miss Kilburn and Mr. Cookson acting as scrutineers. •</p>
        <p>- - JOSEPH PRITCHARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High. et.. and 4, Bridge st., Runcorn.—Advt. FUNERAL OF MR. J. J. WATERWORTH OF RUNCORN. The remains of the late Mr. J. J. Waterworth of the Egerton Arms, Bridge-street, whose death was recorded last Saturday, were interred at Runcorn Cemetery on Monday at ternoon. The funeral procession started from the hotel at half-past two and in front walked a large number of licensed victuallers, then came representives of the Star of Freedom Lodge of Cddlellows, followed by about 30 members of the Earl of Ellesmere Lodge of Freemasons. Next came the hearse, and coaches. In the first coach were Master Harry V. Waterworth, Mr. H. Coleburn, Mr. William Waterworth, Mr. Cockerlyne, and Mr. James Whinyates ; in the second, Messrs. John Maggs, John Dykes, Walton, and Woodhouse; and in the third Messrs. Nickson, Collinge, H. A. Hankinson and Garrett. The first part of the burial service was gone through at the Holy Trinity Church by the Rev. A. Wiley, the congregation singing the hymn When our heads are bowed in woe," and as the mourners left the edifice Mr. Savage, the organist played the Dead March ' in " Saul." The Rev. Mr. Riley also conducted the ser- vice at the graveside. The streets were lined with spectators and there was a great concourse of people at the cemetery, to witness the last sad rites, and the win- dows of the houses and shops en route were darkened as a token of respect. She grave was covered with floral emblems sent by the widow and children, Mr. and Mrs. Coleburn, Mr., Mrs., and Ethel Waterworth, Messrs. Flankinson and Son, Mr.and Mrs. Tom Price,Mr.and Mrs. John Price, Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Hough, Mr. and Mrs, Collinge. Mr. and Mrs. Ackerley, Mr. and Mrs. Wood- house, Mr. and Mrs. Nickson, Mr. and Mrs. Dykes, Jennie Hetty, and Polly Ann Jones, Alice Edwards, Clara Moores, Mrs. Dunn, Mr. J. France, Miss Stubbs, Miss Tyrie, Miss Massey, and one from a few friends in the trade with deepest regret. Mr. W. Nickson was the undertaker and Messrs. F. and W. Yarwood supplied the hearse and coaches. 2e. TO ss. IN THE 2, OFF, on all cash orders at R. W. MARsH'S, Furniture WArehouse, Bridge-Foot, Warrington. —See fuller advt., page 4. - Advt.</p>
        <p>LORD GERARD'S NEW ESTATE. Lord Gerard, of Garswood, who has just come in- to possession of Eastwell Park estate, Ashford,Kent, is spending about £BO,OOO in improving and enlarg- ing the beautiful mansion and grounds. At present about 300 men are employed in the work, and the scene in that part is an animated one. His Lord- ship quite recently met his new tenants and acted towards them in a most generous manner, making a reduction of from 20 to 33 per cent in the rents. ALL HOUSEHOLD LIN iLf, hemmed and marked, free of eharge.---7.and W. Durres, Sankey-st.—ildut. LANTERN ExilißiTioN. On Tuesday evening, a lantern exhibition was given in St. Peter's School. The Rev. B. Percival, vicar, occupied the chair. There was a good attend- ance. The slides were illustrative of "Views in Palestine," "Buy your own Cherries," "The Bottle," The Wreck of the Hesperus." The connective readings were read by Mr. Dring,. Earlestown, of the Manchester City Mission, Several humoreus slides were also shown. The lanternist was Mr. Lawson, of Earlestown, who used his new patent lime-light apparatus. A vote of thanks was passed on the proposition of Mr. Taylor (P.C.M.) seconded by the Rev. B. Percival. UMBRELLAS RECOVERED.-4. and W. DUTTON Sankey-it. Warrington.—Artvt. ALLEGED THEFT OF A BICYCLE AT CROFT. At Warrington Occasional Court, on Monday, two youths, Lawrence Curley and George Brookes, were each charged with stealing a bicycle the property of Frederick Pennington, at Croft on Sunday the 19th inst. Complainant said that he went into the Horse Shoe Inn, Croft, leaving his bicycle outside. Soon after the landlord told him his bicycle had gone. Witness went out, and went with P.C. Gallagher down Smithy Lane and found the machine lying on the ground. Lane, got on the machine and caught defendants a short distance further on, and P.C. Gallagher on coming up ar- rested them. P.C. (lallagher corroborated com- plainant's statement, and said that when charged with the theft, Brookes admitted taking the bicycle, but Curley denied doing so. Defendants were committed to the Quarter Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>HARRIERS--EARLESTOWN V. ORFORD.</head>
        <p>HARRIERS--EARLESTOWN V. ORFORD.</p>
        <p>Much interest was displayed on Saturday over the meet between the Earlestown and Orford harriers. About a month ago, the clubs met at Warrington, and had a run round by VVinwick, which ended in an easy win for Orford by 36 points At that time the Earlestown club had only been in existence about three weeks, and the performance on that occasion was not considered discouraging. On the 4th inst. the Leigh harriers visited Earles- town, but they only succeeded in beating the home club by 9 points. Thus when the return visit of the Orford team came round, the interest, as to the result was increased. It appears that the Orford team have not been beaten this season, but they came perilously near being vanquished on Saturday, and only succeeded in making a draw of 40 points each, by Burke struggling into the 13th position in the race home. The run was rather lengthy but the ground was in good going condition, the scores at the finish beirur as under:—</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN. Eastwood 1 Williams Price</p>
        <p>ORYORD-.</p>
        <p>Baker .. 4 My 6 Grounds 7 Waywell Woods l2 Boardman Burke l3 Rylance</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>Total 4O</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN WESLEYAN CIRCUIT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN WESLEYAN CIRCUIT.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Runcorn circuit I was held in the vestry, Brunswick School, on Mon- day, under the presidency of the Rev. T. Wilkes, superintendent minister. There was a large at- tendance of representatives from the various societies in the circuit. The stewards presented their financial statements, which showed that the expenditure had been somewhat heavy, and a little in excess of the income. The steward, however, had a small balance in hand. This was considered very satis- factory. The Chairman read a report showing a slight increase in the membership of the circuit and also in the junior society classes. Then ensued an animated dis- cussion, introduced by Mr. Kniveton, or the present state of the town, with regard to the opening of shops on Sundays, gambling, and bad language in the streets. all of which were strongly condemned. Ultimately a 1 committee was formed to see what steps they as a church should take to remedy matters in this respect. On the proposal of the circuit stewards, thanks were given to the ministers for their services during the past twelve months, and they were invited to remain for a third year. They accepted the invitation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>PUBLIC COMPANIES AND THE POOR-RATE INTERESTING POINT AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC COMPANIES AND THE POOR-RATE INTERESTING POINT AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, at Widnes Petty Sessions, before Messrs. J. G. Gamble and J. Lister, John W. David- son, accountant, 48, Castle-street, Liverpool, liquid- ator, of the Lancashire Alkali Company, Widnes, was sued by Mr. John Goff, assistant overseer, for .£52 165., poor-rate due. Dr. Sparrow, barrister, (instructed by Messrs. H. W. Collins, Robinson, and. Co., solicitor, Liverpool), appeared for the defendant. Mr. Goff proved the amount from his books, and stated that he had made applcation for the money in the customary way. Replying to Dr. Sparrow, he said he had sent the application by post to Castle-street, Liverpool. Witness believed that business had been carried on at the defend- ant company's premises during the year.—Dr. Sparrow read the 163rd section of the Public Companies Act, which declared that any distress warrant issued by the magistrates against a company was null and void to all intents and purposes, and he (Dr. Sparrow) would now give Mr. Goff warning that they (the defendants) in- tended to enforce their rights if the overseers obtained a distress warrant, and attempted to put it into force. Mr. Lewin (deputy magistrates' clerk) : You apply for a distress warrapt, Mr. Goff ?—Mr. Goff : Yes.—The Chairman: The warrant is granted.—The Clerk : You take the onus on your own shoulders, Mr. Goff ?—Mr. Goff : Oh, Yes.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR. J. W. LIGHT- BURN OF RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A select company of gentlemen gathered in the large room of the Devonshire Hotel, on Wednesday evening, to do honour to our townsman Mr. J. Lightburn, by the presentation to him of some token of their esteem and regard. Proceedings commenced with a most excellent dinner provided by the host., Mr. George Shone. Afterwards Mr. James Collier took the chair. Amongst those pre- sent, in addition to the guest of the evening were: Messrs. F'. Jolley, F. Lightburn, A. Linaker, Dr. MacKenzie, W. Carroll, W. Farrell, sen., W. Farrell, jun., Stoll and Branch. The Chairman in presenting Mr. Lightburn with a pair of handsome and valuable silver trays, said he felt honoured by having been requested by the subscribers to perform that duty. He had known Mr. Lightburn for upwards of twenty-years, and during that period always found him straightforward and honourable in everything he undertook. Socially he was generous, warmhearted, and a true friend, and it was his hope, and the hope of the subscribers that he might live for many years to enjoy to a happy old age, the success which had attended his labours.—ln acknow- ledging the gift, Mr. Lightburn said the expressions used by Mr. Collier and further embodied in that handsome present, overwhelmed him with a sense of indebtedness. To enjoy the love and respect of his fellow-men, was one of the greatest pleasures that he knew of.—An appropri- ate list was gone through, including the health of Mr. Lightburn and his family, and the host Mr. Shone and Miss Turner, and Miss Fardoe, who had prepared and served the dinner in such admirable style.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>LECTURE ON MR. GLADSTONE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>LECTURE ON MR. GLADSTONE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Union Hall, Runcorn, was crowded on Wednes- day night by an audience gathered to listen to the Rev. James Hanby deliver a lecture entitled " Gladstone, patriot, orator, statesman. The chair was occupied by Mr. R. Posnett,who in introducing the lecturer said he was sure all members of that association were grateful of the untiring efforts that the present Liberal Government had made on behalf of the men of the country, notwithstanding that they had been taced by an obstruction almost ' unprecedented, especially by the Front Opposition bench. The: Lecturer, in the course of an eloquent speech, stated that Lord Rosebery said that Mr. Gladstone's life would have to be undertaken by an limited liability company, and therefore he could hardly be expected to do justice to such a life in the course of one hour. Years ago their knowledge of a man was supposed to be in- complete unless they had some information concerning his ancestors, this idea being based on the fact that cer- tain qualities of head and heart could be transmitted from parent to child. Mr. Gladstone was of Itlyal line- age, the blood of Henry 111. of England and of Bruce of Scotland flowing in his veins; and in spite of this it had always been Mr. Gladstone's boast that he sprang from the people ; and it was a source of extreme gratification to his followers that the greatest commoner of the time refused to accept an empty title and pass into the oblivion of the House of Peers. After reviewing the whole of Mr. Gladstone's political life and describing the marvellous prosperity of the country during his Chancellorship of the Exchequer, the lecturer concluded :—As I see him, the most heroic figure of our age, the St. Patrick of the 19th century, lam reminded that when St. Patrick had done his work there were no snakes left in Ireland, and as I look to- day and see the hideous brood of vipers in that fair island, which has been hatched into vitality by a cen- tury of British misrule and oppression, I am satisfied that deliverance dra weth nigh. The ttru,,gle in West- minster will be keen, but the result is assured : and when the Grand Old Man submits his Bill to the Queen for signature, tht, brightest day for Ireland will dawn, and for England too, for in the crown of England's Queen shall be set firm an unfractured emerald, and the monarch shall set the fashion lathe wearing of the green. (Applause.) Mr. D. Bishrown proposed a vote of thanks to the Rev J. Hanby for his interesting lecture, which was seconded by Mr. Temperley, supported by Mr. Kniveton, and car- ried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>MR. J. T. BItUNNEIt, M.P., ON THE</head>
        <p>MR. J. T. BItUNNEIt, M.P., ON THE</p>
        <p>DIRECT VETO BILL.</p>
        <p>Replying to a letter conveying a resolution of the Wesleyan Temperance Society of toe Runcorn circuit, Mr. Brunner's secretary writes as follows : Dear Sir,l am desired by Mr. Brunner to acknow- ledge with thanks the receipt of your letter of yesterday, containing the resolution passed by tbe Runcorn circuit of the Wesleyan Temperance Society. Mr. Brunner, as doubtless you are aware, as in thorough accord with the views expressed in your resolution on this subject ; though he regrets the Government Bill does not contain some prevision for a vote for reducing the number of public-houses as well as for closing them entirely.— Yours faithfully,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>" COLIN H. BROWNINIII. ‘• 9, Ennismore Gardens, S.W., March 16th, 1893."</head>
        <p>" COLIN H. BROWNINIII. ‘• 9, Ennismore Gardens, S.W., March 16th, 1893."</p>
        <p>WESTON TOWNSHIP MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the ratepayers of Weston was held in the National School on Thursday night. There was a good attendance. Mr. E. Aaron was voted to the chair. It was resolved on the motion of Mr. James Ford, jun., seconded by Mr. G. H. Wale, that the names of the following persons be submitted to the magistrates as fit and proper persons to fill the position of overseer : Messrs. E. Aaron, Thomas Ford senior, William Griffith, and James Ford. The Chairman announced that he wished to retire from the position of way- warden as he was advancing in years.—Several ratepayers said Mr. Aaron had paid the greatest at- tention to the affairs of the township, and he was pressed to again accept office,and eventually he was unanimously elected on the motion of Mr. Thomas Ford, secouded by Ur, Thomas Griffiths.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>A special m.eting of this Board was held on Wednesday, under the presidency of Mr. 1.1. J.Grice. Present—Messrs. Humphreys ii ankinson, Bisbrown, Fisher, Marshall (sec.),Lake oaw clerk), mid Barker (surveyor).—The Surveyor said that there was nothing taking place in the construction of the Ship Canal at the Old Quay, which would prevent the company carrying out their agreement to complete the graving dock there within twelve months of the completion of the canal.— Mr. Fisher said it would also be welcome news that piles were now being driven for the purpose of constructing the lay-by. (Hear, hear.)—Mr. Bisbrown said ship- building had formerly been the staple trade of the town, and it was now languishing if not altogether dead, and it was of the utmost importance to the industrial inte- rests of the town that the graving dock should be made. —After a few remarks from the Chairman the subject dropped, and the Board went into committee on the bye-laws.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>AN ADVERTISING FRAUD ON A RITNCORNIAN.</head>
        <p>AN ADVERTISING FRAUD ON A RITNCORNIAN.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes on Wednesday, before Mr. Justice Wills, David James Morgan, 40, des- cribed as a paper bag manufacturer, was indicted for haying obtained, by false and fraudulent means, the sum of ZI from Samuel Webster, of Runcorn, at Liverpool, on the 23rd February ; £2 from John Daniel Heron on the 18th February ; and 103. from Win. Turner on the 24th February. He was further charged with having attempted to obtain by false pretences the sum of £lO from John Dixon, at Liverpool, on 24th February. Mr. Collin.wood Elope prosecuted, and said that on the 13th February the prisoner engaged a young man as clerk at RI a week, having previously leased some premises in Islington. He instructed the clerk to insert an advertisement in a Liverpool paper for a traveller for a firm of wholesale paper merchants and paper bag manufacturers, liberal terms and commission being offered. There were 98 applications, and letters were written by the clerk to some of the persons apply- ing, at the prisoner's dictation, on note-paper headed " A. B. Walker and Co., paper merchants and paper bag manufacturers." The prosecutor Webster was told : by the prisoner that he would be required to deposit CIO as a retaining fee. Webster gave him LI, and promised to deposit the remainder subsequently. The alleged false pretence was that the prisoner described himself as A. B. Walker and Co., whereas he was not A. B. Walker' and on being asked if there was any security, pointed his hand to four horses in a stable beneath. It was further alleged that the prisoner was not a paper bag manufacturer, that he was not engaging a traveller because no business existed, and that the four horses did not belong to him, but to a hobby-horse proprietor, who had the stabling beneath. %%filen the premises were searched there was absolutely no business being carried on there, and all the property found consisted of a few bill-heads with the name A. B. Walker and Co. upon them, a bottle of ink, and a few pens. The jury found the prisoner guilty and he was sentenced to twelve months hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>MR. J. S. GILLIAT, M.P., ON THE EDUCATION QUESTION.</head>
        <p>MR. J. S. GILLIAT, M.P., ON THE EDUCATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Widnes School Board,on Tues- day, the following correspondence was read from Mr. J. S. Gilliat, M.P.,respecting a circular from the Education Department, alluding to certain defects in the elementary schools:—"Dear sirs,—After con- siderable pressure upon the department, and per- sonal conference with Mr. Acland in the House of Commons, all I have received is a civil letter fr-m Mr. Kekewich, copy of which has been fOrwarded." The following is the letter alluded to :—" Education Department, 7th March. Sir,—l am directed to state that the object of the inquiries which are being made is clearly indicated in the circular. The schedule is now being tilled up for all schools which have been visited by H.M. Inspectors. If the managers of any school in- quire whether any specified defects of their schools will beat once revired to be remedied, my lords will answer such inquiry to the best of their powers.—Yourg, G. W. Kaitawics."</p>
        <p>"it amounts to nothing. This wretched circular can- not rest where it is. I and my friends regard it as a very mean side-blow at the voluntary schools. I have always considered that the voluntary schools ought to work in perfect harmony with the well-ordered and managed Board schools, and the attempt to destroy the former is to cast a heavy additional burden upon tax- payers. I have arranged with Sir Richard Temple to support him in the attack which he will make upon this emanation from the department, for I am sure that we shall have the support of Sir William Hart-leyke, the late vice-president of the Council.—Yours faithfully, JOHN S. GILLIAT." The Clerk also read the following letter relative to the above from Mr. Gilliat "1, Prince's-gate, February 25, 1593. Dear Sir,— The resolution of Sir R. Temple in favour of a State aided system of superannuation for teachers in elemen- tary scbools has now committed the Government to take it up. It is right to say in my opinion that both Sir W.Harcourt and Mr.Acland took a very sympathetic and fair view of the subject, and though the Chancellor dilates as it was his duty to do, upon the cost to the country, there was but one opinion as to the obligation to provide fer one of the most meritorious of all classes of public servants."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RUNCORN MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>PERFORMANCE OF "St. PAUL."</p>
        <p>One of the largest. audiences that has patronised the concerts of the Runcorn Musical Society this season, assembled in the Public Hall, on Thursday night, to witness the performance 3lendelssohn'e "St. Paul." Every part of the large building was I comfortably filled, it not crowded, and there was an interest and an appreciation manifested that must have been highly gratifying to all concerned. It ' was the first performance of this work in the town, and though it is usually placed second in pcint of merit to the great musicians composition "Elijah," all those who had the pleasure of listening to the tuneful solos and soul-stirring choruses went away with a desire to hear it again. The chorus about 70 strong, under the guid- ance of Mr. Frank H. Crossley, the able conductor, proved fully equal to the heavy demands made upon them, and it would be no exaggeration to say that as a whole they have never excelled the performance of Thursday night. The parts were tolerably well balanced and they sang with an accuracy tunefulness and sus- tained power deserving of the highest praise. The chorus was aided by an augmented orchestra and the accompaniments of the band was a mast pleasurable portion of the concert. Great interest was manifested in the first appearance of Miss Clara Taylor, daughter of our esteemed townsman Mr. S. Taylor, a local profes- sional, who has achieved distinction at the Royal Aca- demy of Music. She had a most enthusiastic reception. The beautiful airoso "But the Lord is mindful of his own" was given in a rich contralto voice and with a depth of feeling that came upon her auditors with de- lightful surprise, and she acknowledged with a bow the enthusiastic encore that followed. The soprano was Miss Margaret Hoare, of London, who gave an admir- able exposition of an exacting and heavy part, and she was several times encored. Mr. Kendal Thompson, of Manchester (tenor) proved somewhat disappointing. His nasal expression was especially objectionable, and he failed to secure the sympathy of his audience. Mr. Robert Newman, of London, is the possessor of a bass voice of good quality, and to an artistic method of treatment., he adds a dramatic force that is very effec- tive, and he scored a distinct success. The concert was a most enjoyable one and brought to a conclusion a season that will always be looked back upon with feel- ings of pleasure. It has shown that the foundation of the Society are fixed deeply in the appreciation of the people, and the members may rest assured that their efforts to present new works to the public will never fail to receive the acknowledgement of a crowded auditory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>WIDNES DIVISION UNIONISTS AND</head>
        <p>WIDNES DIVISION UNIONISTS AND</p>
        <p>HOME RULE.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a meeting of Conservatives and Liberel Unionists resident in the Widnes Di vision,was held at the Reading Room, Garston, to protest against the Home Rule Bill. There was a full audience. Mr. A. H. Holme, J.P., presided, and was supported on the platform by the Rev. H. Sandford, Killylea, county Armagh ; Councillor Austin Taylor % Messrs. W. Oulton, J.P. ; Holbrook Gaskell, J.P. ; H. H. Flornby ; G. W. Moss, J.P. ; J. E. Shep- herd, J.P. • A. E. Jacob, C. E. Maples, Alexander Garnett, and Major Larmor.—The Chairman, in opening the meeting, said there had been a great deal of talk about Ulster, and the question had been asked, " Will Ulster fight ?" He did not kncw whether Ulster was going to fight or not. There were many instances in history where people had fought for their liberties, and it was quite likely some people in the north of Ireland were made of the same "kidney," and if they made up their minds to fight they would not be allowed to fight alone. (Applause.) But there would be no question of fighting at all if the men of the Widnes Division, of Lancashire, and of England would do their duty and declare they would not have this Bill.—The Rev. H. Sandford spoke upon the Bill, and said if they imposed this degrading yoke upon Ulster they would find Ulster would rise up in two years and shake it from her neck. (Applause.)—Mr. A, E. Jacob moved 3 resolution expressing the meeting's determination to strenuously oppose the proposals of the Home Rule Bill.—Mr. William Oulton seconded the motion.—The resolution was supported by Mr. Holbrook Gaskell, who said he had almost worshipped Mr. Gladstone from his younger days, and felt it hard that in his last days, and Mr. Gladstone's last days he was compelled to opp- ose him to the utmost.—This remark was delivered with much pathos, and evoked continued cheering.—The resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday. Mr. Mclntyre presided, and there were present, Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Rev. J. W. N. Tanner, Messrs. S, Taylor, T. Lea, A. R. Nor- man, E. Aaron. A. Jackson, H. E. Heywood, J. Baguley, T. Lewis, J. Brandreth, James Whitlow, E. Bretherton, J. Weir, Joseph Knowles, W. Whitlow, John Ashton (clerk), and G. Ashton (deputy clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>REPRESENTATION. OF RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE REPRESENTATION. OF RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board had forwarded copies of the order which they had issued assigning two addi- tional guardians for the township of Runcorn, and uniting for the purpose of the election of a guardian in each case the townships of Aston by Sutton and Aston Grange, and the townships of Whitley Superior and Seven Oaks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>THB MICE AND VAGRANTS.</head>
        <p>THB MICE AND VAGRANTS.</p>
        <p>A communication from the Congleton Union respect- ing the increase in vagrants was read. It was stated that up to February 1892, the county police in the union acted as assistant relieving officers for the purpose of granting admission tickets to vagrant wards, but since that time the Chief Constable had refused to allow his officers to be appointed, and the Guardians had had to make other arrangements. The Guardians found that since the withdrawal of the police (but whether entirely from that cause they could not say) the number of vagrants had increased by rapid strides from 66 weekly to 130 weekly. The Guardians were of opinion that the employment of some member of the police force as an assistant relieving officer had been proved by experience to be one of the most expeditious courses to adopt in order to keep down the number of professional tramps who as a rule, are unwilling to confront such an officer, while to the honest, but destitute wayfarer, his inquiries occasion no alarm. The letter concluded by asking the Runcorn Guardians to petition the County Council to allow police officers to undertake such duties. Mr. Brandreth said at one time they were paying the police at Runcorn £8 for the week, and they only distri- buted twopence or fourpence a week. In addition the Board thought that it was not very nice for the poor people to have to go to a policeman to ask for a ticket ; and for these reasons the Board took the duty from the police.—Mr. Heywood said that at the present time they were wanting more vagrant wards, and he did not know whether there was a connection between the two things. He moved that they support the petition because they would have tha option of appointing policemen if they desired. He pointed out that a tramp might have £5 in his possession, but as the relieving officer could not search him, he was compelled to give him a ticket. The police had the power of search.—Mr. Aaron seconded the motion which was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>DIAINTENANCE OF AGED PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>THE DIAINTENANCE OF AGED PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>Wigan Union forwarded the &amp;Bowing resolution That the Local arvernment Board be respectfully urged to bring in a hill to enable aged, infirm, or sick parents who are destitute, to apply to the justices acting for the district in which they reside, for summonses against such of their ,children as are able, but refuse, without just cause to render necessary assistance ; and of conferring upon such justices, the powers of making and enforcing orders for payments direct to the parents.—The resolu- tion was adopted on the motion of Dr. Gastaldi, second- ed by Mr. Lea. MR. BRUNNER AND TEIE WEAVER TRUST. The foll)wing communication was received by the Clerk.—" 9 Ennismore Gardens, S.W., March 17th, 1893. Dear Sir,—l am instructed by Mr. Brunner to send you a draft of a suggested petition against the Weaver Navigation Bill, together with the Board of Trade report on the same. This report sufficiently ex- plains the nature of this trust and is, Mr. Brunner ven- tures to think worthy of your careful p.7,rusal. Mr. Brunner has in preparation, the draft of a Bill for amending the constitution of the Weaver Trust, with a I view to making it representative of the interests involved ' and proposes that your Board should, amongst others, elect its quota of th,. trustees.—Yours faithfully, Colin H. Browning, the Clerk of the Runcorn Guardians." The petition points out that the trustees are a self- elected body, with large statutory powers of levying tells on all goods carried on the River Weaver, which runs through the salt district of Cheshire into the River Mersey, but they do not in any way represent the in- terests of the trade which uses the navigation. ['his trade arises almost• entirely from the Salt Works of Cheshire, the salt being brought down the Weaver for exportation from the port of Liverpool, and the aggre- gate amount of tolls received by the Trustees for this ten years, 1880 to 1889, inclusive was £638,673. For many years past.a large surplus from the tolls has been under the old Weaver Navigation Acts, handed over to the county of Chester, in relief of the county rate, while there has also been a considerable expenditure by the Trustees, in building and endowing churches. The aggregate amount paid to the county of Chester for the ten years, 1880, to 1889, inclusive was £115,000. In the report of the Board of Trade on a Weaver Navi- gation Bill in the year 1857, the following passage oc- curs :—" In short the owners of property in Cheshire levy on the trade in salt a tax of £42,000 a year, and of this tax they apply about £21,000 only to the purposes of the trade and use the remainder to defray their own local burdens. It is further worthy of notice that the expenses of management which are included in the amounts above stated to have been expended on the Navigation have gradually increased since 1823, from £5,770 to £19,580." The report goes on to observe that the producer of salt, the merchant and shipowner, and the consumer must all be prejudiced by a tax of this description, and that such a state of things ought not to be allowed to continue. In the year 1872, the Board of Trade again reported to much the same effect on another Bill, promoted by the Trustees, and your petitioners crave leave to refer to both of the said reports.</p>
        <p>Your petitioners allege that the constitution of the Trustees is anotnalous and the working of the Trust thoroughly unsatisfactory and they expressly complain of the high tolls levied on .goods and the unfair exemp- tions therefrom and of the application of the surplus for the benefit of the county of Chester. _</p>
        <p>During the last three years the export of salt from Liverpool has considerably decreased and your petition- ers who are deeply interested in the trade and prosperity of the Port of Liverpool are of opinion that it the man- agement of the Weaver Navigation is allowed to remain as at present their interests will be seriously affected. Your petitioners humbly pray that in accordance with the recommendation of the Board of Trade made in 1857, over thirty years ago, emphasised again in the report of 1872. Your Lordships will be pleased to insert in the Bill now pending in Your Lordships Right Hon- ourable House,Clauses.—(i) Placing thenavigation in the hands of a body fairly representative of the trade and navigation interests. (ii) Revising the tolls so as to make them fair and equal and abolishing exemptions therefrom. OH) Providing that any surplus shall be applied to navigation purposes and in reduction of tolls. (iv) Providing for a proper audit and publication of accounts. If such clauses cannot for technical reasons be inserted in the present Bill your petitioners weuld respectfully suggest that at least, the suggestion made in the last -clause of the Board of Trade report of 1857 should be adopted and a clause be inserted in the present Bill requiring the Trustees to prornotein the next session of Parliament such a Bill as may enable Parliament to deal with the whole question in the name above sug-</p>
        <p>gested. Before thet petition tehetChelerskuirnplaunsstwoergototoa i d question sai - • mr Bruwniaies read, the the salt trade instead of the county. Mr. Aaron : He wants to take money out of this district into Northwich and Winsford.—Mr. Taylor said he did not think that was the case. He believed the petition was an attem.t to place the Trust under representative control of the county and not of the s ilt district. He thought it was a good idea .—The Clerk then read the petition.—Mr. W. Whitlow said the chief objet appeared to he to get better representation.—The Clerk: That is not altogether the object in view. The object was no doubt to take toll off the salt trade.—Mr. Heywood said there wire two reasons why they should not support that petition. First, because he did hot think that the shipments of salt from the Mersey, as they were left to infer from that petition, had been decreased through the Weaver tolls. In his opinion this diminution of exports of salt</p>
        <p>was caused through the operation of the Salt Union,who immediately after formation made a rise in prices. Secondly he objected to the abolition of the present exemption. One of these exemptions was the convey- ance of manure, frona all parte, to farmers occupying land adjoining the Weaver, free from toll. This exemp- tion was all in the interest of the occupiers and he did not see why they should go in for the abolition of these exemptions.—Mr. Brand reth said it was a question of the Salt Union and Brunner, Mond and Co. who wished to reduce their dues on the Weaver.—Mr. Lewis: Better let it lie on the table.—Mr. Heywoo3 said Mr. Brunner had been very good in reply to the Board when they had communicated with him and in justice to him and to themselves they ought to fully consider the matter. He suggested that the Clerk should read through the Weaver Navigation Acts, and advise the Board on the petition at their next meeting.—This was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>TIER CONTRACTS.</head>
        <p>TIER CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. T. Lea the following contracts were accepted :—Beef, mutton, Ex., Mr. Gill, Warring- ton ; butter, eggs, and milk, Mr. Basnett ; coffins, Mr. Lightfoot ; coals, Mr. Richardson ; carting, Mr. Jackson; drapery, Mr. Wright, Halton ; clothing, Messrs. Unsworth, Runcorn; boots and shoes, Mr. Sharrock, Runcorn ; repairieg shoes, Mr. Frankland ; disinfectants, Sanatos Company; groceries, Gregory, Runcorn; drugs, &amp;c., Executors of Mr. I. Speakman, Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>– WIDNES SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>- WIDNES SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this body was held in the Town Hall on Tuesday afternoon. There were then present : —Mr. H. Wade Deacon, J.P., C.C. (in the chair), Rev. J. H. Garrett, Dr. Olieeffe, Messrs. D. Lewis, C. E. Barlow, S. Quinn, C. Flynn. and G. H. Denby (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>DISTRICT EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this committee contained the following report of school cases summoned before the Committee: —Applications for free places without payment of fees, 16 ; for contravention of Education Act 277. Free places granted without payment of fees in 16 cases, refused none ; number of prosecutions before the magis- trates 33 ; number of convictions 31; withdrawn 2. It was recommended "that on the completion of five years service, the salary of attendance officer R. Grant, be increased according to the scale of the Board ; the increase to commence April Ist, 1893." Also " that the tender of Mr. F. H. Pankhurst, of Widnes, for the supply of the Attendance Officer's Uniforms, be accepted at the ,following prices :—Overcoat. £1 17s. 6d. 'Mackintosh, £1 ss. ;tunic, £1 14s. ; trousers, 15s. ; leggings, ss. 6d. cap, Bs. 6d. The tender to be in force until March 31st, 1896." There was also a recommendation that the seal of the Board be affixed to a petition asking for certain amendments to Sir R. Temple's Bill for "The Em- ployment of children of School Age." The Bill provides for the employing of children upon their attainments and not their attendance at school. The Board is of the opinion "that the proposal to allow children of school age to be employed without any guarantee as to their possessing an elementary education, is contrary to the spirit of the Elementary Educatisti Act, 1870, which was intended to secure that every child of school age shall receive such an education, and not simply attend school a certain number of times. The Board, therefore, prays that Sir R. Temple's Bill may be amended in accordance with the statements above men- tioned."—ln moving the adoption of the minutes the Rev. J. H. Garrett mentioned that the Committee felt strongly against the clause in Sir R. Temple's measure for allowing children to leave school without proper ex- amination, and merely upon their attainments.—The Chairman agreed with the Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>RATE OF WAGE QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE RATE OF WAGE QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Dr. O'Keeffe moved the adoption of the minutes of the Finance Committee's minutes which contained orders for the payment of the following Committee's expenses: —District Education Committee, £26 15s. 6d. ; School Management Committee, £561 123. 3d., and the Finance Committee, £49 13s. 4d. The following school fees were taken for February :—Simm's Cross School, £ll 45.; Warrington-road School, £5 7s. lid.; and West Bank School, £7 16s. 3d. There was a recommendation that the tender of Mr. Jones, of Castle-street, Liverpool, for printing be accepted. Dr. O'Keeffe mentioned that this was the lowest tender.—The Chair- man : I should like to ask whether the Finance Commit- tee intend to do as the Building and Sites Committee in letting tenders—stipulate that the contractors pay the current rate of wages in their trade ?—Dr. O'Keeffe : We did not think it necessary to insert such a stipulation in our contracts for the requirements. We trusted suffi- ciently in the honour of the contractors to pay and treat their employes fairly without our intederence.—The Chairman : Well, I don't propose to do any- thing in the matter now, but I most certainly shall give formal notice of motion that You do insert such a stipulation.—Several members here ex- pressed their belief that a standing order had been passed, affecting all Committees alike, but the Clerk explained that it was only an order affecting the Sites and Buildings Committee. It was unanimously agreed that the Finance Committee be requested to insert the stipulation in all their contracts, and the minutes were I then adopted.</p>
        <p>THE TIAIMIS FUND:</p>
        <p>Upon a motion made by the Chairman it was agreed that the Finance Committee be appointed to administer the grants from the Timmis fund each month, upon the receipt of the same from the Parr's Banking Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF MR. CHARLES TALBOT OF ASTON.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MR. CHARLES TALBOT OF ASTON.</p>
        <p>The village of Aston near Runcorn was en fete on Wednesday, on the occasion of the marriage of Mr. Charles Aston Talbot, youngest son of the late Captain Talbot, of Aston Hall, to Miss Elizabeth Mary Best, youngest daughter of the late Mr. Thomas Best, of Ellville House, Didebury, near Manchester. The bride had for some time resided with her brother, Mr. James C. Best, barrister, at Aston Lodge, and the bridegroom belongs to one of the oldest county families, and in honour of toe event the children in Aston and Dutton were given a holiday from school, and work on the estate was also at a stand- still. Triumphal arches of evergreens had been erected along the main road, and flags were flying, while the quaint edifice of Aston Church,was also beautifully decor- ated, and at twelve o'clock when the ceremony took place it was crowded with an interested audience. The bride entered leaning on the arm of her brother, Mr. J. C. Best. She was attired in a fawn and blue striped material, with pale blue mirroir velvet trimmings, and a dark picture hat. She carried a beautiful bouquet of white orchids, and wore a diamond brooch, with Eat, initials " E.M.T.," the gift of the bridegroom. She was accompanied by Miss Maud Wilson, of East Sheen, near London, who was attired in keeping with the bride, carried a bouquet of daffodils, mad wore a gold chain bangle, presented to her by the bridegroom. Mr. Wm. S. Power, of Lichfield, acted as best man. The other guests included Mr. and Mrs. Harvey Talbot, their two daughters and son, Miss Best, Mr. and Mrs. Wilson,Miss Noel Wilson, and Mr. Charles Linaker, (agent to the Aston estate). The ceremoney was performed by the Rev. E. Marsden, rector of Aston, assisted by the Rev. H. B. Blogg, vicar of Frodsham. The choir under the conductorship of Mr. Hutton, organist, and the congre- gation heartily joined in singing the hymn commencing " The vsice that breathed o'er Eden." As the party left the church, to the strains of Mendelssohn's " Wed- ding March,"they were the recipients of felicitous wishes. They drove to Aston Lodge, were the wedding party were entertained. The newly-married pair left at 1-30 ' amid showers of rice and slippers for Warrington, en routs for Scotland,where they will spend the honeymoon. In chi, meantime the villagers were supplied with refreshments at Aston School, the catering for which was ably carried out by Mr. Youd, of the Bear's Paw Hotel, Frodshani. In the evening the workmen and their wives on the Talbot estate were provided with a substantial tea. The presents were numerous and costly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>STABBING AFFRAY BETWEEN</head>
        <p>THE STABBING AFFRAY BETWEEN</p>
        <p>RIINCO iZN BOYS.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning, at the Runcorn Police Court, before Mr. J. Brundrit, a lad named Thomas Fisher, aged 13, of 2, Queen-st., Runcorn, was brought up on remand charged with unlawfully wounding another boy named Arthur Royle, aged 10 years, residing at 27, Blantyre-st., by stabbing him on the 10th of January last. Mr. J. W. Knowles solicitor, Widnes, defended. The prosecutor was that morning able to appear in court for the first time since he received his injury, and he stated that about eight o'clock on the night of the 10!.h of Jan. he was in Lord-st., in company with a number of other lads near Fearnett's chip petato shop, where he was employed during the evening. Whilst they were standing there about ten other boys came up from Cooper-street, the prisoner e being one of them. In a few minutes the two gangs of Wit- ness boys did dmnmoet Wantnced t o t o fightfight, n 1-te ll° Sawsawbetlhtse übseeldts. and he was running across the road when Fisher followed him and hit him in the left side, just as witnes= had reached the eurbstone. He saw Fisher's hand, bur did not see any knife in it. He ran into a shop and be then felt that he was bleeding. The two gangs of boys had had a fight before the same night. Cross-examined by Mr. Knowles, witness said he had been in three fights that same night, but he had not fallen on anything, and was only struck once before in his chest. Corroborative evidence was given by several of the boys who were in the" Dukefield gang." Dr. A.-Mlienna deposed dressing the wound, which he described as being an indeed id- one situated over the region of the left ney. For a long me ' the lad was in a very critical %li condition. His iTiness lasted ten weeks. A. sharp- pointedt cutting instrument was likely to cause he , Brundrit considered that wound, such as a knife—Mr. there prima facie eaNe against t prisoner, and committedlere s a him for trial at the Knutsford Quarter .S.s- sions, bail being allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
