        <p>caught the lad before and warned his parents. It being the that offence the magistrates only recorded a convic- tion against him. FRlDAY.—Before Councillor Fleming (chairman) and Mr. W. Pierpoint. THEFT FROM A LODGING HOUSE.—Robert Blanchard was charged with stealing from a model lodging in Friars Green, between Sunday night and Monday morn- ing, the 19th and 20th inst., a man's reefer jacket, cot- ton shirt, and handkerchief, valued 305., the property of John Williams. On Sunday night complainant put the above mentioned articles on his bed, and next morn- ing they were missing. The police were communicated with, and the prisoner was arrested by the Runcorn police on Thurclay, on suspicion. D.S. Monaghan went to Runcorn and identified him. Prisoner was sent to gaol for 1 days hard labour. Dacrxx AND Disonresam--John Garvey, 7, Wakefield- st., ss. or seven days. DESERTER.—James Lyons was convicted of being a deserter, from the N. Staffordshire Regiment. stationed at Portsmouth and was remanded to Strangeways. P.C. Corcoran received the usual reward. BREACH OF CONTRACT.—EIiza Rowlands, School Brow, was convicted of not fulfilling a contract with Edmund Taylor, fustian cutter, Orford-st. Defendant was ordered to pay 58., and costs. A DISHONEST TENANT.—EIiza Wrigley was charged with unlawfully carrying off certain goods and chattels to prevent the landlord, John Corradice, from distrain- ing them for rent. The case was remandkd for a month.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MONDAY.—Beforo Messrs. J. Brundrit and A. Thomas. LlCENSlNG.—Applications were made for the tempor- ary transfer of the following licences :—The Village Inn, Halton, from Albert Edward Matthew Milton to Thomas 11ifolyneux ; The Fleetwood Arms, Church-street, Run- corn, to Martha Oates, from her late husband ; and the Millstone Inn, Mill-street, Runcorn, from Annie Eliza- beth Ashbrook to William Fuller. The two former were granted ; but Superintendent Atherton objected to the request of Mr. Fuller, on the ground that he was not going to conduct the house himself.—lt was decided to adjourn the matter for a week. KEEPING DOGS WITHOUT LICENCES.—Thomas W. Baskerville, of Vicar's-street, and Alfred Ellison, landlord of the man at the Wheel, Church-street, were summoned for keeping dogs without licenses.—P.C. Booth proved the cases, and a fine of 23. 6d. and 9s. 6:1. costs was imposed on the first defendant, and Ellison was ordered to pay 4s. 6d. costs. UNMUZZLED Doos.—The following persons were each fined ss. for allowing their dogs to be at large unmuzzled and without collars :—Edward Kelly, Fryer-street, Run- corn ; Thomas Clough, butcher, Church-street, Runcorn, and William Clarke, Sutton-by-Aston. A CYCLIST IN TROUBLE.—Reginald Johnson, of Aston, was summoned for riding on his bicycle on the footpath at Aston, at 6.15 p.m. on the 11th inst.—Ordered to pay ss. DEFAULTING RATEPAYERS.—SeveraI persons were sum- moned by the Assistant Overseers of Halton and Run- corn for the non-payment of their rates. In three cases remaining unsettled orders for payment in a week were</p>
        <p>made. INannisTas.—Annie Jones, of no fixed residence, Thos. Burton, address unknown, and Margaret Norden, Mer- sey-street, were fined for drunkenness. REFUSING TO QUIT LICENSED PREMISES.—Richard Lewis, of no settled abode, was charged with being drunk in the Nelson Inn, Church-street, and refusing to quit when requested to do so by P.C. Booth, on Satut- day afternoon. —Fined 10s. and fis. Bd. costs, or 14 days' imprisonment.</p>
        <p>WIDNES POLICE COURT. MONDAY.—Before Mr. John Brock and Mr. H. Wade-</p>
        <p>Deacon. DRUNKENNESS.— Catherine Lyons. Upton Rock Michael Harvey, Ann-st. ; John Townsend, Travis-st. ; Patrick Moran, Cromwell-st. ; and John Martin, Patrick Conway, John Madden, and Brian Eavers, of no fixed abode, and Kalachi Handley, Prescot we,e each fined 2s. 6d. and costs of seven days in default, with the exception of Madden who was mulcted in a penalty of Ss. A BEGGING PEDLAR.—Edward Holland, a pedlar, of no fixed abode, was chareed with begging in Albert- road. The prisener who urged that he was selling boot laces and spectacles was sentenced to seven day's hard labour. ASSAULT ON THE POLICE.—Peter Byrne, of Harrison- st., Ditton, was charged with being drunk and disorderly on Saturday night, and with assaulting P.Q. Clarke. The prisoner was takeu into custody on the former charge and on the way to the Police Station he violently kicked the officer and bit him. He was described as more like a madman. The Bench fined prisoner 2s. f3d. and costs for the drunkenness and 59. and costs for tke assault.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>HE LOOTED THE LODGER'S CLOTHES. James Harrison was charged at the Warrington Borough Court, on Saturday, with stealing from the Model Lodging House, Friars Green, a pair of trousers, coat, cap and vest. The prisoner who had beem em- ployed as manager for seven months, pleaded guilty. It appears that the articles had been put on a shelf in the kitchen, and the owner missing them informed P.C. Clarks who was on duty on Friars Green. The con- stable met the prisoner coming out of the lodging-house wearing the coat and trousers. In answer to questions by the constable he said he had them given to him,and that a man named Ted Burke had sold the other articles missing. It was afterwards found that the prisoner had sold them to Catherine Gibbons for 2s. He was then charged with having stolen the articles value 10s., and in reply said, " It is all right,l have nothing further to say." He was sent to gaol for three weeks hard labour.</p>
        <p>A WINDOW SMASHER. At the Borough Court, on Saturday, Josiah Mansfield from Nottingham, pleaded guilty to being drunk and disorderly in Scotland-road on Friday night. and also with bteaking three plate glass windows at 11, and 13, Scotland-road, to the damage of 15s. 9d. Elizabeth Gleave, stated that she heard a smash, and running out saw the prisoner put his foot through the window. The prisoner then went away, and she followed, and with the assistance of two men had him brought to the police station. Annie Gleave, shopkeeper, next door, said she saw tha prisoner put his foot through two of her win- dows also. The prisoner said he came from Liverpool to Warrington to find work. The magistrates sent him to gaol for one month hard labour.</p>
        <p>THE WIFE ASSAULT CASE. At the Borough Court, on Saturday, before Mr. L. Voisey (chairman), and Mr. .J. H. Fleming, William McMutrie,was again brought up on the charge of assault- ing his wife, on the sth inst. Mr. Percy Davies repre- sented the defendant.—Ellen McMutrie, wife of the pri- soner, said her husband struck her on the face, and pulled her about by the hair. He had also thrashed the children. Mr. Davies on behalf of his client stated that the prosecutrix was in the habit of neglecting her hus- band, and the house, and annoyed him by bringing her mother and other women there, drinking, as the pri- soner alleged. The Bench considered both parties were to blame and dismissed the case, but fined prisoner ss. and costs for an offence of drunkenness committed on Friday.</p>
        <p>A ROUGE CRIPPLE. Thomas Gore, a cripple, was charged with being drunk and disorderly and assaulting P.C.'s Leithwood and Sankey, while in the execution of their duty at Friars Gate. From the evidence it appeared that de- fendant went into the Lion Vaults, Bridge-st., but they would not serve him owing to his drunken condition. He then proceeded to Jenkins' boot shop where he wanted to fight Mr. Jenkins. A constable was sent for and while being taken to the police station he kicked Constable Sankey on the thumb, knocking it up. He also abused the other constable. Owing to its being his first appearance the magistrates dealt leniently with him, fining him ss. and costs for being drunk, and 103. and costs for assaulcing the police, or 21 days hard labour.</p>
        <p>WITHOUT At REDEEMING FEATURE. At the Warrington Borough Court, on Friday, Mary Williams was charged with having travelled from Wigan to Warrington, without a ticket, on the 13th ult., with intent to defraud the London and North-Western Rail- way Co. Mr. Fenna for the railway company, said that this case had no redeeming feature in it. Defendant, on the day in question,came off the Bank Quay Station,and told the ticket collector that she had not come off the train, but had merely been on the platform to look after ' some luggage. The.ticket collector believed her and let her pass. A few minutes afterwards the guard of the Wigan train, who had seen defendant get in the train at Wigan, but had no time to get a ticket, came and in- formed the ticket collector. Some luggage belonging to defendant was planed in the cloak room, where Detective Osborne waited until defendant came for it. He then took her name and address,and after some hesitation,she admitted trai7elling without a ticket, and tried several times to settle the matter with Osborne. The guard Thomas Newton, the ticket collector. William James Williams, and Detective Osborne confirmed counsel's statement, and defendant was fined 20s. and costs, or a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. Mort-par.—Before Alderman Holmes (chairman), Major Pickmere, Messrs. F. Monks, S. Wright, and J. }air. dough and Captain Ridgway. DRUNK AND DISORDERLT.—EIIen Carter, Mersey ~on Sunday afternoon, fined ss. including costs ; John Kelly and Jane Kelly, Ship Yard, on Saturday, ss, and costs or 7 days ; Wm. Lythgoe, Winwick-road, 55., or 7 days; Robert Jackson, School Brow, on March 12, 2s. 6d. and WAS. ROOI7E AND VAGABOND.—Mary O'Keilly was charged with being a rogue and a vagabond and with sleeping out in Crown-st., without visible means of subsistence. ' Prisoner was sent to gaol for one month. TRANSFERS OF MUSIC LicaNsas.—Transfers of music licenses were granted to Frank Nield, Ring o' Bells, and Mrs. McCraadie, of the White Hart Hotel. ASLEHP WHILST IN CHARGE OF A HORSH AND LURRT. 1 —Joseph Urey, 28, Fennel-st., was charged with being 1 asleep in Wilderspool-road, while in charge of a horse 1 and lurry, on March 10.—P.C. Bell said that he was in 1 Wilderspool-road near the srossings,when he saw a horse and lurry coming along, and he could see no one in charge. He went to the lurry and found defendant 1 wrapped up in a rug, with his legs hanging over the ' side of the lurry. He had to be well shaken before he awoke. Prisoner was fined 2s. 6d. and costs. UNJUST SCALES.—For having an unjust pair of scales, John Jackson, 32, Dial-st., was fined 2s. ad. and costs, and for having a pair of unstamped scales, in addition to the above offence Wm. H. Harper, was fined 10s. aad costs for each Offence. CHIIiNIITS ON FIRE.—For this offence Alfred Scott, 204, Longford-st., and Robert Jackson, 5, Brick-st., were fined 24. TIIESDAY.—Before Councillor H. Roberts (chairman) and</p>
        <p>Dr. Starkey Smith. Senoor. CASES.—The following persons, for neglecting to send their children regularly to school,were dealt with thus :—William Barnes, School-brow, adjourned for a week,as his child had been sent to Lymm ; John Fiddler, Dial-st., fined 2s. 6d. ; Joseph Langhorn, Beaton-street, 2s. gd. ; Sarlh Jones,Bewsey-st.,2s. 6d. • Patrick Neston, Orford-st., 2s. 6d. ; George Jaundrell, Winwick-road, 25.6 d. - - - • -</p>
        <p>_ VVEDNUSDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclough (in the chair) - • ^ -- •</p>
        <p>Messrs., John Geddes, and J. El.-Fleming. Bloorszo.—JameS Pearson was charged with begging In Bold-st.,, the previous night. He was dismissed on promising to leave the town. FATHER HAD SENT HlM—John Hayes, a youth was charged with stealing 17Ibs. of coal from the siding of the London and North-Western Highway Co., Dalian:, lane, the previous day. owing to complaints received from Messrs. Pendlebury and Co., detective William Etch, of the Railway Co., along with P.C. Turner kept watch, when about 1.30 p.m., they saw the lad enter the siding and VI a bag with coal. When arrested he said "My father has sent me for it." William Timmins, representing Messrs. Pentilebury and Co., Said he had '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note tha reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the head office, Bewsey-street. Warrington, not later than ten o'clock on Wednesday morning.</p>
        <p>CRICKETERS should procure the 1893 Pocket Diary and Companion published by GEO. G. BUSSEr FL CO. It is full of information and has the requirements of an ordinary Leather bound, gilt edges, post free 6d., from the Sports manufactory, Peckham /lye, London.</p>
        <p>RUGBY. WARRINGTON T. BARROW.</p>
        <p>Barrow's appearance at Wilderspool is always suffi- cient guarantee for a good gate, and such happened to be the case on Saturday, when they journeyed to War- rington to fulfil the return engagement. The fine open play of the Furness men has long been admired, and no matter whether in defence or attack they played the game as it ought thi be played, devoid of rough play,and in that interesting way which enthusiasts are not slow to appreciate. Warrington were handicapped on Satur- day by the claims of the county in requiring Boscow and Jolley for the Glamorganshire match, whilst Evans, " Smack " Bate, and Donohue were also away from various causes. With no small amount of anxiety did the homesters take up their position in defending the railway goal, but</p>
        <p>Turner's kick-off</p>
        <p>being mulled, hopes were at once entertained that Warrington, despite their weakness, would come out of the ordeal in a creditable manner, Ashton, Bailey and Fair Turner ably led the forwards, whilst a passing movement initiated by Nevins was the means of Bate, Heesom and Webster maintaining the attack. The last- namA made an effort, but was held by Woodhouse, whilst an infringement by Warringzon saw a slight re- lief coming from Bell's kick. Massey was feeble in his reply, and Woodhouse still gained further ground by a ' judicious kick into touch at half-way. Eventually Massey punted well inside the Barrow quarter, and Ash- ton made a struggle, being grassed near the line, but Lawless was eventually reached with no response. Massey, however, saved; and Handles a minute later sent up a minor with a long kick. Another similar point was forthcoming soon after the drop-out. A free kick was negatived by Frank Turner, and the homester's eagerness to score spoiled a splendid chance. The homesters were now having a lot the best of it, and although Haney relieved neatly the visitors fell back on their lines after some fine work by Bailey,Ashton, Nevins and Berry. Passing by Nevins, Bate, LI eos,am and Web- ster saw the latter °hauled down en the line, whilst a deaf ear was turned to an</p>
        <p>Appeal for a Try. From the following line-out, Nevins saved well, and the ensuing scrimmage saw John Bate Tricking his Opponents and scoring in such a position that Fair Turner was enabled to augment the score five points. Barrow went off with commendable dash on the re-start, and not re- laxing their energies they for the first time invaded the home quarters. A free kick taken by Dickenson saw Webster clearing, Satterthwaite eventually tackling him. Ashton and Frank Turner showed up with respec- tive dribbles, and then Bandies charged a kick and Bell was only just up in time to effect a daring save. VVeb- star failed to take a pass from Ileesom, and Barrow raced away, being checked by Nevins, Ashton, and Fair. Turner, who eased the pressure, and Massey re- lieved a monotony of forward play by a sharp run which once more placed the game at neutral quarters. Eventually Lawless was tested, and having a kick charged by Bell, the latter raced over to score an Easy Try, but the place was. too difficult for Kelly. Turner was early prominent on the drop-out, and tackling his oppo- nents Woodhouse was held on one occasion in close proximity to his goal. Bate and Nevins supplemented his effort, but Higham lost his head when the finishing touch had to be put on. Webster again tried to break through, and Thorniley was almost a scorer in one rush, the chief leaders of which were Berry, Buckley, and Bandies. A fine run by Fair- Turner, in response to one by Kelly, was followed by a pretty bit of passing along the popular touch by Massey, Nevins, and Thorniley, who re-passed to each other until on the line, when the latter dashed over to Score an Unimproved try. Warrington were again within an ace of scoring, after fine passing by Nevins, Bate, and Heesom, Webster again being hauled down, but he eventually got over the line a moment later, but was not allowed to ground. With this slice of ill-luck ends were changed with War- rington leading by a goal and a try to a try. Bowker re-started, and Weatberall at once made tracks for the home goal. His career had an abrupt termination when Webster tackled him, and then pretty work by Haney and Hindle caused Warrington trouble. Although Ash ton relieved, Kelly was especially prominent in helpi•g his side to maintain the aggressive, and although Thorniley and Fair. Turner changed the venue to within a yard of the Barrow line, Chapman, Kelly, and Dicken- son got away, and Kelly had passad the half-way flag before his progress was checked. An attempt by Nevins and Bate gained little ground, whilst Webster was all but a scorer after a run through the tbick of his oppo- nents. From this point the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 25, 1893. AT OUR LOCAL CLIURCHES.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 25, 1893. AT OUR LOCAL CLIURCHES.</p>
        <p>Play of the Ilemesters Deteriorated and despite the frequent calls of the spectators, they seemed powerless to respond. The patience of the home enthusiasts had been tried to its fullest extent, and in a moment cheers were sent up for Barrow, which tended to put fresh vigour in the North Lancashire men, for Kelly and Dickenson seconding some clever play by Haney, the Warrington goal was soon placed in jeopardy. The homesters failed to withstand the attack for a lengthened period, and after Massey had dropped at goal in a flying excursion of Warrington to the Barrow goal, the visitors claimed a monopoly at the pavilion goal, and</p>
        <p>Fla'salt Scored after some fumbling of the home backs. Kelly had a narrow shave for a dropped goal a minute later, and Barrow were decidedly unlucky in not scoring. Nevins and Ashton relieved to half-way, and wken the finish came the score read—Warrington, 1-1-3 ' Barrow, 0-2-2. Teams :—Warrington ; J. Lawless, back; H. Webster. F. Heesom, and J. T. Massey, three-quarter backs ; John Bate and W. Nevins, half-backs; H. Ashton Fair. Turner, Frank Turner, J. Berry, J. T. Thorniley, J. Hip,ham, W. Randles, E. Bailey, and J. Buckley, forwards. Barrow : Wo•dliouse, back ; Kelly, Dickenson, and Bell, three-quarters; Haney and Chap- man, half-backs ; Satterthwaite, Bowker, Weatherall, Parkinson, McDonald, Halsall, Babbs, Smith, and Hindle for wards.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CUP COMPETITION. WARRINGTON v. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>Originally this match was fixed for New Year's Day, but owing to the severity of the frost, the match was unavoidably postponed until last Wednesday evening, when the Wigan enclosure was lined by considerably over 4,000 enthusiasts. Both teams occupied a similar position in the League, and Warrington having won the first tie in somewhat decisive style, speculation was rife as to whether the visitors would maintain their previous good form. Wigan had whipped together a strong team including as it did that smart player J. Seddon, of St. Helens Recreation. Warrington however were handi- cappPd by the absence of Evans and Nevins, business preventing these capital players from performing in so important a contest. The Ki„k off _</p>
        <p>was no advantage to either side, and Webster's (Wigan) kick off was well met by Boscow. The Warringtoniaus were soon in their stride, and matters had a serious as- pect from a Wigan point of view. Jolley was a conspi- cuous figure in the attack and the Bates were both in turn held on the verge of scoring. The visitors main- tained the attack, and Trow eventually got the sphere away only to see Boscow make his mark and a passing movement between the Bates arid Heesom terminating in Whitehead cleverly saving. Again Wigan relieved only to be beaten bask by Massey, and then a fins piece of work between the Bates, Jolley, Turner and Ashton almost ended in a score. James Bats did eventually get over but the Point was Overruled whilst a minute later H. Webster when in difficulties had a start at goal, a minor being sent up. Fair Turner Donohue, and Berry, seconded some smart play, by the Bates, and Heesom, but Wigan now hada turn and fine passing by Holtwell, the brothers Seddon, and Halliwell finally ending in the Warrington quarters being invaded for the first time, Webster and Boscow saving daringly. Warrington quickly returned to the attack, and Trow again relieved. Exchanges between BOSCOW and J. Sed- don ended emphatically in favour of the Warrington back. Another bout of passing by the Wigan backs gave the homesters an insight of the Warrington goal, and a penalty kick was granted the homesters right in front of the posts, but J. Seddon failed to turn the easy place to account. Webster respo—ded to the drop whilst Heesom met the reply in true style, and then a grand run by James Bate from a pass by his companion saw the War- rington star again in ascendancy. Donohue, Berry, and Thorniley dribbled well, but J. Seddon and Trow neu- tralized matters, and another bout of passing this time between John and James Bate, Fair. Turner, and Massey saw the latter finding touch at the Wigan quarter flag. The game continued to be brilliantly interesting, and another grand spell of passing by Wigan was nulli- fied by the safe tackling of Warrington. From a return by Heesom, Webster raced up and passed Trow, but hesitating he was collared by Whitehead and thus a glorious chance was lost. The Bates upset a movement initiated by Halliwell, and then Ashton led the way in one rush which Whitehead turned aside, and the Interval Arrived .</p>
        <p>with nothing substantial scored. Fair Turner restarted I and the Warringtonians again became the aggressors. I Try as the Wiganers did they could not clear, and the Bates and Webster were likely scorers on several occa- sions. A ne pass from John Bate travelled almost from touch to touch, to Massey who was tripped by R. Seddon, and for an infringement Warrington were pen- alized thus again affording Wigan relief. A series of kicks by the Wigan backs eventually saw Warrington mulling one, and Wigan now attacked. From a line out Arkwright and Unsworth got clear, and a bully opposite the posts saw</p>
        <p>Lowe Scoring. From now Wigan pressed hard but the defence of Bc,s- cow and James Bate was brilliant, and nothing else resulting,the game ended in a victory for the homesters 1 by a try to Warrington nil.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. STOCIEPORT.—The return engagement be- tween these Cheshire organizations took place at Stock- port, on Saturday, before a very large concourse of people ;in fact, it was quite a "Derby day." Stockport had whipped together their strongest team, while the champions only took a poor one, being without Kings- ton, the Bros. Faulkner, Stubbs, and Langley, and of course they paid the penalty; Stockport, for the first time in their history, lowering the Runcorn colours. It is needless to say that their victory was received with great jubilation by their supporters. The following were the teams that faced each other :—Runcorn : S. Houghton, back ; E. J. Morris, J. H. Crompton, T. Warder, and G. Webbe, three-quarter backs; S. Abrams and G. Robinson, half backs ; A. Riley, A. Hampson, H. Williams, J. Davies, A. Taylor, S. Derbyshire, F. Dar- lington, and H. Farmer, forwards. Stockport : T. Haslam. full back ; Grainge, Saville, Ashton. and Bailey, three-quarter backs ; Norris and Pickard, half backs ; Potts, Morton, Speet, Mills, Walker, Bailey, Beard, and Downs, forwards. Potts started the game, scrimmaging being indulged in for some time. Play was then stopped owing to Abrams being hurt. Stockport pressed hard until Houghton removed with a good kick. Saville, I however, got the ball back to the Runcorn 25, Norris getting over near the corner, after a scrimmage on the line. Saville kicked a magnificent goal. Even play followed this, the ball being held very tight. Saville ultimately made a mark, and another fine bid for goal. A free kick brought Runcorn relief, play being taken to the Stockport quarters. The visitors now began to show improved form, and a hot attack was made on the home lines. Grainge was penalized for lying on the ball, and Warder, who took the penalty kick, was successful in placing a pretty goal. Following the drop out, a fine passing movement between the Stockport backs caused hostilities to be waged in the visitors' 25. Its stay, however, was but brief, as the Runcornians again rushed down and were within an ace of scoring, having extremely " hard lines," luck being dead against them. I scrimmage brought relief to Stockport, but Runcorn again assumed the upper hand, and a good piece of combination between Abrams and Robinson gave Morris a chance, who cashed grandly over the line. No goal resulted from the kick. The score at half-time was Runcorn. on goal, one try (five points) ; Stockport, one goal (five points). Crompton resumed, Bailey returning to Webbe, who mulled, and nearly let Stockport in. Pretty passing between Abrams, Robinson, Warder, and Crompton took the ball almost to the Stockport line where Crompton passed to Webb who had the line at his mercy, but hesitated, and lost a rediculously easy try. Now followed some exchange kicking between Saville and Houghton, the latter having yards of room "to get his kick in, but allowed it to be charged down, Ashton scoring an easy try. Saville again placed a grand goal. This reverse seemed to demoralize the Runcornians, and again Mills was allowed to score another lucky try. Saville again placed with success. At this point Webbe, who had been playing a miserable game, was put into the pack. Runcorn now made des- perate efforts to draw level, and had considerably the best of matters, hut the worst of luck. Time eventually was called with Stockport winners by 3-0-0 to Runcorn 1-1-0, the heaviest defeat that Runcorn have sustained this season.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" V. ROCHDALE CLEMENTS "A."— Warrington had a rather weak team for this match, which, owing to one of the semi-finals of the Rochdale Charity Cup having to be contested on the same ground, was only of 40 minutes' duration. There were 6,000 spectators present when the home teait commenced operations by dribbling into the visitors' quarters. liampson nullified the advantage, but the Saints, who were showing marked superiority in the forwards, rushed to the Warrington 25, where Ashurst picked up and returned finely to midfield. Lally then made head- way. and a free kick to the visitors was grandly utilized I and Rochdale pinned on their line. The poor packing of Warrington allowed the home team to clear to mid- field, but Gibson made a splendid effort to retrive the lost ground. Some kicking between both sides ensued to the visitors' advantage, the Sainks' forwards, how- ever, quickly transferring the scene of warfare to centre. This effort was supplemented by Rochdale forcing the leather to the line, where a general scramble followed, I the referee giving a try to Ashurst. After this Warring- ton , plAaeliontg a goal from a free: scrimmages opened ' kick,and attacked, halftime Pot ts almostarrived. the second half, the home time in the end pressing. Ashworth tried a shot at goal from a penal ty,bu t Mannion kicked safely to touch at quarter flag. A grand dribble by Dakin, Tears, Monks, and Molyneux carried the;rame to the centre, Potts improving the advantage by good kicking. Some more scrimmaging was seen, followed by Rawson passing to Lally, who ran finely and throwing to Potts this player rushed to the line. Warrington were now playing much better,the forwards particularly, a hot fusillade being made on the Rochdale line. A splendid piece of work by Lally, Itunpson, and Gibson, who was pushed into touch at the corner flag, was cheered to the echo. The visitors pressed determinedly, and it was wonderful how they were kept from scoring time after tim?. riampson and Lally made s4.):endid</p>
        <p>runs, and although Warrington pressed strongly to the finish, the poaching proclivities of the home half-backs proved too much and the time too short for the visitors, who were beaten by one try to nothing. WIDNES V. MOSSLlM—Representatives of the above clubs met on Saturday afternoon on the Lowerhouse- ' lane ground before a large number of spectators. Davie- son kicked off for the visitors, erith the sun shining brilliantly in their faces. The home men pressed, Moss- ley gradually relieving and working up field. ForstEr put in a neat kick, and improved the home position. A minute later Brennan punted smartly and scored a minor. The leather was rushed up field in a dangerous position, but a splendid run by Peters improved matters. Again the visitors attacked, but with little result, as a series of passes amongst the home boys brought the game once more into the visitors' quarters, a scrimmage en- suing on the border of home. Eventually they relieved. Hughes got poseession and passed to Peters,but he failed to tak* the opportunity. Druinmoncl made his marts, but could not score. Play continued in the Mossley quarter, Widnes pressing, but both teams failed to make a point up to the whistle sounding for half tittle. On play being resumed the Mossley men seemed determiaed to improve matters, and attacked vigorously. The ball was rushed down, but Brennan saved. Several scrim- mages followed in the home defence, when F. Lorriman picked up and dropped safely over the bar. The home men pulled themselves together after this, and took the oval up field ; and Forster getting possession scored after a good run, but W. Faulkner failed to convert. Mossley kept the game in a dangerous position, result- ing in a series of scriinmages near the line. Peters put in a splendid run, but was floored within a few yards of the line. Smart play followed, and several brave at- tempts were made by the home men to score, but with- out EVCCeSS. Final result—Mossley four points (dropped goal). Widnes two points (try). Widnes team :—Back, Brennan ; Peters, W. Faulkner, Hughes, three-quarter backs ; Drummond, and J. Faulkner, halt backs ; John- son, Frazer, Forster, Wynne, Whelan, Shaw, Hogan, Ridgeway, and Matthews, forwards. WARRINGTON A V. WIG.IeN A.—This hastily arranged match was fought on the Wilderspool ground on Wed- nesday night in beautiful weather. The visitors who have only suffered defeat on one occasion brought with them excellent credentials and a very strong team. War- rington were the first to attack by the aid of a vigorous rush and Barnes almost dropped a goal. After Wigan had visited the other end, Lally and Barnes put in a fine piece of work, Lawless again receiving the ball passed to Barnes who ran in splendidly, and placed a goal. By strong forward play and long kicking, Wigan became the aggressive party but clever work by Evans and Mannion caused the visitors to act on the defensive. Again the Wigan forwards beat their opponents, and a misunderstanding taking place, a rather soft try was got by Houghton. The teams now turned round, War-, raigton soon showing much improvement. Lawless by splendid play was much in evidence whilst Barnes from a penalty, kicked a very fine goal. The game was much above the average, some very clever exciting episodes being witnessed, and alter Holmes had made a grand run to the line, Lally dodged in. Warrington forwards showed op in some irresistible loose rushes, and Evans throwing to Ashurst the last named ran in a fine try. Warrington retired winners by 2-2-3 to 0-1-3. WARRINGTON 2ND V. ROCHDALE ST. CLEMENT'S 2ND.— Played at Warrington on Saturday. Owing to the "A " team taking five of the second team's best players they had to find substitutes, who acquitted themselves creditably. Rochdale started, and Warrington rushed up the field, Slater scoring a try, Boldsworth converting. Grand passing between Rowe and Boldswarth tosk play again on the visitors' lines, and flamblett dropped a neat goal. From the kick-off Warrington again displayed remarkable form, and Rowe, from a scrimmage, got away in grand style, and, passing to Lawless, enabled this player to race ever. Warrington continued to attack, and after ancther spell of passing Daniels ran in at the corner. Halt-time score—Warrington, 2-2-4 ; Rochdale St. Cle:nent's, nil. Rowe restarted, and sterl- ing. After a spell of passing by Rowe, Smith, and Garrity, the last-named scored under the posts, which was quickly followed by Thornton. The remaining portion of the game proved to be in favour of the borne team, who ran out winners by 2-5-5 to nil. Patina Crtuacn v. Si'. MARY'S.—Great interest was centred in the meeting of these teams, which event took place on the lower ground of the Warrington Football Club. The first match had resulted in a victory for the Parish men, and being already leaders in the competi- tion, their success was looked upon as well nigh certain. An attendance of 600 witnessed the match, and on the game being commenced,both goals were in turn attacked. However, the Churchmen's combination was by far the better, and as a result of brilliant passing between Garrity and Carey, Welsby ran in. This was the only point scored during the first half, though mention should be made of a smart drop by Hampson. During the last stage of the game the Saints commenced well, but ill-luck followed them, and the Churchmen showing superior science and stamina, ultimately came out win- ners by a two goals and a try to nil, Daniels and Mason adding the tries, whilst Bolesworth placed the goals. ROCHDALE HORNETS V. RuNconss.—These teams met in the ordinary match on Tuesday, at Rochdale, before 2,000 spectators. Rochdale started the game, and after ten minutes play Midgeley scored, the game failing at goal. Half-time score—Hornets, 1 try ; Runcorn, nil. On resuming, the Cheshire team played finely, and War- der dropped a beautiful goal. Runcorn ran out victori- ous by a dropped goal to a try. RUNCORN VICTORIA V. WIDNES ROVERS.—Played on the Victoria ground, being the return fixture, and after some exciting play the Victories were declared winners by one goal to one try.</p>
        <p>ST. ALBAN'S CHURCH. On Sunday at St. Alban's Roman Catholic Church, Bewsey-street, solemn High Mass was celebrated at 11 o'clock. The Rev. T. I. Craven, 0.5.8.. was celebrant, assisted by the Rev. T. B. Feeney, 0.5.8., deacon, and the Rev. J. S. Cody, 0.3.8., sub-deacon. The Very Rev. Canon Woods, 0.5.13., of St. Michael's Pro-Cathe- dral, Hereford, preached an eloquent sermon from the text" And Christ hid himself from them." In explana- tion of the text, the Very rev, preacher said that al- mighty God was in a sense hidden in the whole of crea- tion and at times there was a distinct manifestation of him. For example, in the world round about us, in the roaring of the torrent, in the thunderbolt, and in the storm, as also in the working. of man's heart, and as in his different manifestations in the world at sundry times. _ . . _ _ .</p>
        <p>Continuing the Rev. Canon said to come to the 6atholic Church of this day, he was specially hidden in the real presence, in the tabernacle, and the blessed sacrament. : It was from this central source of life that Almighty God poured out his graces and his lights upon the whole of his faithful followers. The preacher made a special reference to the beautiful and exquisite work of art which was present before their eyes that day in the magnificent altar which was a work of genius. Be ap- pealed to his hearers not to leave God's altar in debt, but to consider it was a privilege to contribute to this great work of sanctifying souls. There was a very large congregation who listened to the discourse with much attention. In the evening the " Stabet Mater" was sang by St. Alban's choir under the conductorship of Mr. Whitehead, with great effect and precision, and Rossini's greatest production was perhaps never better heard than on Sunday evening. The light and shade of the music were attended to with wonderful accuracy, and reflected considerable credit upon the choristers. As regards the solists nothing but praise can be said. each singing with an earnestness that always carries success with it. Mr. Joseph M. Bate added to his re- putation by his excellent rendition of the " Cujus Ani- mam." Those actually conversant with the music will readily understand the difficulties this solo presents to amateurs. Mr. Bate is the fortunate possessor of a tenor voice of good range, power and sweetness and ia addi- tion he uses it with discretion and skill. Miss Cavanagh was eminently successful in the "In Flammatus," the ' dramatic intensity of which was heightened bv the effective singing of the choir. The "Pro Peccatis 3' was vigorously rendered by Mr.A. Cavanagh, who sang with much feeling. Mi. J. B. Caldwell as the accompanist discharged his task with creditable skill. The congre- gation was overflowing and the Very Rev. Dr. Larkin, 0.5.8.. occupied the pulpit. The subject was" Christ, the son of God, the God of Peace." The service con- cluded with the Benediction and Divine Praises. THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>On Sunday, the P.S.A. at Wycliffe Church was well attended, there being an increase on the previous week's attendance, despite the fact of the counter-attraction of the sacred concert at the Royal Court Theatre. The number of members now enrolled is over 400. The Chairman was Mr. Robert Garnett, J.P. An earnest address was delivered by Mr.Drysdale,of Liverpool,who, it may be stated, was the gentleman concerned in the tract incident with Professor Huxley at Llandudno. The musical portion of the service was enhanced by a solo contributed by Mrs.A. Lane, a quartet "I was a wander- ing sheep " (Paston Bonus), by Messrs. R. H. Pearson, E. Booth, and Miss Payton and Miss B. Ratlibme. Mr. R. H. Pearson also contributed a violin solo in an excellent manner. Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ with his usual ability.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening at Wycliffe Church, the Rev. J. Yonge preached a sermon upon "Christian Unity" tak- ing his text "For we, being many, are one bread, and one body, for we are all partakers of that one bread." 1 Cor : 10c., 17 v. Mr. Yonge said the theme for their thought that evening was fixed for them by the deeply interesting service which was to follow the present one. In many places it had long been a more or less familiar custom, but among the churches of this particular town it was still almost strange, for the occurrence of that .. .</p>
        <p>evening was only the seitOrrd of its kind in Warrington. Never could a " Re-union of Christendom" that was sometimes spoken of be realised, so long as any one church stipulated acceptance of its particular, essen- tial, and characteristic idea as an indispensable condi- tion. The only unity possible to all the churches, was that which some of the churches of this town would join in at Christ's table that night, and the unity of percep- tion that " One is our Master, even Christ, and all we are brethren." Never let us forget that the unity of His people in the world was expressed by Christ Himself, as even His supremest desire. Every Christian commun- ity which would find representation in the united coin- munion service that night had its own work for Christ and men to do in this town. All the churches put to- gether had not yet impressed Warrington with the stamp and seal of vital christianity. This town was yet a stronghold of Satan, and the majority of its people were more or less under the slavery of sin. The churches of Christ in his opinion would act with exceeding wisdom if they would come as closely and compactly together as possible and present a united front to the common foe. In his opinion the difficulty and reluctance which the Anglican friends experience in the matter of religious co-operation with members of the Free churches, ought to be a subject of the most serious concern. They were prepared to work with them in every possible way for the advancement of truth and righteousness, but they, however, discovered doctrinal and ecclesiastical reasons which made it hard for them to work on any lines except their own, fie was presuaded that whatever hinders or defeats essential Christian unity, could only be essentially wrong. The practical end which the united communion services of that evening was designed to serve was two-fold. It was meant to deepen the feeling and conviction in the minds of the members of their churches and denominations that the matters in which they agreed were infinitely greater than those in which they differed, and further it was meant to testify to the world that though as ecclesiastical denominations, they each followed their own conscientious convictions in matters of method and detail, as christians they were brethren. In all the essentials of gospel belief, the Methodist, Presbyterian, Congregational, Rehrmed Episcopal, and Baptist Churches were more or less in agreement. The only hindrance of the Baptiste, in sharing in the united communion service was created by their belief that only immersed believers were eligible for the Lord's Table. They would no doubt come out of that latest remnant of intellectual and spiritual bondage eventually, as many of their denomina- tions had already done. Warrington was waiting to see christian unity displayed among the ! churches of this town. Just now, the town was becoming newly impressed by the differences of Christians. In some quarters those differences were be- ing sharpened and accentuated by acrimonious contro- versy betweon rival Christian communions. Our en- deavour had been to avoid bitterness and only to set forth truth. Honest discussion that searched for truth, was different from heated coctroversy which thirsts to overcome. But after all was said, there must ever re- main matters in which Christian intelligence would differ. Let them agree to differ! What was wanted was " the unity of the spirit in the bond of peace," and then a glorious and manifest " striving together for the faith of the gospel." Let us minimise our differences, and magnify our agreements. Men would be drawn to the churches in proportion as the churches were drawn to each other. But if, instead of this, there be a jealous spirit, a narrow denominational, sectarian spirit abroad in the Churches it will bar their progress, and dishonour Christ. Before very long, a public and general move- ment may probably be inaugurated that will call for the united action in our whole community. When that tine arrives, may our Christian men of every name be found willing to combine against the common enemy. the one supreme Christian aim and endeavour must he more intensely than ever, to win Warrington for Jesus Christ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>UNITED COMMUNION SERVICE:,</head>
        <p>A UNITED COMMUNION SERVICE:,</p>
        <p>_ A united commudion service, the second in the religi- ous history of the Evangelical Nonconformist Churches in Warrington, was held in Wycliffe Church on Sunday night. A very large number of friends from the several churches were present. The Rev. J. Yonge presided ; an address was given by the Rev. A. Freeman, and prayers were led by the Rev. J. Warnock, and the Rev. E. T. Reed. Other ministers present were the Revs. T. Kirtlan, J. Gunnell, T. Ross, and W. Thornley. Repre- sentatives were also present from several local No %icon- formist missions. The collection was in aid of the British and Foreign Bible Society. HILL CLIFFE BAPTIST CHAPEL. Owing to the confined and somewhat structurally dangerous condition of the Hin Cliff e chapel, the trustees have decided to extend,overhaul and altogether remodel , the building, and to this end drawings have been pre- pared and the work has been put in hand, and it is an- ticipated that the alterations and additions will be com- pleted by about the middle of June. The building is to be cousiderably extended, reseated, and a large portion re-roofed and floored. A commodious vestibule is to be formed at the entrance, and, altogether, it is hoped, as I a considerable amount of money is being expended,that a great improvement will be made to this historical old place of worship. The work is being executed by Mr. J. Houghton, builder, Whitley, from drawings prepared by Mr. Percy Silcock of Warrington. - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>" P.S.A. MOVEMENT."</head>
        <p>" P.S.A. MOVEMENT."</p>
        <p>At the weekly meeting of the P.S.A. held in the Young Men's Christian Association Rooms, Friars Green, on Sunday afternoon. Mr. T. S. Kent delivered an address on the " P.S.A. Movement." In the course of his re- marks he showed how the meetings would bo made thoroughly successful. The institution, he said, was not intended to be in any way a rival to the ordinary churches and chapels and Sunday Schools. They were intended principally for those who did not attend any place of worship, but who spent their time in lounging about the streets. Miss Piekton sang in an excellent manner " The Chorister," and " Thou art passing hence." Master Piekton accompanied on the plan o,and Mr. Brayshaw On the organ. Mr. R. Wylie presided. There was a fair attendance.</p>
        <p>Cala!' T.TNE.—Ce,ment for Prolm Artivies, 61 and postage. 21. z.zolit every where, home and abroad.—Atiet•</p>
        <p>A SATURDAY NIGHT WITH A WARRINGTON DETECTIVE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ASSOCIATION. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT MEDALS COMPETITION. FIRST ROUND</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT MEDALS COMPETITION. FIRST ROUND</p>
        <p>_ WARRINGTON CRICKET V. PADOATE INSTITIITE.—TIIRSe teams met on Saturday on the Cricket Ground at Arpley, before a fair gate, in the first round of the War- rington and District Medals Competition. Padgate won the toss, and Connor started the ball towards the pavilion goal. For a short period from the start, play was of an even character, but the forwards breaking through their opponents' defence, Connor missed an easy chance. A moment later, however, the same player put the ball out of the visitors' custodian's reach with a grand shot. Again the home forwards bore down on the Padgate goal, Hoare this time putting on the finishing touch. Three more goals for the home team were put through by Connor, and half-time arrived with the score—Cricket, five goals; Padgate nil. After the re-start the visitors very seldom got past the home halves, or when by chance they did, Francomb and Hannah were quite prepar.d to meet them. A good run on the home lett wing resulted in Isherwood scoring with a splendid shot. The Cricket centre forward, Connor, seemed to possess a scoring mania, and during the second half piled on seven goals. The home left half, Chidlaw, centred finely, and Connor headed the ball from abeut two feet off the ground, while the Pad- gate men stood still and wondered. The final result of the game was—Cricket, 13 goals ; Padgate, nil. The play of the home eleven was excellent, and Connor scored eleven goals himself. WARRINGTON Sr. ELPHIN'S V. MaasroN.—Played at Marston, on Saturday. The Saints kicked off against the wind, Taylor forcing the home custodian to handle. Play was of a very even nature, but the Saints' forwards began to feel their feet, and the visitors' left, who was playing in grand form, centred the hall, Scarisbrick hav- ing no difficulty in scoring. This stirred the homesters up and they scored from a scrimmage. Half time result —one each. On the resumption of hostilities Clayton gave the visitors' the lead, Hickbottom scoring. One of the visitors' backs next headed through, and a minute later Hatton scored. Taylor and Hatton getting posses- sion, Hatton shot for goal, one of the homesters deliber- ately handling the ball. The visitors got a penalty kick, and R. Colly shot a goal. The visitors' forwards here played well, and Clayton sent through from a corner kick, but no one had touched the ball. Hatton then scored another goal, the whistle sounding shortly after with the result—St. Elphin's, six goals ; Marston, one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WARRINGTON ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE. RATHABLE VALUE OF THE GAS WORKS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSESSMENT COM- MITTEE. RATHABLE VALUE OF THE GAS WORKS.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Assessment Committee of the Warrington Union, was held on Wednesday. There were present Mr. Ttalharry (in the chair), Messrs. Heaton, Ratcliffe, Shaw, Davies, W. Hough- ton, J. Houghton, and A. Jolley, J. C. Sutton (clerk) S. Sutton (deputy clerk). Wm. Owen (valuer), J. L. Tunstall and Henry Houghton (assistant over- seers.) Mr. OWEN said that Mr. Tunstall and he had met the Town Clerk (Mr.J. L.Whittle), the gas manager, and Mr. Newbigging, the valuer for the Corporation, and re- ceived the following report of the valuation of the works —" The assessment on the gas works undertaking in Warrington only before March 1891 was, rateable values, urban £5,229 105., rural £6l. The amounts were raised by the overseers in March 1891, rateable values, urban £7,662 10s., rural £6l. After going through the figures prepared from the accounts by Mr. New brigging, and his valuation for the Corporation with considerable discussion, it was settled by Mr. Tunstall and himself tq accept, subject to the Committee's ap- proval, the rateable values, urban £5,910, rural £35. If the Committee approve ov the figures, a oasis would be settled on which the rateable value of the undertaking could be annually ascertained. The report was adopted on the motion of ,Mr. ftATCLIFFE, seconded by Mr. A. JOLLEY. The CLERK said that his salary would have to be con- sidered at that meeting. He had a fixed salary of £125 which was repaid by the Government on death duties. He also had an allowance of £25 for extra work. Dur- ing the past year he had had a great amount of extra work, and it would be for them to consider to what he was entitled, but on a former year he had been allowed £5O for exceptional work—Mr. Huarox moved that the Clerk 11817 e £3O extra for his services and Mr. A. JOLLEY seccnded and the resolution was passed unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>STRANGE SIGHTS AND QUEER STRANGERS.</head>
        <p>STRANGE SIGHTS AND QUEER STRANGERS.</p>
        <p>BY LUKE BLUNT.</p>
        <p>As I sit in the full play of the sun's warm rays, and listen to the chirping of the birds,and smell the fresh turf and see the green grass from the open window, my thoughts go back to a Saturday night of the winter. lam fond of reality as a rule, but have a greater affection for a holiday in the sun- shine than a night of realities such as the one I am about to write of. For the purpose of assisting in the identification of a person who was "wanted" by the police, I accompanied a local detective round to haunts of mendicants and others who are the pat- rons of the common lodging-houses of Warrington's slums; slums which the ratepayers of Warrington possess, but which, unfortunately, few know any- ing of. He said the Town Hill houses would be the first ; so we started.</p>
        <p>We proceeded along Buttermarket-st. until we reached the narrow passage leading up to Town Hill. It was a dark,dirty, little alley, and we thrust our way through a throng of ragged, slovenly- I dressed men and women, who were engaged in a Saturday night row. We stopped opposite a closed ' door, and the officer lifting the latch,led the way in. It .was a small, low-topped room; along one side was a table with forms at each side, and seated at which, were two tramps con- jointly enjoying something which savoured of fried liver. Standing up at one end of the table were a German-looking individual, and a strongly-built, loutish kind of person with shag tobacco coloured whiskers, and ragged dirty clothes. They were in the midst of a hot harangue which bade fair to end in a fight. The landlady who stood over the fire frying a herring was tearing away at the couple with her tongue. She called one a " shrimp " and the other a "son of a gun," and by way of try- ing to arrive at a settlement she threatened to throw both out if they didn't "Climb down some." Immediately we entered, all but the foreigner cooled down; they knew my compan- ion. But the foreigner just fizzed away with a cascade of bad English. He danced round the lout in a clumsy elephantine kind of manner; and nothing seemed to occur to him whereby he could express his rage, but "Oh-vel you vas a donged tief."</p>
        <p>The detective inquired what was the matter, and the lout in a dogged kind of way tried to explain that he had a loaf and the German had once had one. But now he from the Fatherland had demo- lished his and wanted to claim that he hadn't, and that the lout had taken it. And during all this the German danced round with his "(longed tief." The detective with that discriminatioa born of the knowledge of his subject had soon got the lout to hand over the loaf; and seem glad to do it without [ being locked up. He was a character who had served " Time '' more than once for thefts com- mitted in common lodging-houses. We turned to examine the room properly and in one corner espied a figure very familiar to anyone who has lived in Warrington long. Ile is a cripple. He is aged 49; connected with a respectable Warrington family but is destitute and cannot work. He lives by beg- ging of people who know him, and has been in the workhouse twelve times, but never stayed longer than a week, lie is an ardent salvationist, but unlike most pious men he has a poor con- ception of what distinguishes right from wrong. Once, a certain well known publisher in town tried to help the poor fellow by giving him 10 dozen papers to sell and offering half the pro- ceeds. But the pious cripple did'nt come back to him and say "here take all the money. lam grateful for a day's work without pay." Oh, no! He came back and said he met a man in that lodging-house-who told him he was entitled to al/the money; said he had spent it, and wanted some more papers at the same price. Once he had a valuable overcoat given him in winter ; when the giver's heart had been stirred to pity by the sight of the shivering cripple all un- protected, and a homeless wanderer in the biting wintery blasts. Well, did he wear it in summer and point out that it had saved his life; and shed tears of gratitude? Oh, dear, no ! He went to Manchester next day to look for a cousin. When he came back he was without the coat ; and he con- fessed at the " meeting " that he had "done in Rome what Rome does. Sold the coat for a penny." Just new he was wearing a private porter's uniform ' with a straw hat decorated with streaming rib- bons, and the name" Victor " printed on in golden letters. He was reading his Bible assiduously, and no one seemed to heed him.</p>
        <p>We left and continuing along the passage came on to Town Hill. Here we turned sharply down a still more narrow and dark entry; and in a few minutes we had been in and out of quite a number of common lodging-houses ; all hidden away in this dark gruesome alley. The houses are all pour crazy- looking structures ; with little ventilation, and less accommodations of a sanitary character. And what creatures we c•aught passing.glimpses of in these places? Take one place. On a rude wooden sofa was a grey-haired woman, whose worn frame and wasted features said she was soon for eternity. She lay there helplessly drunk. No oue noticed her apparently. In a corner of the room was a ragged ' tramp, with hair bedecken with hayseed. He was swearing in a drunken incoherent way ; and blood was flowing from a wound on his head on to his face. But no one noticed him--not any more than if he was a blue bottle fly buzzing. We emerged from these dark spots, crossed Buttermarket-street, and were soon again diving in amongst a maze of dark deserted slums; in narrow alleys, between rickety houses, in places which few in Warrington, except those who live there, know to exist. No one teemed to be about until we passed an entry, and then regularly a heavy lounging figure would 101 l out and confront us; and it wouldn't speak ; it just used to look at my companion, recognized him, and then skulked back into its dark cave of an entry. Through this kind of thing, I was in a constant state of fluctuating goose flesh. I wouldn't go down those places alone on a dark night for any- thing less than £lO.</p>
        <p>We had visited twelve more houses ; in all of which were tramps and corner-men. It struck me that when the Corporatioa get up their municipal lodging-houses it will be a good thing. Fancy 70 or 80 common lodging-houses—and not the most sanitary places—situated in localities of the charac- ter I have referred to, and harbouring a class of people who require to be under the strictest police supervision—probably containing about 100 corner- men and a moving army of about 200 tramps. "Just keep close behind me,' broke in the voice of the detective presently, "We are getting to the two last houses." Then he turned into a long, arched pas- sage; it was as dark as the Hole of Calcutta; I held the lap of the detective's coat, and couldn't even see his back. Every step I stumbled over a stone or an unevenness in the passage, but at last we arrived in a large square yard, and saw the sky overhead again. Two houses faced us and as we approached, the first one the sounds of a wheezy, dyspeptic, broken-backed concertina came floating on the air, keeping indifferent time to a rebellious kind of voice, which was howling : .</p>
        <p>" Sing ho! me bhoys, me bully bhoys Sing ho ! ye-ho! ye-ho ! "</p>
        <p>I could have laughed any other time ; but it seemed wierd now. We stepped into the house, and there was an old sailor fellow entertaining his fellow lodgers with a concert. They stopped when we entered ; but we were not there long. Half a dozen strides brought us to the other house. We opened the door, but stopped on the threshold. The room was lighted by one candle ; a small fire flick- ered in the grate. and opposite it was a basket over which leant a young woman. Her haggard face was drenched with tears ; she was sohoing, and hysterically kissing at something in the basket. "Is there anyone else in ?" A shake of the heaciand another outburst of sobs. " What are you blubbering about ?" More sobs.</p>
        <p>-a I The detective walked up to the basket, followed. I saw in a minute what she was ".131,u,'„ bering" for. There was an eight months old ch11".,1; the basket; it's little features bore an innocent .soll'a; but its face was white ; its cry was stilled; it Wno asleep—in the sleep of death. The young vicgilh,:r was a tramp. The child's father—who was not husband—had reported the death to the police.</p>
        <p>Next I found myself in the " model " in t„:1 Old Road. Forty years ago this place was eree ass Primitive Methodist Chapel. It now ace° es modates about 80 male lodgers—all ship canal .1114 except a few. I was making some inquiries apl',.er this old place when one of the few, a chars well known to the police, came in. He is a rilf of about 30, and is a sad instance of the result 0 4 misguided early lite. When we entered he grit'iss. a grin of recognition and apologized for his tis Bence there. The detective said, You can tell 15 gentlemen something about the old chapel ea' you 1 " Well," was the reply "I've lived about her Btieria I was a young 'un, so I know a bit. I can't ess you much about the preaching cause, it was alissa my creed to encourage it. I know a joke or s' about it though. Haw! bawl haw ! " Pk° "Is it convenient to tell one of those now ?" " Well, you see yonder front entrance as is bloc 10 up now. Me and a pard o' mine had a Pod theere one Sunday. We wos standing arc'ser Ohe door—cussing a bit for a change. Old-05i gets his name—was praching ; is was about ' 4a among us; and my pard rays Jack will bring "„ s among 'em' and we done it. We went and 0.0 donkey from one of th' fields close by and hougeos it along. When we got to th' door my pard it gently: then we lead th' moak to it mid Plis 'end In it went with a Haw-how; right among sei my pard let go waive cabbage stalk at hit tb' ti) es mon preaching on the nose. Well, they howleuses, if owd Nick were among 'em. And we clutte` Haw ! haw! haw r This story will be remembered by several el ' for Primitive Methodist preachers in Warrington' I have heard it told by two of them myself the oldest in the town who preached in the easrPiss• The place had to be closed as a chapel in quence of th o behavour of the neighbourbooa wards it and the congregation. 10' THE NEW EDUCATION CO The Revised Code of Minutes of the Education ment, for the regulation of sch,ols under the Elementary Education Acts, w