        <p>AN ABOMINABLE CALUMNY. TiCußarles Campbell was charged on remand, on rsday, before the Warrington magistrates, on a ...,uttrge of • m neglecting to semi his son regularly to • aty's Catholic School,Latchrord. Mr. G. Riley, ir°ol Attendance Inspector,said that this case was tolourned from the previous week to make inquiries as bottle extraordinary allegation which the mother of the seil_lnaae to the effect that the boy was afraid to attend because he was punished by the mistress because !ailed to observe Some religious duty. He (Mr. Riley) deed, inquiries of the boys who attended that sta:n- -eati,:l,. and not one of them could remember him being 14itritliatlijahad had He boy before him, and the boy mother made him say it because she wanted off the fine. 10,14 t. Arthur Browne representing Miss Coleman, the earolinistress, said that he had no right to appear in the thise, btit still when a person's character was blasted like v 4..itli their Worships' permission, he should like to t,iittlitit Miss Coleman asserts that there is not a word of tvith In the allegation. She has had a lot of trouble to_ the defendant' who was continually drinking. Be- aalq the last summons defendant came to Miss Coleman, WoZid that unless she wrote a letter begging their she w ,Ps to thrash the boy so as to get out of the fine, rath °ulci round on her. She has carried out her threat. the et Rennelly as well as Mr. Riley had inquired into iancitn atter. That case had gone through papers in Eng,- te alioancl he throught it was only right to Miss Coleman, , .%‘, her to deny it on oath. teirr Unsworth said that he appeared for Father Hen- repYl nne of the managers of the school, and he utterly vislitkiiated the calumny, and the managers of the school the el to thank their Worships for the consideration Mr flowed in adjourning the case for inquiries. tat Chandley then asked Mrs. Campbell if she reite- threllor retracted the statement she made last week, to k Mrs. Campbell answered that it was untrue. tit Chaney said that that was quite the impression Bench last week. They were certain that an iphZtlable libel had been committed ; and they thought the case was first heard, that the teacher should loete, 1111 opportunity of refuting it in public. They %hie ,owever, so convinced that this was an aborrnm that 'ntithY that they did net now think it necessary terfee,s3 Coleman should deny it on oath. They were Th_" wY satisfied of her innocence. Bench inflicted the full fine of ss. or seven days.</p>
