        <p>Acta„uting Sunday afternoon, the house of Mr. c:tt. rerley, near St. Thomas's Church, Stockton :;;e,34th n, was broken into, and a gold watch stolen Jani"„nne of the bedrooms. Mr. Timperley and his .Intn"Y Were all out during the afternoon and upon Paa,,rning home Mrs. Timperley observed that a /tin" nf glass in the kitchen window was broken, 'the ow the Window" catch." It was then seen that Ann, indow had been opened, and further investigations -Ita,ved that the watch had been abstracted from a one; in one of the bedroom tables. %sat t„r,i,day evening last week, the house of a farmer Ohre,'" the Warrington Cemetery was broken into by 'keen- In". It seems that the mother of the tenant ifil°ll one of the ground floor rooms, in consequence beari- About 12 o'clock she was awakened by tat npsg a grating noise at the front door. It continued Iligh°t—vetnne, and then she heard the door pushed `aain'Peri• It was further fastened with a strong Old lady had remained quiet until now, but she k. ili,"„owever,and the burglars were unable to wrench kivak: 14,D0n hned a servant girl who slept in the same room rasp ea„,r,ing the buimilars round at the back of the :ne , ne girl was 'afraid to leave the roam to awaken '4ag t "„'ady's son and remained for five minutes listen- -4 raspinG of the lock. At length the lock was Atieten'° break and the back door opened. The girl *tobborip„°,,n shrieked out for help, and the would-be 'hroe ,`-'ecarriped. The old lady said she distinctly saw niOraltenl,t,hern pass her window. Her son who was tta: s" 0Y the alarm instituted an investigation but Iti.teps of th 6 men were found. The matter was re- ', Tlie-r„tc'hth° Police. %Ikbouti`h"ave been a number of thefts from outhouses an 0 town reoorted and it is believed that there is of thieves in the neighbourhood. tsz-</p>
