      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>STRANGE DEATH OF A WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>STRANGE DEATH OF A WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>GIRL. ALLEGED RESULT OF "CRAMMING."</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse. county coroner, held an inquest on Monday at the Horse and Jockey Inn, Winwick- road, on the body of Sarah Glover, aged ten years, , the daughter of Thomas Glover, labourer, 141, Win- wick-road.—Mrs. Glover said that her daughter had , been healthy and had never required a doctor until quite recently. About a month ago, she fell with her forehead against the sink pipe, while playing in the yard. She only received a slight wound which soon healed up. On Saturday week, deceased com- plained of earache and headache, and, as she did not get better, Dr. Fox was called in, and he attended her until her death on Friday, the 17th inst. Witness asked him if the illness was caused by the fall, but he said 'Not at all. She is suffering from inflammation of the mem- brane of the brain. The fall had nothing to do with it." When deceased died, the father went for a certificate, which Dr. Fox, however, declined to give saying that an inquest would be necessary—The Coroner said it was strange that Dr. Fox should tell the mother that the fall had nothing to do with the illness, as he told the police the opposite.—Mrs. Glover said that she was not mis- taken as to what Dr. Fox had told her. She said that her daughter had suffered from over work at school.— The Coroner: "Yes they do worry them. I wish they would give them more physical recreation."—Ellen Glover gave evidence of the fall. Her sister had never stopped away from school as a result of the fall.—Thos. Glover confirmed his wife's statement that Dr. Fox had said that his daughter's illness was not the result of the fall. Dr. Fox had asked him to allow his daughter to be taken to the Infirmary for an operation, but he had re- fused giving permission.—The Coroner said that if the jury wished to have Dr. Fox's evidence they could have it, but the inquest would then have to be adjourned. The doctor had wished to have a post mlortem examina- tion, but if he knew what was the cause of death there would be no need for it. His (the Coroner's) opinion was that being over-pressed in school work must have caused over-pressure of the brain. The children now-a- days were not so strong looking as they used to be, and wore spectacles at an early age like old folk, as a result of over-pressure at school. He would suggest that the verdict inflammation of should be that deceased died from of the membrane of the brain, but how caused there is not sufficient evidence to show.—The jury returned a verdict accordingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>EJECTMENT OF AN APPLETON</head>
        <p>THE EJECTMENT OF AN APPLETON</p>
        <p>FARMER.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER QUESTION IN THE HOUSE OF COMMONS.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday evening, Mr. Jordan asked the Secretary of State for War if he had seen, in the "Northwich Chronicle," a report of an interview with Colonel Warburton, in which it was alleged that he said he would require in future that any new tenant coming to his estate must be a Churchman, and that he must also join the yeomanry ; whether, considering that her Majesty's forces were recruited by voluntary enlistment, it was competent to Colonel Warburton so to press men into his troop; and whether the Secretary of War would inquire further into this case. Mr. Campbell Bannerman: I answered a question on this subject a few days ago. I have no power to inter- fere with the terms of a lease given by a landlord to his tenant. The condition as to serving in the Yeomanry is certainly not well-advised in the interests of that force. CHESHIRE FARMERS AND THE YEOMANRY. The Chester Farmers' Club on Saturday discussed the question of compulsory yeomanry service on a resolution of sympathy with Mr. Joseph Knowles, of Yew Tree Farm, Appleton. This resolution having been carried, Mr. Thomas Parker moved another asking that the yeo- manry cavalry should be disbanded on account of its obnoxious compulsory service. In a speech which caused a good deal of laughter Mr. Parker said no Englishman objected to paying a fair share of taxation to support a thoroughly efficient army, but he did begrudge support- ing a system which called away farmers from their work to play at soldiering. A hundred Uhlaus would cut a thousand Cheshire yeomanry into ribbons. Many of the yeomen were too stout to gallop very fast either be- fore or after an enemy. Mr. George Cooke entered a protest against this attack, and Mr. Fryer moved a direct negative. The resolution was, however, carried by nineteen votes to three.</p>
        <p>protest</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>COTTON STRIKE. SIGNIFICANT CIRCULAR.</head>
        <p>THE COTTON STRIKE. SIGNIFICANT CIRCULAR.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning the shareholders of the Glod- wich Spinning Company's large Oldham firm received circulars calling a general meeting for the 30th instant, to consider the propriety ot re-opening the mills at the earliest opportunity, and, if desirable, to withdraw at present from the Employers' Federation. If the dispute is settled before Thursday next the meeting will not be held. The circular is regarded as indicative of the end of the struggle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>PRIVATE CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>A PRIVATE CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The conference arranged between the representatives of the Employers' Federation and the Executives Com- mittees of the Operatives' Associations with regard to the settlement of the dispute in the spinning industry was begun on Thursday at an hotel a few miles from Manchester. Considerable care was taken to arrange the proceedings with privacy. The conference began at three o'clock in the afternoon and was continued at intervals until midnight. At one o'clock on Friday morning the conference was still sitting and no result had been attained.</p>
        <p>SETTLEMENT OF THE DISPUTE. BROOKLANDS, SALE, FRIDAY, 3.30 A.M.—The Confer- ence has ended, and the dispute has been settled. The operatives asked a reduction of 2i per cent, and the employers one of 3 per cent. Finally it was agreed by the operatives to accept a reduction of 7d. in the £, The trade-union clause was withdrawn by the employers. Clauses dealing with the furtherance of the trade in future were agreed to. Copies of the agreement will be put before full meetings of both employers aed opera- tives for their approval, and the mills are to start work- ing on Monday.</p>
        <p>COLONEL SAUNDERSON "LET DOWN."</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday, Mr. Labouchere said : I beg to ask the Secretary for War whether his attention has been called to the report in the " Times " of a speech delivered by the member for the North Armagh (Colonel Saunderson) on the 15th March at an Orange and Unionist demonstration held in Hope Hall, Liverpool, from which it appears that he announced his intention of resisting Home Rule if necessary by force, and expressed his belief that the British army would refuse to act against Ulster Loyalists ; and whether, in view of the fact that the member for North Armagh is , colonel of the 4th Battalion of Irish Fusiliers, it is intended to take any action in respect to these observa- tions. Mr. McCartan : Might I ask the right hon. gentleman whether he is aware that there is no person in Ulster who believes for a moment that under any circumstances it is the intention of the hon. gentleman to oppose Her Majesty's forces? (Laughter.) Mr. Campbell-Bannerman : I should be sorry to answer for the whole of Ulster. I had not read the speech referred to till my attention was called to it by this question. As is indicated by my hon. friend, the hon. member for North Armagh has two existences ; he is the colonel of a regiment of Irish militia and he is also an Irish member—(laughter)— and an active, if not excited, politician.—(Roars of laughter.) It was solely in the second capacity that he made the speech. My hon. friend will agreed with me that we have long ago learned in this House to guage the value of the exag- gerated language sometimes employed by the hon. and gallant member, and I hardly think special notice need be taken of this particular ebullition. (Loud cheers and renewed laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>MURDERS IN RUSSIA.</head>
        <p>MURDERS IN RUSSIA.</p>
        <p>Capital crimes, mainly in consequence of excessive drinking, seem (says the St. Petersburg correspondent of the" Daily Chronicle ") to he on the increase in Russia. A few days ago an unusually horrible murder, directly traceable to this cause, was committed at Alexandroffsk, a place in the neighbourhood of St. retersburgh, when a drunken working-man in a fit of delirium tremens killed with an axe an old man and three women. An- other working-man recently. on returning home mad with drink, slaughtered several children in a similar manner. An Austrian subject, named Haendel, living at Kieff, has been arrested on the charge of murdering his mistress, a woman named Schilling. It appears that after committing the crime he set fire to his lodgings, in order to obliterate all traces of the tragedy.</p>
