        <p>I.etters to tbe CAL_ THE CHURCHES AND THE LOCAL VETO BILL. Sir,--I have been greatly surprised to note that in our good old town no action has so far been taken either by the Churches, Conformist and Nonconformist, in support of the Government Liquor Traffic (Local Control) Bill. Where are the clergy and ministers ? They are generally supposed to be leaders, and as 311Cil to the forefront in the advocacy of all measures affecting the good of the people. When the " compensation ' proposals ware introduced by the late government,,l remember that the Bev. H. Burson was soot' in evidence leading the crusade, and Runcorn more than once unmistakably pronounced against the proposals. lam now led to ask, have we neither churches nor temperance organizations? or must we be forced to give a decided yes ! to the question so earnestly asked from the pulpit of Bethesda last Sunday evening," Is Christianity played out ?"—Yours, Runcorn, March 17, 1893. VIGILANCE.</p>
        <p>BUDDHISM AND CHRISTIANITY. Sir,—May we not consider the question re Buddhism at an end, as between Messrs. Esmond and Dignum? Is not the subject sufficiently thrashed out? The original contention of Esmond was, that there are many analo- gies in the lives of Buddha and Christ; I think we must all own that it is so,and indeed Mr. Dignum has himself partly acknowledged it, so why lengthen out the discus- sion ? A perusal of "The Light of Asia," which is the life of Buddha in a condensed form, culled from the mass of sacred books, is sufficient evidence in itself, that ana- logies are frequent and obvious: such as the miraculous birth, the temptation, the care for the poor, the aboli- tion of sacrifice, &amp;a. Mr. Dignum, as a staunch Chris- tian, has acknowledged as much as may be expected of him, and whatever the controversy may ultimately develops into, Mr. Esmond's original and intended posi- tion, is unassailable. The question of dates, is a hard and open one. Each party Wi3llC, to say : " Pooh ! You copied and bor- rowed from us." But suppose the dates of the different books—Buddhist and Christian—to be satisfactorily and irrevocably established, the later may not have copied from the aarber. The mind ot man has somewhat the same bent, when the state of civilisation is about equal, and man has groped throughout all ages for " God's right hand in the darkness,' and he has beon wont to lay hold of any- thing tangible, that offered itself as a medium of com- munication, with the Deity. We consider, as Christians that all other parties have been deceived, and of course, smile at their credulity, and never imagine for a mo- ment, that others may smile at ours. Your correspon- dent " Isoa " is In a state of poise : I cannot under- stand a person being in any other state, unless indeed be boldly says : "None of you know ; if there is a Deity, he ha 3 never yet revealed himself." Christians do not know themselves, what to be- lieve, and latterly a great many have given up a belief in hell, which has always been the backbone ef Christianity, for in this case, as "1308" says, we have nothing to be saved from. If Buddha and Christ loved the poor, and, wept for the world, is it wonderful that there are similarities in their lives? Latter-day Bud- dhism may have many black spots, and so, indeed, has ' Christianity, but this is not the fault of Buddha, or . Christ. If our God is the true one, would He not, in His ocnniscence, and as the Essence ol Truth, find means to tell, and convince this great submerged Third, that they are deceived ? If Buddha was a deceiver, or himself deceived, this great mass is not to blame. Ali, no! He has never yet revealed himself, though many have thought he has ; he is unable,unwilling or indifferent ; to the longings of man to know him, lie has not deigned to reply ; to the cry of man for help,has been heard but the echo of his own voice ; to the wail of the widow and orphan for pity, there has been the blank silence of eter- nity. Man is still groping in the darkness, and his only help isles himself.—Yours, &amp;c., SNODGRASS. E'EN IN OUR ASHES LIVE THEM WONTED FIRES. Sir,—The last sessional meeting of the Literary and Philosophical Society was held on Monday evening, and was one of the most interesting and instructive of a good series. The lecturer (Dr. Richmond) fully made out his case of Cremation v. Earth Burial. Decidedly is crema- tion better than burial in the crowded populous centres we find in Lancashire, Middlesex, fic. False sentiment and superstition alone stand in the way of its general adoption. The lecturer dealt with cremation in its religious aspect. I Wd3 much amused by his quoting, the bishops in favour of this very necessary sanitary reform. It is really too, too absurd, though, to see the pickle these dignitaries are in when brought face to face with questions of this sort. Too wise to attempt to brand the coming custom as unchristian, they, as an alternative, throw overboard all logical consistency and declare that cremation is not imcoinpatible with bodily</p>
        <p>resurrection ! Nor indeed is it mora. than the present disgusting custom of allowing our dead to rot in heaps. To those who have ever so little knowledge of the chemistry of organic decomposition,both are equally des- tructive of the idea of bodily resurrection, and the general acceptance of cremation will he the death knell of this essentially christian dogma. The incompatibility of these ideas with this custom will then become evident even to the congregations. Now I d ,n't see why a man, because he has been a nuisance all the days of his life, should elect to be one also after death. Personally I should much prefer cremation to the privilege of rotting with my fellows even in consecrated ground. And Dr. White was very funny on the sentimental side of the question. He was great on Gray and other poets and led us to infer that he would prefer to be coffined rather than inturned. But for sentimental symbolization which would the good Dr. prefer for tombstone decoration a coffin or a cinary urn ? Finally, compulsory cremation has as much to justify it as compulsory vaccination.—Yours, !sc., JOIN PEERS. "The Haws," Penkqh, Lancashire, March 22, 1893. TIIE RECTOR AND UNITARIANISM.</p>
        <p>Sir.-1 am stirs every Warringtonian will give the Rector credit for sincerity and courtesy in giving "In- struction on dissent," and I am not inclined to enter into any theological controversy upon points of doctrine where no agreement is possible. If the /Lector is content to rest upon the authority of the fathers and ecclesiasti- cal councils for his religious beliefs, I do not see that anyone has any right whatever to find fault with him or call him hard names, or even to say positively that he is in error. But when he ventures to pronounce that those who differ from him are in awful error, I would ask him by what authority he ventures to condemn others for a mere difference of opinion upon matters that rest en- tirely upon oral tradition for their truthfulness for a period of 50 to 70 years at least, and the documents upon which these aasulaptions are founded are based upon the same traditional sources. Will he venture to say that, Unitarians are not so capable as Churchmen of silting evidence,and forming an opinion upon any matter sub- mitted to them for consideration? I fully admit the Rector's right to assert his own belief, but not to con- demn another's as an awful error, and I hope I am charitable enough to believe that his will regret the re- , mark that Unitarians are "Christian in a aense, because they professed to he Christian." As a Unitarian I say we should deserve to be called " professing" Christians only, if we were uncharitable enough to deny to others the Christian name simply on the ground of a difference of opinion about Christ. The Rector, I think, made s mistake when he said Unitarians worshipped Reason. From my experience all they claim is that both Reason and Conscionee shall be brought to the consideration of religious beliefs, the one to prevent imposture, and the other to assert what they believe to be Truth. AS to the truthfulness of their position, history will speak. They are content even to be hated if their testitnony furthers the cause of Truth. Their teachings are leavening the churches and broadening out Christian doctrine until it is difficult in some churches to distinguish where Orthodoxy ends and Unitarianism begins. The future is theirs. In my own lifetime many doctrines that were taught as Orthodox Truth have been given up in favour of a more Christian belief • and it is only a question of time how long the Orthodox belief in the doctrine of the atonement will last. Fifty years ago, Orthodoxy and the Church of England was content to rest doctrinally upon the Bible and the Prayer Book, but the Church of England is more and more resting upon the authority of the church and the fa hers and looking more and more to priestly ordinances, rites, and ceremonies. If the Lenten lectures at the Parish Church have done nothing else, they have disclosed the tendency of the Church of England to Romish doctrine. Romish prac- tices will follow as surely as the darkness follows the light, and dissent will have to fight both if they are to preserve their civil and religious freedom. The aggres- siveness of the Church of England clergy in educational matters, the sacerdotalism of her orders, the open attempt to aubititute the authority of Convocation for the authority of Parliament, are all evidences of her growing power vrhieh can only be restrained by Discs- tablishment and Disondownient of the spoils of the Roman Church in the reign of Henr the y faithfully, &amp;b.—Yours F. MONKS. Walton Old Hall, Warrington, March 24, 1893. -- TILE PROGRESS OF CHRISTIAN MISSIONS IN IN DI A. " Blind unbelief Is sure to err And scan His work in vain." Fir,—There is very little which can be called new in the boasted discoveries of modern writers on the scienca of religion. Ancient literatures, other than Hindu, abounding with due moral sentiments, arid great spiritual truths, have beets familiar in Christendom for ages past. But for the peculiarities of the gospel which , Christ has given to the world,. we may Irk in vain else- where, and certainly not in the ancient religions of India. Hear what the great Sanscrit scholar, Sir Monier Whims, has to say upon this subject. Addressing a Church Missionary Society in London, Some few years ago, he said.' Non-cliriAtian scriptures are all develop. ments in a wrong direction ; they bugin with some Illshes of the true light, and end in darkness. Listen to me, youthful students of so-called &amp;term' books of the East : Search them through and through, and tell me, (la they affirm of Zoroaster, of Confucius, a Buddha, what our Bilk affirms of the Founder cf Christianity—</p>
        <p>"That He,a sinless Man, was made sin—not merely that He is the eradicator of sin, but that He, the Sm of Man was Himself made ltsin. It stands alone, wholly un- paralleled. Once again, ye youthful students of the so- called sacred books of the East,search Wein through and through, and tell me, do they affirm of Z noaster, of Confucius, of Buddha, what our Bible affirms of the Founder of Christianity, that Ile, a dead and buried Man was made Life, not merely that He is the giver of life, but that He is Life, and that " Except ye eat the flesh of the Son of Man, and drink His blood, ye have no life in you r Again I say it is absolutely unique, and I defy you to produce the shade of the shadow of a simi- lar declaration in any other sacred book of the world. And bear in mind that these two matchless, unparalleled declarations are indissolubly connected with the great central facts and doctrines of our religion—the Incarna- tion, the Crucifixion, the Resurrection and the Ascen- sion of Christ." Again, he says, speaking of these sacred books of the East, " We ask every missionary to study their contents, an d thankfully lay hold of whatso- ever things are true and of good report in them—but we warn him that there can be no greater mistake than to force these non-christian bibles into conformity with some scientific theory of development, and then point to the Cliestian's Holy Bible as the crowning product of religious evolution. I have quoted these passages from an authority which few will dare to gainsay, for the purpose of emphasising two special points. 1. That our gospels have a mes- sage for the world of the most vital import, and which no other religion can pretend to ; and (2) that it is the merest dream of modern theorists to imagine that the religion of Christ is only the natural outcome or develop- ment front the world systems which preceded it. It is on the ground of this unique importance and Christ's special command, to go forth into the whole world and disciple all nations, that the churches find their justification in missionary efforts. Difficulties, even the most stupen- dousoire tribe no obstacles, and any apparent want of success can only be met by the Iron Duke's reminder to the despondent curate " attend to your marching orders." But it is well that we should sift what truth there may be in the statement of modern scoffers at what they call the little success of Christian missions in India. It is to be feared that some people don't trouble. themselves much about the actual success of missions in India, and certainly they don't estimate very carefully the elements which enter into sucePss. In the month of May last year the churches were celebrating the 100th anniversary of William Carey's first mission. This devoted servant of Christ laboured semi years or more before lie made a single convert. But what a convert that was, a high class Brahman, of immense learning, rejoic- ing in the name of Chrishna-Pal, a prophetic omen surely of how certainly the deluded followers of the " sweet shepherd god ' are destined sooner or later, to be led captives by the religion of the Cross. Carey's work was necess rily slow, but no statistics can represent the success of those first seven or more years labours ; for after savarestudy and toil, he gave to the world his translation of the New Testament in Ben- gales, there being at that time in all India, but one version of the Scriptures in 'Famul. This event issued in a momentous advance for the cause of missions, arid the earnest pioneer was speedily appointed to the professor- ship of Begalee, Sanscrit and Maliratta languages in the College of Fort William ; " a circumstance which greatly augmented his means of usefulnes as a translator of the Scriptures; for learned natives crowded from all parts of India to the college, and through their instrumenta- lity, Dr. Carey saw all the learning, and almost all the languages of India placed within his reach." (Bags- tar's Bible of Every Land, p. 110.) So successful were the labours of this one devoted Christian missionary, that before his death the entire Scriptures, or portions of them, had been translated into forty languages or dialects of that vast country:. . .</p>
        <p>Now it requires no great e-flort of mind to grasp largely the meaning of this one fact. The word of God in those scriptures is the divine seed, which, despite all croak- ings to the contrary, is as surely uprooting Braliamanism as the first preachings of that same word overturned the deep-seated paganism of ancient Rome. Only, be sure of this, that in comparison, the progre,s will be of a vastly accelerated character. The spirit of prosecution is assuredly not dead in India hut it can never attaiu the dimensions of the persecution of Christians in the first three centuries of our era ; and the magnified appli- ances of our present civilization with the advance un- der Christian influences already attained give promise of future successes—scarcely imagined in any previous age. Confining the view then to this one special agency of Christian missions—the circulation of the Bible in India during the present century—it is not beyond the truth to say that Brahmsnism has been gradually sapped at its foundations, and that under the stimulating in- fluence ef western thought its devotees have been fed to the researches and study of their own sacred books. But with what result ? Tlear what one says who knew India wwll. " The Hindus in ever-increasing numbers have now an opportunity which their ancestors never had of inspecting the foundations of their faith,of discrimin- ating between the perishable and the abiding. Hinduism has been a kingdom of darkness, and for the first time its intricate chambers have been penetrated through and through by light. Its composition ba-s been ruthlessly exposed. Its institutions, instead of inspiring the awe of the worshipper, excite the curiosity of the Hindu stu- dent, who is now perceiving that its sacred books are not revelations, that its chronology is not history, that its account of natural phenomena is not scionce,and that . .</p>
        <p>Its laws aro not relrgion. Whatever its disciples may affirm, Hinduism is slowly bocomirg a relic ; in other words, it is losing its hold of the mii,l of the country ; the youth of India are slipping away from Its grasp. To the busy or superficial sight this intellectual revolution is not apparent, because a Hindu may repudiate the authority of Hinduism, and deem it expedient to respect its social obligations, and this respect is its apparent conservation. The new life of the people will abide for the present in the ancient structures of usage ; but it will make for itself other habitations, in which its libe- rated vigour will have room for play, and its old homes will then become tenantless, and be ready to vanish away." These choice and powerful words were expressed in a paper read by the Rev. E. E. Jenkins, secretary of the Wesleyan Missionary Society 14 years ago, at the Mildmay Conference on foreign missions,a famous Hindu scholar Sir William Muir,being in the chair at the time. But I am trespassing, Mr. Editor, on your space, and have not yet come to the positive and manilest proofs which the churches are supplying of the triumphs of the gospel In India. This, by your permission, I reserve for another letter, when the ideas so beautifully expressed in Mr. Jenkin's paper will, I think, appear to he rapidly fulfilling tacts, and that the "old homes" of lirahmanism are fast " becoming tenantless " and " ready to vanish away."--yours truly, Penketh, 23rd March, 1893.</p>
        <p>JOHN DIGNUM.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS CHARGES AGAINST WAR-</p>
        <p>RINGTON BAILIFFS. At the Warrington Borough Court, on Monday, William Henry Once, 36, Wellington-st., was charged with having, between the 3rd and 9th ult., falsely endorsed a summons issued by the Warring- ton County Court, defendant being then a bailiff at that court.—Mr. Buckton prosecuted and Mr. Arthur Browne defended.—Mr. Buckton said that the charge was brought under the 78th section of thesiwnunitdi ntenoCnmerisidmn witnesses, to as iwtCaaa aosso usrafirtoi uarAebdai summonsl;et to otimayai anpnwsp(wtihe Thomasa,al (cars pr, 40ai rwnsh Hitt making a false punishable tho) tolroeri elaeyisyl.lp.norenrisejisue. of r:Tielle hyl and defendant sent the Whilst the etinirdnourgsehmrmostt,wwashit,ch, was not a legal service, the effect that it had been served on Chorley'a wife. This was found out next day, and a fresh summons Was served later.—Mr. Harrison, high bailiff, Identified de- fendant's handwriting on the endorsement, and In answer to Mr. Arthur Browne said that notice was Rent to both plaintiff and defendant before the County Court trial came off. Witness said that since the above date, he had discharged defendant.—Mr. Browne said that he would like it to be known that this prosecution had not been ordered by the County Court judge, and he con- tended that as the summons had been served on both the plaintiff and defendant before the Court day, no offence had been done. He objected to a remand. The Case was remanded until Thursday, bail being granted. Philip Arthur Naylor, 3, Benniqt-at., was charged on a similar offence between 9tl, and 16th ult.—Mr. Buck- ton said that this was a judgment summons and was alleged to have been served pernonally, but defendant who was called said that he had never received it.—Mr. Harrison identified defendant's handwriting, and said that he had discharged him sins the time of the alleged offence. Questicned by Mr. Browns, witness said that defendant had been his coachman. He paid him 65., a week as a coachman, but