      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>THE -</p>
        <p>THE "NEW LIBERA.LIBM." • ADDRESS BY MIL A. H. CRASFIELD. The last meeting of the Warrington Dabating Society's Session was held on Diuraday night in the Masonic Hall. There was an exceptionally large gathering, which was no doubt the result of the announcement that Mr. A. 11. Croefleld Would Open a debate upon the "New Liberalism." The Chair was taken by Mr. Ilulse. Mr. A. H. CHO:MELD who was greeted with loud applause and cheers upon rising said :—When I was invited to deliver an address to this Society and had the temerity to accept that honour, it was suggested that the new Home Rule Bill would be a most fitting subject for my paper, but it occurred to me that the New Liberalism would be even better, and a sort of compli• ment to the nese Conservatism, on which topic I had just had the pleasure of listening to Mr. Arthur Bennett. My choice lay between these two and in contemplating them I felt like the mariner of old approaching the Straits of Messina, that there was difficulty on either hand—a cer- tain difficulty as to time. It has been said of Mr. Bright, I believe by competent critics, that he has made speeches which it would be hamossiblo to improve by I altering or striking out a single word or sentence, but even Mr. Bright with all his great power of concentration must have failed to deal exhaustively with the pros and cons of the various provisions of the Home Rule Bill, had he attempted to do so in the short space of half an hour or so. You may say that to treat in any sense exhaustively the other subject in that brief period, is still has pos- sible. That I cannot deny, but it has one advantage. It may at the present moment present some features of novelty, which any general observations that one can possibly make on the question of Home Role Lira society like this could hardly possess. So it struck me that as the general arguments on Home Rule are so familiar, not to say thread-bare and stale, the best plan would be to face the more ambitious subject. As to the only way in which it can be treated within reasonable limits of time, a comparison that has been drawn between Mr. Gladstone's and Mr. Bright'a methods will illustrate that. lam not sure whether Mr. Bright himself did not draw the comparison saying that while he was content in dealing with a subject to dwell upon the leading points and features, touching only so to speak the great promotories and entering the large bays, Mr. Glad-stone explored every inlet and creek. It must be my endeavoui to confine myself to the main features, entering only upon detail insofar as it is necesaary to do so to avoid vague- ness and uncertainty on essential points. Then what is the MAIN FEATURE Or THE NEW LIBERALISM ? That is the important question which I shall try to answer, but perhaps it may be well to state first what It has in common with the u!ci. As to that, there is of course no question. The time honoured raison d'etre of Liberalism remains and will remain the same. Its fun- damental purpose endures in reform ; reform by which the institutions and law are kept in harmony with the requirements and best ultimate interests of the people. And here at the very outset, I am compelled itaurder to escape the charge of talking mere platitudes to give in scone detail, an illustration, not I think without practi- cal interest to us in Warrington. I refer to the QUESTION OS' ROTALTINS and in the following facts, which I think are substanti- ally correct, though I am not in a position actually to guarantee them, I will first show the effect these may have on trade. My facts are taken from an account ,d the state of trade in Workington a little more than a year since ; in which the writer says :—" For many months Workineton has been in a pitiful state. Last August some 5,000 men were out of work, and the chil- dren were crying in the streets for bread. Evan now,after many men have been sent away to other iron districts, there are 3,000 out of work. Over £lOO a week is paid by the town in relief, and two of the largest factories— steel-rail works, are standing idle. Why? One master, I see, attributes all the trouble to trades unionism. But this is simply ridiculous. According t) Mr. P. Walls,the secretary of the Blast-furnaceinen's Union, trades-union- ism has not raised the price of pig iron 81 a ton. The present selling price of pig iron is 48a. a ton, and in flush times 733. a ton. Unionism is therefore only an- swerable for a fractional increase, and when it is con- sidered that the cost of the blast-furnaceman's work per ton of pig iron is 2s. 31., and that the charge per ton for royalties is 53. 31., I think I need say no more to prove that the mei, are in no wise answerable for a state of things by which they are the greatest sufferers. On the other hand,l ain pleased to find that the ironinasters are not culpable. The profits of the ironmaster are very small at present, and in flush times, when the price of iron is higher,his profits are even less. Who is to blame ? Mr. Valentine, formerly a managing director of a large ironworks and an ex-M.P. attributes much of this trouble to foreign tariffs, lie says that the markets of I Russia, Germany, Austria. France, the French colonies, and the United States of America are practically closed against British iron, and those of Spain aud Italy are partially closed, while Canada is expected to follow. That is one of the evils of Protection. But it is also true that pig iros is imported and that iron ore is im- ported to England from Belgium and Spain if not from other places. This, of course, helps to keep Cumberland pig iron out of the home market, and so adds te the stagnation and distress at Workington. Now, how is it that while we are shut out of all those foreign markets, we can be undersold in our own? The following table is the most concise and conclusive answer to this ques- tion : Cost of royalties per ton of manufactured pig iron. . Cleveland 3-8 Scotland 8-0 Cumberland 8-3 Germany 0-8 France 0-8 Belgium 1-3 And in the three foreign countries, the royalties go to the State. In England they go into the pockets of pri- vate persons. 'The total royalties paid in 1888 on coal alone was £8,000,000. Lord Lonsdale is said to draw £130.000 a year in royalties from one mine. Mr. Wharton, of Cleveland, draws £l5O a day ; Lord Zetland draws £lOO a day. The Duke of Hamilton draws £3OO a day. Lord Lonadale actually levies a charge on. one of the Workington ironworks for water taken from the river Derwent." These are the essential facts, I won't trouble you with further particulars or attempt to forcast the conclusions at which we shall arrive on the matter, say- sing one. As to how far circumstances may warrant us In reducing these royalties, or in what cases it may be justifiable ultimately to cancel them, or what length of notice should be given, those are questions which cannot be decided until all the facts have been thoroughly in- vestigated, (Applause) hut there is ONE GREAT PRINCIPLE, Which will I believe, be the dividing line between the r Liberalism and Conservatism of to-day, just as it would have been 25 or 50 years ago. I have heard only two proposals made as yet on the Conservative side to meet such an impasse as that I have described. One is the sliding seals, by which amounts short paid in bad times should be repaid in good times, no reduction being made Is the average payment. The other is simply direct compensation in full from the State. There we join issue. We cannot listen to either proposals, nor shall we agree to accept the principle of the endowment in res- pect of these royalties any more that; Mr. Goschen's proposal for the endowment of licenses. Whenever we come to deal with this question on Radical lines there will be a deafening chorus of Tories denouncing us for spoliation in their well known style, and yet for such legislation, we have pre- cedents, which will hold good as such, going a pod way back. 'fake the abolition of protection on corn. IleSidel giving the people cheaper food, this has hail not a little to do with the present low value of land. Have the landlords received compensation? If excep- tion is taken to this illustration because the repeal of the corn laws was opposed by the Conservative party, I must point out that this measure has since had the sanction and approval of the most intelligent section of that party, who surely have never suggested that the country ought to have been taxed to recompense the original landlords, or those who had chosen to assume that the corn laws were a permanent institution, and who on the strength of that assumption had invested their earnings in real property in land. There is a more recent precedent still. In 1882 Gladstone's Land Bill establishing courts where tenants could get their rents fixed bylaw, came into force. • This, I need hardly say, was denounced by the Tories. A tale I heard the other day, shows what they,regardless of the urgencies of the case thought of this government interference with priv- ate ownership In land. During one of the Debates that year Mrs. Gladstone was sitting in the ladies' gallery of the Housed Commons and noticed a purse on the floor which she picked up, and presented to a hativ next to her:J*oll'49llm hoped it was well filled. "No," said the lady, a wife of sonee great land owner in Ireland, your I husband has taken good care to empty it. Perhaps Mrs; I Gladstone rather kid herself open to the smart rejoinder but, if so, her husband is not the only culprit. What happened in 'B7 ? Lord Salisbury in 1888, when Mr. Parnell introduced his Bill for a revision of the rents owing to the state of the sericulture' population, said that to interfere with the judicial rents would be abso- lutely dishonest. We know what followed in the winter and how, when a great deal of mischief had been done, Lord Salisbury reversed his opinion in 1887 and was forted to interfere In the way he had previously con- demned. And now, with the continued appreciation of gold and the continued tall in prices of silver, you are face to face with the fact, failing the adoption of legisla- tive measures to bring about by increased demand for It, the appreciation of silver and depreciation of gold in terms of it and a general rise In prices—failing this there will again have to be it reduction in the rent, propor- tionate to the disastrous and progressive decline in the Table of Agricultural produce. Are these not precedents? So far as precedenta count for anything, do they not warrant us in upholding liurke's maxim that, shonid they come Into sharp and gerbille conflict, the iiitereata of the State are greater than, and are prior to, any pri- vate title or charter that exists. If in case of real tieces- city, you are thus free to deal with the surface value of the land, are you to be prohibited from dealing with the treasure of unknown and once in a large degree 11n5,14. pl'ard values below the settler ? We hold that the opposite is the case, and when this reform comes on. We</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>DAY.</head>
        <p>DAY.</p>
        <p>shall in reality he amending one of those old customs and olisolete laws, whieh, if suited to an early stage 0 civil- isation are certainly not in harmony with the needs of our great industrial age. And we shall be carrying out the principles and poticy of the old school who under- took the great reforms by which they cancelled much of the pernicious legislation passed in the day when landlords ruled the roast, and freed our trade and com- mercial enterprise from the shackles and impedi- ments which hampered them. We can hardly over-estimate the value, the permanent value, of the work done by these trien—men like Bright and Cobden and Villiers, and Gladstone. Theirs was the</p>
        <p>task of emancipation: On us fells the labour Of—c4;h: struction, AIM] therein we find the dividing lime, which, in Some respects separates new liberalism fromold. By no means entirely even in this respect,, for in both you find emancipation and construction at work,, and insep- erably interwoven. But that which used to he the great feature of Liberal legislation has now given place to its positive rival. It was the aim and ettect of the last generation to give free play to INDIVIDUALISM. In ours, we have the duty of maintaining that policy,. but subject to such increasing qualifications' and gov- ernmental activity as may be needed to counteract the • bad results which follow the system of pule lisi•se:- faire. Ido not mean, that thero is going to bs any sud- den change in the policy of the Liberal party. These things are the work of years. The influence and doc- trines of one generation merge into those of the next and for new Liberalism we have precedents going a long way back. A striking instance is the _ten hours bill for _ .</p>
        <p>factories t., whicil as we all know Mr. Bright was oppos- ed, just as lie was also opposed at one time to trades unions, as interfering, with freedom of contract and hence with individual freedom. 1(10 not quote Mr. Blight as having been in all respects an extreme individualist,but he belonged to the Manchester school whose doctrines of non-interference were carried in certain directions to an extreme which cannot now be entertained. This sort of freedom at any price which is now being, preached to us by a great authority whose arguments I will refer' to in a moment, will not do. Mr. Chamberlain has</p>
        <p>described it in the f.llowing passage, where he refers to the " individualists " whose doctrines now somewhat discredited, were once the stock-in-trade of the Liberal party. " According to these gentlemen, writes Mr. . . . .</p>
        <p>freedom of comrnerce shoUld have its</p>
        <p>e-ompliment in freedom of labour. Restriction to indi- vidual liberty are to be removed except in so far' as they are absolutely necessary to protect the liberty of otl:er individuals. Freedom to get drunk, freedom to con-</p>
        <p>tract and even spread disease, freodo'm to he ignorant, and to keep childten in ignorance, are all sacred rights to be carefully preserved ; and even freedom to decline taxation has been suggested by an extreme adherent of the principle. There may be freedom to combine, and there must be freedom for time smallest minority to defeat</p>
        <p>the objects of the most beneficent .combination. While</p>
        <p>the LiGeral party is by no means in entire agreement as to how far it is justified in interfering with the individ- .. . . . .</p>
        <p>nal freedom thus described by Mr. -Chamberlain, to a certain extent our constructive legislation has interfered and must interfere with it. The high authority to whom I have just referred, the uncosipromising opponent of any interference of that kind, the greatest representa- tive of individualism is of course Mr. Herbert Spencer. Our illustrious and stately philosopher impresses en us the fact that soeiety is is natural growth, and that with it as suck, you cannot surely interfere. The Government</p>
        <p>has no right to interfere excepting in to matters with which it would by gener.tl consent bit empowered to deal. lie enumerates these as foll.,ws :-1. Defence _ _</p>
        <p>. _ against invasion :2. Prevention and punishment of crime and everything by which a man makes himself a nui- sance to his neighbours ; 3. the enforcement of contract. Mr. Buckle gave us substantially the same definition 40 years ago in his " History of Civilisation," but if we accept Mr. Spencer's conclusion, who is to decide how far there would be a general assent as to the limits to be placed on the power of Government, and where we shall be if anyone who considers that the limits have been exceeded is to be at liberty to ignore the law. If that principle which at me of our friends in Ulster seem to favour is tube adopted, will Irish landlords he justified in repudiating the decision of the land courts, or to take a less striking example, manufacturers in ignoring the increased strin,gency of Mr. Asquitti's Employers' Liabi- lity Act. however much we may sympathise with individual freedom, we must admit that such an inter- pretation is beyond the bound of reason, and that in practice Mr. Spencer's theory, to put it mildly, would not work. But he raises another objection to our con- structive policy, namely, that it involves taxation of those who have proved themselves most fitted to sur- Vice for the benefit of those who are least fit. For this he says there is sanction neither in nature nor in equity. Mr. Spencer shows that contrary to what we must cer- tainly regard as the very extraordinary opinion of Pro- fessor Jevons and Mr. Matthew Arnold that there was no such thing as abstract</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>!OMIT TO FROPROTT</head>
        <p>!OMIT TO FROPROTT</p>
        <p>he shows that this right called in German jurisprudence Naturrecht "bas an absolute and indestructable found- i ' ation which may be traced back to the earliest customs of primitive societies, lie shows hi opposition to Ben- them, that this right to private property is not • creation by law, but one existing prior to and indepen- dent of Government. To quote one 3r two examples from the many proofs he gives, take the following :—" Now among the customs which we thus find to be pre. governmental and which subordinate governmental power when it is established, are those which recognise certain individual rights—rights to act in certain ways and possess certain things. Even where the recognition of property is least developed, there is proprietorship of weapons, tools and personal ornaments ; and generally, the recognition goes far beyond this. Among such North American Indians as the Snakes, who are without government, there is private ownership of horses. By the Chippewayans," who have no regular government," game taken in private traps " is considered as private property." Kindred facts concerning huts, utensils, and other personal belongings, might be brought in evidence from accounts of the Ahts, the Comanches, the Esqui- maux and the Brazilian Indians. Among various uncivilised people, custom has estahlished the claim to the crop grown on a cleared plot of ground, though not to the ground itself; and the Todas, who Sr. wholly %without political organisation, make a like distinction between ownership of cattle and of land. Kolffs' state- ment respecting" the peaceful Arefuras "well sums up the evidence. They " recognise the right of property in the fullest sense of the word, without there being any (other) authority among them than the decision of their elders, according to the customs of their forg- athers." Thence Mr. spencer's conclusion that the rights of property subordinate governmental power, to Use his expresion. Ills further argu- ment against constructive policy is based os analogies with the animal kingdom. Ile reminds us that the sickly the malformed and the least fleet or powerful, are weeded out by the purifying process of fighting, so that all vitiation of the race, and the multiplications of its inferior samples is prevented, and the maintenance of a constitution completely adapted to surrounding conditions and therefore most productive of happiness Is insured. Ile says none can fail to see that If reward always were great in proportion as desert was small, fatal results to the society would quickly follow; and if so then even a partial intrusion of the family repme into tbe regime of the State, will be slowly followed by !Mal results. And so without going into further detail he arrives at the conclusion that Inisies .faire, or what is sometiines called "survival of the fittest and devil take I the hindmost" is the only safe plan and that construc- tive Liberalism is wrong and means progressive degra- dation. These extraets, I have taken from Mr. Spencer's popular treatise entitled "the Man versus the State." I regret that time does not admit of more than this very Inadequate summary of liii views. What are we to say in reply. We agree with him AS to the immemorial and indestructible right to private property. We agree with him that undue meddiesomeness on the part of govern- ment leads to results that are often equally unforeseen and equally mischievioua. I dare say most of us have at one time or another had to mend a log fire. You see a bright spot or two in front and apply the pipe of %/alit bellows to it, and presently are able to create a little flame that flickers up but dies out as soon as you stop. The log is large and damp and does not lend itself to your purpose but while blowing away you have created cur- rents of air arid produced quite unlooked for results in other quarters, where the fire is beginning to blaze up in another part and probably at the back, s, that most of the heat goes up the chimney consuming good fuel with- out giving you the warmth you require. Such has often , been the result of ASIDDLIISOUII LAM SLATION</p>
        <p>as the individualists, and with good reason, are con- stantly insisting. Therefore Mr. Spencer's compact and instructive work comes as a salutary reminder, and even . wing to any who may favour hasty and extensive developments of State, Socialism or any of the other isms we are now asked to consider. It will make us none the Ws chary of what in the expression adopted by NW Cleveland is termed " paternalism "—viz. the adoption of ' any policy which may excite among any section of the people expectations of direct material benefit in return ' for nothinr,. Rut when Mr. Spencer urges In reply that free education and free libraries are in direct opposition to this view and condemns them as unmitigated pater- nalism, then WOCAII only reply that we must accept them and even further developmenta as such, and as excep- tions to the rule. And in rejecting them, we must frankly tell hiin that he rides his hobbies or theories to death. Gentlemen, In this treatise from which I have quoted there is an important declaration. There is, can's Nip. spencer, the Indisputable fact that each hutnan being is in a certain degree modifiable both physically and mentally. Every theory of education, every dis- cipline from that of the .arithmeticinn to that of the priza fighter, every proposed reward for virtue or punish- ment for vice, implies the belief embodied In sundry proverbs, that the use l)r distis.e of each faculty bodily or mental, is followed by an adaptive change in it IoM of power or gain of power accordfng to demand." " There is the fact, also, in Its broader manifestations univerailly recognised, that modifications in one way or other produced are inheritable. No one denies that by the accumulation of smelt changes, generation after gen- eration, cimstitutien Ats itself to conditions." Does not this admission undeerninf; the whole fahric et his adverse critie.ion ? The grant fact lie here admits, is ititerwon with the whole terure of A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>SATU</head>
        <p>SATU</p>
        <p>fAAMP</p>
        <p>AR</p>
        <p>25, p 1893.</p>
        <p>PROGRESSIVE POLICY. We do not wish or intend to make rewards great ie. pro-1 portion as desert ig small. We do not aim in the near or distant future for that absolute equality which even were it not clearly opposed to the laws and fact•f nature would be, whether in respect of worldly 'goods or the varioas faculties or capacities of mind and body, &amp;111, flat, and uninteresting, and therefore hardly loss desir- able than impossible. But what we do aim for is that at least a chance of fair reward should not be denied to merit or capacity whether actual or so far as we can apply the principle, potential. That is the justification for our free education and libraries. That is why under an improved poor-law and in addition to the establish--1 ment of a competent and energetic labour bureau, we would give as in Germany a few nights shelter without charge to a destitute workman in search of employment and without inflicting on him the taint of pauperism or the ignominy of the causal ward. That of itself would justify us under such a reformed law, in subjecting to a severer discipline than at present the loafer and vaga- bond, though even here reformatories should as far as possible be curative and corrective, I mean as regards cases which are not hopelessly incorrigible. That is to a large extent, though far indeed from exclusively, the raison rears of this movement in favour of recreation</p>
        <p>_ for young and old inside and surely also reasonable access to the same in many cases outside cities if the infinite beauty and healthy pleasure of mountain and</p>
        <p>--.... moor are not to remain always a closed book to so many of our people. I read some months since extracts from a book—l forget the title of it—in which it was stated that close to the wealthiest warehouses in New York, thererwere to be found tenements where human beings lived crowded to the extent of ever 500 to the acre. The thickest population in Manchester is, if I remember . . .</p>
        <p>rightrsom-e 'Olt°. the acre. Possibly the height of building may have something to do with this, but as to the actual conditions in which the people live, we may gather a conception from one sentence where the writer stated that the Mr breathed inside the house, was more . .</p>
        <p>foul, if we Gould see it, than the gutters that ran by them. something like 80 per cent, of the crime of New York is traced, so it is stated, to these quarters. Will Mr. Spencer deny that life under such conditions whether in New York or in the sweaters' dens in Lon- don must lead to progressive degradation ef body and soul. Can he find us any parallel in natural history for the tremendous inequality in environment and condition —i.e. in its widest sense the inequality of epportunity— given to individuals of the same species living within a stone's throw of rash other. His answer may be that remedial efforts should be left to the altruistic activity of private indi•iduals. There we go a long way with him. Of scope for private benevolence there will los enough and to spare, and much may be safely under- taken by private individuals which is beyond the range of public authorities. But we have plain proof that in themselves the altruistic forces are insufficient as this sole supplement to his policy of laissez faire. Such</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>_INEOZAZATY OP CONDITiON</head>
        <p>_INEOZAZATY OP CONDITiON</p>
        <p>as I have just desciibed,if lett to develop unchecked, the miseries that result from it must prove as large a source of danger as of evil in modern civilization. We are well aware, that In the operation of the law by which the fittest survive, and by which nature eliminates the worthless—the average standard of the rest is maintained and raised, but what Mr. Spencer seems to us to ignore, is the fact that we may reduce to an extent upon which it is impossible to place a limit the numbar of those who require to be thus eliminated. This in lie nature of things, can only be done by degrees, possibly by slow degrees. But supposing that Miss Octavia ! Hills' efforts and pleas for more spaces in the crowded ! parts of London for more light and sunshine let us say in Tower Hamlets are successful ; or supposing art inducement were offered some day to the working classes to live more of them outside cities, instead of crowding already over-crowded centres, as might be done by subsidies to Railways Company's enabling them to run morning and evening trains at absolutely mini- mum rates. Will any one deny that each oh such under- takings is calculated to modify in the right direction, both physically and mentally the people affected by them. I would not ask such a question, except as an PI,CCUMI to answer one last objection raised by Nlr.Spencer, that in this way we are going to add to the burden of taxation and rates. That may be true, but then we intend the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>BURDENS TO REST ON BIGHT MOULDERS,</head>
        <p>BURDENS TO REST ON BIGHT MOULDERS,</p>
        <p>While some important measures for the public good have involved and will involve increased public expense, we have already reached a 'point where the small capitalist struggling for narrow margins of profits, the man working for x moderate salary the unfortunate class compelled by circumstances to maintain a genteel appearance on small means. when these people are carrying a load quite sufficiently heavy. For this large aection of the community the income tax instead of be- ing increased, should grow gradually small and beauti- fully less. Then we come to the only solution ot the difficulty. We must go in for</p>
        <p>0 RADCATXD TAXATION.</p>
        <p>It must come soon. This principle already ad.ipted in the case of the death duties in Canada and parts of Switzerland and Australia, and for aught I know else- where, must find a great and important development in this country and the United States. If Mr. Spencer or anyone else imply that such would be spolia- tion or injustice, we must decline to admit anything of the sort. Advanced Liberals are some- times spoken of a little harshly in twisty. We are called communists- and revolutionists or other things as had or worse. We can afford to let these misnomers, which lam bound to say are not usually applied in I earnest, pass be. We may not see our way to place before reform of royalties, or the liquor traffic, tho enormous,if not insuperable impediments which modern Conservatism would fain create. Not that we are all extremists. We may be willing that under a reform of the licensing law—which would:have plaesed some of us a great deal better than the proposed but impracticable Veto Bill—the public should trar the expenses involved in changing the premises of a public-house so as to make it suitable for some other branch of trade. But the ex- cessjve claims whether of landlords who draw royalties, or brewers with tied houses, we will resist and we flail not resist in vain. We may think it high time also to deal, as, mind you, competent lawyers have decided it possible to deal equitably and adequately, with the un- earned increment which has gone on long enough. But we individual, whether not going to take the property of a single i whether it be personality or reality, and whether n England or in Ireland without equitable compensation. We do not adopt in taxation the principle of graduation in any spirit of lhostility to the man who makas a for- tune, but we maintain that lie owes the greatest duty, all things considered, to the state, and that it will be quite possible, to put his principle in practice without depriving individuals of one single legitimate incentive to energy, thrift, enterprise and even speculative enter- prise, which equally with the rest is essential to our wel- fare in the world wide competition of nations. And now we have considered the last objection raised by the individualista, sad in answering or endeavouring to answer it, I bring my protracted paper to a close. I have tried to give some illustrations of the</p>
        <p>NY.CErITT FOR CONSTRUCT! VR 118555 LISM and some of its purposes, but as to the exact direction and extent to awhich It may be applied, it is impossible to speak definitely. Whilst repudiating any wholesale extension of State Socialism, such as you see in the monopolies—on the whole, we may, I think, say the ex- cellent monopolis--of the post office and the telegraph, there will probably be some gradual extension in this direction, and an increase in the way of corporate un- dertaking and responsibilities will assuredly be thrown on representative local bodies as time goes on. But we cannot forecast the future. We cannot foresee the solution of all the pmblems that confront us. They are difficult problems, and no more to be settled by the penny a lining of cheap philanthropy than that sneering anobbism, personified for instance, in what John Bright enCe termed the "Saturday Reviler." There 15 DO royal road open to Us, by the instruction of science and experience, by the careful sifting of each proposal, perhaps even as Lord Rosebery says by some experi- mental legislation, by these means alone shall we he able to arrive at the settlement of our problems. whether of currency or labour, or drink or poverty. There may be rocks afield and new shiftingsin the centres of political power. There must arise difference of opinion amongst the progressive party both as to the method and the degree to which construction can be applied to publie advantage present and remote. The old and simple party boundaries sass rule sharply defined at times, grow i rather indistinct. There is greater complexity n tkeir politics,and in the future,we may have to look forward for the delicate process ef government by combinations, as an the continent'. The prospect is not quite satisfactory, and may lead to an undesirable instability in our party government. But, gentlemen, we may trust to the • native common sense and strenith of England to pull us through all difficulties, of which Mr. Morley said the other day, quoting a French philosopher that we must take them all seriously, but none of them tragically. And for the rest, we have to consider something else be- sides the convenience of this party or that. (Rear, hear.) Every true Liberal among us has to remember both the duties he owes to the reformer of the past and obligation to continue their work for the benefit of those who are to follow. For accoFding. as we yield to the benumbing influence of mental inertia and stagnatien.or rouse ourselves to the full use of our energies, according as we aceept a dreary nontatturfaxs, or brace ourvtires to face each difficulty, and grasp each favourable oppor- tunity, so shall we be able to effect, be it in a great or small degree, fcr good or. for evil, the fortunes not only of great multitudes on the present, but of our country in the future. (Applause.) That is surely a cr,nsi.lsraticn the: may well take as out of ourselves. It awakes the tcprit cle rorps that exists amongst thou who are working for a great end,and loyalty to those who leads onward towards it. It raises us up tu a high stand. point from which beyond smaller local nod domestic in- terests, we,rnay survey the living history' that is being enacted in our country, and in that great world that lies Novena the waters that wash our shores. It may not Indeed generate the stern and lofty selfesbnegation which belongs only to the rarest an 4 noblest type of humanity, and which others may at least scorn to ape. Hut it may wen stir within us a not unworthy or ignoble ambition whieh,in whatever sphere of life oar lot is cast may N. turned to good portyer. It may do more. In proportion as we cease to limit Cur intereots to our own persoual siatisfactions and, wenn% tu proportion u wit</p>
        <p>identify and vssociate ourselves with the great move- ment embraced hy advancing civilisation, so 811,11 we be able to lint solace and consolation if,in the full inLri- dian of life, the, frail bowl of happiness be struck from our hands and broken, and when the lengthening sha- dows warn us tlxst our day is nearly over, perhaps even as the moments of its close draw near, hope and tran- quility may steal upon Us like strains of distant music, and we may realise the fulness of their beauty aril in- finity as we recall perhaps the lines of poetry's great masterpiece, and say with Wordsworth : What though the radiance which was once so bright De now for ever taken from my sight ; Though nothing can bring back the hour Of splendour in the grass, of glory in the flower, We will grieve not. rather end Strength in what remains behind.</p>
        <p>I'm the soothing thoughts that spring Out of human suffering, l`n the primal sympChy, WhieAi, having been.must ever be . .</p>
        <p>In yeai'a that bring the philosopie mind. ',Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>THIN DEBATE. Mr. Phu. who opened the debate for the" Opposition" said he was sure they had all been extremely privileged that night in hearing words of wisdom and prudence from a gentleman who he believed was destined not only to take a prominent part in the politics of their town, but in the politics of the country. He in leading the "opposition " would naturally be expected to criticise the address, but he believed that Mr. Crosfield had enunciated a scheme—s sketch of a policy—which must essentially harmonize with the opinions of politicians of all grades. Ile reviewed several of the points made by alr.Crosfisld and contended that they were points which claimed the sympathy of Tories and Liberals alike.—Mr. limo, who followed, considered that Mr. Crosfield's new . Liberalism was a half-way house between Orthodox Liberalism and Socialism. Be thought Mr. Crosfield must be a socialist; and if he was he was in sympathy with him.— Mr. GANDY speaking as one on the verge of socialism, thought Mr. Crasfield did not go quite far enough.—Mr. A. BENNETT said the address was a most masterlr one, and the policy enunciated met a great difficulty which had arisen out of bigotted partizanship ; and it wac a policy with which he agreed in every detail almost. It was a rational and comprehensive policy which, if Mr. Crosfield offered it to the Warrington constituency when he came before them as a Parliamentary can- didate—(which he thought was very probable)— he would support with his own vote. (Applause.)— Mr. GORTON approved of most of Mr. Crosfield's opin- ions, but took exception of his objection to the Veto Bill.—Mr. London said he was a supporter of such a Liberal policy as Mr. Crosfield had enunciated. lie believed its slight bearing to soeialism was essential.— Mr. Wasrattocia also criticised certain aspecta of the " new Liberalism." The meeting closed at a quarter to eleven, with a vote of thanks to Mr. Crosaeld.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>AfHLETICS.</p>
        <p>10.00TI3ALL.,</p>
        <p>It's a long time since I remember so much local foot- ball in a week. 'Mere have been quite ten matches, all of a more or less important character since Saturday. The last and most interesting one being a theatrical cos- tume match between members of the Juvenile Opera ipany and other theatricals.</p>
        <p>But let us get back to Saturday' match between our .ocal first team and Barrow. It has been said that Warrington did not cover themselves all over with glory on that occasion. All I can ask is Who really played the most skilful game ; who played with all the tact and compactness I can answer it, too. It was not War- rington. They were certainly the victors by I-1-3 to 0-2-2. But it was not a matter of nuts and apple pie all the time with them. In the first half when they did all the scoring they displayed somewhat of their old form ; but " lawks !" when the second half came along how I did "lan." I couldn't seem to get the hang of what they were doing until that ingenious P.D. of ours came along with a piece of smoked glass ; spied them thro' it ; and said in his wicked, sinful way, " Gosh ! they're jumping about like clams in an obstacle race !"</p>
        <p>I saw the resemblance in a minute then. They did jump about. The forwards seemed to think they were mostly full-backs ; some of them joined in a scrimmage when others were finishing, and I am sorry to say that one or two players reverted back to a policy of selfish- ness in handling the ball. Then there was the full-back. Well, J. Lawless is a smart little player, but why did the committee saddle upon his inexperienced shoulders the arduous duties of full-back?. In the absence of Boscow</p>
        <p>I should most certainly have chosen someone else—say Tommy Barnes. The commiStec don't believe, surely, that the old favourite has lost so much of his gallant old form as to debar him from occasionally taking a tempo- rary stand in the breach which he has filled so often with a success which hung a glamor all about his name, like a Lord Mayor's robe.</p>
        <p>In the second half the Barrow men ;played with splen- did form and openness, so much so indeed that they made the Warrington spectators cheer them! Of coarse, Warrington are entitled to a considerable amount of excuse in consideration of the fact that Boscow and Jolley were playing with the c6unty and Evans, "Smack " Bate, and Donchue were all three away.</p>
        <p>To-day the first team meets Widnes on the Wilders-_ pool field. Now this will be a chance for Warrington to distinguish themselves. There will be a quantity of fun on this occasion. And then on flood Friday they meet the Mona Club from the Isle of Man, the famous winners of the Isle of Man Challenge Shield. It is expected to , be a very difficult match in consequence of the Manx I men having the advantage of an extra leg. I'm afraid the ‘Varrington mem have not been aware of this when they made the fixture. The Manx men will have 15 more legs than they (so I'm told), and that will be equi- valent to seven and a half extra players ! Warrington, be warmed in time—take a reinforcement and make the Mona team jih round some—and make them moan-a little, too. No! Don'tthrow, the pun wasn't intentional.</p>
        <p>The "A" team met P.lachdale St. Clements on Satur- day but they had a weak team in consequence of the demand made upon their ranks by the first team. The number of spectators was large-6,000, but Warrington's score was small ; indeed it was nil against a try. 'Tis pity that the long line of successes achieved by the" A" team should be darkened by defeat. Hut our P.D. tells Inc he'll join just now and put things all right.</p>
        <p>--- The teams in the Warrington Junior Cup Competition seem to have taken my hint that if they don't "hustle" about A little they•will be playing football instead of cricket during the summer. So they have played off a match this week. On Monday the Pariah Church met St. Mary's, and beat them by 2-1-0 to nil.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>"Oh what a time ;;Wre-h.a-ving.." A young min told me he heard this song emanating from the quarter of the Cricket Association Club's quarters on Saturday. I remarked that it' was very likely and then showed him that the team had been playing against Padgate Insti- tute in the District Medal Competition, and in conse- quence of a disposition for gosling they had scored thirteen goals to the P. 1. nothing. And I told him too, that the P. I. had been beaten so much chiefly through their loose inconsistent play.</p>
        <p>CYCLING. The W.C.C.. opened their season on Saturday, with a run to Knutsford. It was a turn out which must have must have made the captain's heart swsli with pride, and other thihgs. No less than 31 members besides a lot of detached riders turned out, and out cf the lot I believe there were 29 pneumatics—machines I mean, mil pneumatic riders. There must have been a great number of the riders hadn't got their sea legs though, because they were all the while stopping to apply lubrication ! Hem ! Another time, thanks all the same. Captain W. hlountield, sub-captain D. Davies, and Secretary Pickavance were all there. I wonder what trouble those young men on wheels would get into if it was'nt for the chaplain and the restraining influences of the secretary ? -- At Knutsford a fine tea was partaken of at the Red Cow, which was followed (not the Red Cow) by a " smoker." By-the-way I WAS glad to hear that the club, had arrange.' permanent quarters at the Shrews- bury Army, Mickle Trafford, near Chester. They have got cricketing tackle clown there have arranged a suit- able tariff for meats, and a party of 50 of them can be put up at a time if they require to stay the night. This Is good !</p>
        <p>'rhe Warrington wheelers hold their last smoking con- :ten on Monday neat at the l'atten Arms. Mr. r. A. Edge, of Manchester, will be there to preside. "All cyclists and friends" are cordially invited, and if there is any encouragement in this we car.. tell thent that "tobacco is free." Down! D., it isn't.meant for you.</p>
        <p>BILLIAItD:4. A bigbilliard ma.teli was held at the 'Vhite 1411 on. Monday eveninz, between J. Craik of Warrington, and Pearce of St. Helens. The stake was £l5 and was car- ried by Craik. Pearce has played four similar -e and won one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Coinl loca,</head>
        <p>Coinl loca,</p>
        <p>metches 'here</p>
        <p>Epps.g COOOA.—URATICIML •ND COMFORTINO.—•• fly a thorough knowledge of the natural laws which govern the operations of digestion and nutrition, and by a careful ap• plication of the fine properties of well-selected COCOA. Mr. Hops his provided our bseakfatt tables with a delicately flavoured beverage which may save us many heavy dottors. bills. It Is by the judicious use of such artleles ef diet that constitution may be gradually built up until strong enough to resist every tendenoy to disease. Iluudreds of subtle maladies are Slating around us ready to attack where ever there Is a weak point. We may usiape msny a fatal shaft by keeping ourselves well fortified with pure blood and a properly nutnialled frame."—Cira Serr;ce Gazette.—Madi simplywith hailing water or milk. Sr.l I i.nle in packets, by Grocers. !abutted J•mais NPPN. Ors.. Hoff recyrthle Chemists, London. Also. Makeizs giijpnes Qogoalue or Ntb-Extrae,•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-25_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>NEW WESLEYAN CIIAPEL FOR SUTTON.</head>
        <p>NEW WESLEYAN CIIAPEL FOR SUTTON.</p>
        <p>In bright and beautiful weather the meori armies of a Wesleyan Chapel for tho distinct o Veaver Sutton were laid on a site near Staten - station on Wednesday afternoon. There was a . company present. The building which will be 01 plain red bricks, will provide sitting accona mods.' tion for 100, and every arrangement is provided.for carrying on a Sunday school. The total cost is estnir' ated at £B5O and Mr. Shallcross, of Aston, ia carrylng. out the work from the designs of Mr. C. E. I,loakl, (Messrs.Linaker and Davies,Frodshani).Up to the proser,l- the followers of Wesleyat Sutton,have met for woriliiptO give the Nonconfortniste of the nod.;hboinal a cottage, and the erection of the chapel is expected' help. After tho cnstoniary devotions conducted 'by rei Rev. P. Pizey and the Rev. F. F. Edmunds, the neemov ial stones were laid by Miss Hutchinson of Roily Lodger West Derby, who contributed £25; by Mrs. Lowe,„t° arhood gr Sutton Hall, who gave a like sum • by Mr. E. J. of Oak Mount, fleshy, who handed in a cheque for L 4 lir ' T and Mr. . Rigby of Ci'ilarA, Sutton who gave e,: guineas. The Rev. P. Pizey said he had the pleasure 0,1 meeting the vicar of the parish a short time ago, , mentioned the proi-ct of building the chapel to bllfl The Rev. E. Marsden expressed his kindly 83'1110'11T with them, and said he would be pleased I they allowed him to give a Bible for the reading, desk. They thanked him not only for time 0 value .0..! the gift, but the spirit which had prom u pted , The Rev. Charles Garrett, of Liverpool, then delivero an address in which he said the event they were celeies- tinir that day was a proof of the vitality which was never Sc, strung, so united, and so aggreBst as at the present time. Last year 44,000 new member; were added to the church,. and 28.000 persons we(' Od trial last year for church membership. One hundred. and twenty new chapels had been built', and 111 e., larged. They had also within recent veara' £2,000,000 in chapel debts,aiiil now had 5,000 pleeS 171 worship perfectly free from debt. A collection v 1144 taken, which realised £l4. Subsequently a number(' L 7 104. was subscribed. An excellent tea vd" or Methodis!tyl:, friends " memorial bricks,". when an additinni Lwwai:ren( when Mr. N,spizra r.r atAan.kati tyn after After!" which a. Norimian, lr:nits)ci,i)otr)frl h ad- dresses were delivered by the lt,vs.P.Piz,iy, and F. F. Edmunds. ii 720, pnOreeefiiii, aa ?le</p>
